{"source":"https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_1/Confucian_Analects","sourceNote":"Wikisource transcription of James Legge's public-domain Chinese-English Analects from The Chinese Classics. Chinese converted from traditional to simplified with OpenCC.","books":[{"slug":"xue-er","number":1,"zhTitle":"学而","enTitle":"Hsio R.","chapterCount":16,"sentences":[{"id":"xue-er-001","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，学而时习之，不亦说乎。有朋自远方来，不亦乐乎。人不知而不愠，不亦君子乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xué ér shí xí zhī bú yì yuè hū yǒu péng zì yuǎn fāng lái bú yì lè hū rén bù zhī ér bú yùn bú yì jūn zǐ hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Is it not pleasant to learn with a constant perseverance and application? 2. \"Is it not delightful to have friends coming from distant quarters? 3. \"Is he not a man of complete virtue, who feels no discomposure though men may take no note of him?\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-002","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"有子曰，其为人也孝弟，而好犯上者鲜矣，不好犯上，而好作乱者，未之有也。君子务本，本立，而道生，孝弟也者，其为仁之本与。","pinyin":"yǒu zǐ yuē qí wéi rén yě xiào tì ér hào fàn shàng zhě xiǎn yǐ bú hào fàn shàng ér hào zuò luàn zhě wèi zhī yǒu yě jūn zǐ wù běn běn lì ér dào shēng xiào tì yě zhě qí wéi rén zhī běn yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The philosopher Yu said, \"They are few who, being filial and fraternal, are fond of offending against their superiors. There have been none, who, not liking to offend against their superiors, have been fond of stirring up confusion. 2. \"The superior man bends his attention to what is radical.That being established, all practical courses naturally grow up. Filial piety and fraternal submission!—are they not the root of all benevolent actions?\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-003","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，巧言令色，鲜矣仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qiǎo yán lìng sè xiǎn yǐ rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Fine words and an insinuating appearance are seldom associated with true virtue.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-004","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，吾日三省吾身，为人谋，而不忠乎，与朋友交，而不信乎，传不习乎。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē wú rì sān xǐng wú shēn wèi rén móu ér bù zhōng hū yǔ péng yǒu jiāo ér bú xìn hū chuán bù xí hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"I daily examine myself on three points:—whether, in transacting business for others, I may have been not faithful;—whether, in intercourse with friends, I may have been not sincere;—whether I may have not mastered and practised the instructions of my teacher.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-005","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，道千乘之国，敬事而信，节用而爱人，使民以时。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dǎo qiān shèng zhī guó jìng shì ér xìn jié yòng ér ài rén shǐ mín yǐ shí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, To rule a country of a thousand chariots, there must be reverent attention to business, and sincerity; economy in expenditure, and love for men; and the employment of the people at the proper seasons.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-006","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，弟子，入则孝，出则弟，谨而信，凡爱众，而亲仁，行有余力，则以学文。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dì zǐ rù zé xiào chū zé tì jǐn ér xìn fàn ài zhòng ér qīn rén xíng yǒu yú lì zé yǐ xué wén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A youth, when at home, should be filial, and, abroad, respectful to his elders. He should be earnest and truthful. He should overflow in love to all, and cultivate the friendship of the good. When he has time and opportunity, after the performance of these things, he should employ them in polite studies.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-007","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，贤贤易色，事父母，能竭其力，事君，能致其身，与朋友交，言而有信，虽曰未学，吾必谓之学矣。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē xián xián yì sè shì fù mǔ néng jié qí lì shì jūn néng zhì qí shēn yǔ péng yǒu jiāo yán ér yǒu xìn suī yuē wèi xué wú bì wèi zhī xué yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"If a man withdraws his mind from the love of beauty, and applies it as sincerely to the love of the virtuous; if, in serving his parents, he can exert his utmost strength; if, in serving his prince, he can devote his life; if, in his intercourse with his friends, his words are sincere:—although men say that he has not learned, I will certainly say that he has.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-008","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子不重，则不威，学则不固。主忠信。无友不如己者。过则勿惮改。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bú zhòng zé bù wēi xué zé bú gù zhǔ zhōng xìn wú yǒu bù rú jǐ zhě guò zé wù dàn gǎi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"If the scholar be not grave, he will not call forth any veneration, and his learning will not be solid. 2. \"Hold faithfulness and sincerity as first principles. 3. \"Have no friends not equal to yourself. 4. \"When you have faults, do not fear to abandon them.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-009","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，愼终追远，民德归厚矣。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē shèn zhōng zhuī yuǎn mín dé guī hòu yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"Let there be a careful attention to perform the funeral rites to parents, and let them be followed when long gone with the ceremonies of sacrifice;—then the virtue of the people will resume its proper excellence.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-010","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子禽问于子贡曰，夫子至于是邦也，必闻其政，求之与，抑与之与。子贡曰，夫子温，良，恭，俭，让，以得之，夫子之求之也，其诸异乎人之求之与。","pinyin":"zǐ qín wèn yú zǐ gòng yuē fū zǐ zhì yú shì bāng yě bì wén qí zhèng qiú zhī yú yì yǔ zhī yú zǐ gòng yuē fū zǐ wēn liáng gōng jiǎn ràng yǐ dé zhī fū zǐ zhī qiú zhī yě qí zhū yì hū rén zhī qiú zhī yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-ch'in asked Tsze-kung, saying, \"When our master comes to any country, he does not fail to learn all about its government. Does he ask his information? or is it given to him?\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"Our master is benign, upright, courteous, temperate, and complaisant, and thus he gets his information. The master's mode of asking information!—is it not different from that of other men?\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-011","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，父在，观其志，父没，观其行，三年无改于父之道，可谓孝矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fù zài guān qí zhì fù mò guān qí xíng sān nián wú gǎi yú fù zhī dào kě wèi xiào yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"While a man's father is alive, look at the bent of his will; when his father is dead, look at his conduct. If for three years he does not alter from the way of his father, he may be called filial.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-012","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"有子曰，礼之用，和为贵，先王之道，斯为美，小大由之。有所不行，知和而和，不以礼节之，亦不可行也。","pinyin":"yǒu zǐ yuē lǐ zhī yòng hé wéi guì xiān wáng zhī dào sī wéi měi xiǎo dà yóu zhī yǒu suǒ bù xíng zhī hé ér hé bù yǐ lǐ jié zhī yì bù kě xíng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The philosopher Yu said, \"In practising the rules of propriety, a natural ease is to be prized. In the ways prescribed by the ancient kings, this is the excellent quality, and in things small and great we follow them. 2. \"Yet it is not to be observed in all cases. If one, knowing how such ease should be prized, manifests it, without regulating it by the rules of propriety, this likewise is not to be done.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-013","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"有子曰，信近于义，言可复也，恭近于礼，远耻辱也，因不失其亲，亦可宗也。","pinyin":"yǒu zǐ yuē xìn jìn yú yì yán kě fù yě gōng jìn yú lǐ yuǎn chǐ rǔ yě yīn bù shī qí qīn yì kě zōng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Yu said, \"When agreements are made according to what is right, what is spoken can be made good. When respect is shown according to what is proper, one keeps far from shame and disgrace. When the parties upon whom a man leans are proper persons to be intimate with, he can make them his guides and masters.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-014","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子食无求饱，居无求安，敏于事，而愼于言，就有道，而正焉，可谓好学也已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ shí wú qiú bǎo jū wú qiú ān mǐn yú shì ér shèn yú yán jiù yǒu dào ér zhèng yān kě wèi hào xué yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who aims to be a man of complete virtue in his food does not seek to gratify his appetite, nor in his dwelling place does he seek the appliances of ease; he is earnest in what he is doing, and careful in his speech; he frequents the company of men of principle that he may be rectified:—such a person may be said indeed to love to learn.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-015","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰贫，而无谄，富而无骄，何如。子曰，可也，未若贫而乐，富而好礼者也。子贡曰，诗云，如切如磋，如琢如磨，其斯之谓与。子曰，赐也，始可与言诗已矣，吿诸往而知来者。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē pín ér wú chǎn fù ér wú jiāo hé rú zǐ yuē kě yě wèi ruò pín ér lè fù ér hào lǐ zhě yě zǐ gòng yuē shī yún rú qiē rú cuō rú zhuó rú mó qí sī zhī wèi yú zǐ yuē cì yě shǐ kě yǔ yán shī yǐ yǐ gào zhū wǎng ér zhī lái zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung said, \"What do you pronounce concerning the poor man who yet does not flatter, and the rich man who is not proud?\" The Master replied, \"They will do; but they are not equal to him, who, though poor, is yet cheerful, and to him, who, though rich, loves the rules of propriety.\" 2. Tsze-kung replied, \"It is said in the Book of Poetry, 'As you cut and then file, as you carve and then polish.'—The meaning is the same, I apprehend, as that which you have just expressed.\" 3. The Master said, \"With one like Ts'ze, I can begin to talk about the odes. I told him one point, and he knew its proper sequence.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xue-er-016","bookSlug":"xue-er","bookNumber":1,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不患人之不己知，患不知人也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú huàn rén zhī bù jǐ zhī huàn bù zhī rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I will not be afflicted at men's not knowing me; I will be afflicted that I do not know men.\"","themes":["analects","xue-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"wei-zheng","number":2,"zhTitle":"为政","enTitle":"Wei Chang","chapterCount":24,"sentences":[{"id":"wei-zheng-001","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，为政以德，譬如北辰，居其所，而众星共之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wéi zhèng yǐ dé pì rú běi chén jū qí suǒ ér zhòng xīng gǒng zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who exercises government by means of his virtue may be compared to the north polar star, which keeps its place and all the stars turn towards it.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-002","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，诗三百，一言以蔽之，曰，思无邪。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shī sān bǎi yì yán yǐ bì zhī yuē sī wú xú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In the Book of Poetry are three hundred pieces, but the design of them all may be embraced in one sentence—'Having no depraved thoughts.'\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-003","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，道之以政，齐之以刑，民免而无耻。道之以德，齐之以礼，有耻且格。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dǎo zhī yǐ zhèng qí zhī yǐ xíng mín miǎn ér wú chǐ dǎo zhī yǐ dé qí zhī yǐ lǐ yǒu chǐ qiě gé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"If the people be led by laws, and uniformity sought to be given them by punishments, they will try to avoid the punishment, but have no sense of shame. 2. \"If they be led by virtue, and uniformity sought to be given them by the rules of propriety, they will have the sense of shame, and moreover will become good.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-004","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾十有五而志于学。三十而立。四十而不惑。五十而知天命。六十而耳顺。七十而从心所欲，不逾矩。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú shí yòu wǔ ér zhì yú xué sān shí ér lì sì shí ér bú huò wǔ shí ér zhī tiān mìng liù shí ér ěr shùn qī shí ér cóng xīn suǒ yù bù yú jǔ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"At fifteen, I had my mind bent on learning. 2. \"At thirty, I stood firm. 3. \"At forty, I had no doubts. 4. \"At fifty, I knew the decrees of Heaven. 5. \"At sixty, my ear was an obedient organ for the reception of truth. 6. \"At seventy, I could follow what my heart desired, without transgressing what was right.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-005","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"孟懿子问孝，子曰，无违。樊迟御，子吿之曰，孟孙问孝于我，我对曰，无违。樊迟曰，何谓也，子曰，生事之以礼，死葬之以礼，祭之以礼。","pinyin":"mèng yì zǐ wèn xiào zǐ yuē wú wéi fán chí yù zǐ gào zhī yuē mèng sūn wèn xiào yú wǒ wǒ duì yuē wú wéi fán chí yuē hé wèi yě zǐ yuē shēng shì zhī yǐ lǐ sǐ zàng zhī yǐ lǐ jì zhī yǐ lǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Mang I asked what filial piety was. The Master said, \"It is not being disobedient.\" 2. Soon after, as Fan Ch'ih was driving him, the Master told him, saying, \"Mang-sun asked me what filial piety was, and I answered him,—'not being disobedient.'\" 3. Fan Ch'ih said, \"What did you mean?\" The Master replied, \"That parents, when alive, be served according to propriety; that, when dead, they should be buried according to propriety; and that they should be sacrificed to according to propriety.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-006","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"孟武伯问孝，子曰，父母唯其疾之忧。","pinyin":"mèng wǔ bó wèn xiào zǐ yuē fù mǔ wéi qí jí zhī yōu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Mang Wu asked what filial piety was. The Master said, \"Parents are anxious lest their children should be sick.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-007","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子游问孝，子曰，今之孝者，是谓能养，至于犬马，皆能有养，不敬，何以别乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu wèn xiào zǐ yuē jīn zhī xiào zhě shì wèi néng yǎng zhì yú quǎn mǎ jiē néng yǒu yǎng bú jìng hé yǐ bié hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-yu asked what filial piety was. The Master said, \"The filial piety of now-a-days means the support of one's parents. But dogs and horses likewise are able to do something in the way of support;—without reverence, what is there to distinguish the one support given from the other?\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-008","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子夏问孝，子曰，色难，有事，弟子服其劳，有酒食，先生馔，曾是以为孝乎。","pinyin":"zǐ xià wèn xiào zǐ yuē sè nán yǒu shì dì zǐ fú qí láo yǒu jiǔ sì xiān shēng zhuàn zēng shì yǐ wéi xiào hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia asked what filial piety was. The Master said, \"The difficulty is with the countenance. If, when their elders have any troublesome affairs, the young take the toil of them, and if, when the young have wine and food, they set them before their elders, is this to be considered filial piety?\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-009","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾与回言终日，不违，如愚。退而省其私，亦足以发，回也不愚。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú yǔ huí yán zhōng rì bù wéi rú yú tuì ér xǐng qí sī yì zú yǐ fā huí yě bù yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I have talked with Hui for a whole day, and he has not made any objection to anything I said;—as if he were stupid. He has retired, and I have examined his conduct when away from me, and found him able to illustrate my teachings. Hui!—He is not stupid.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-010","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，视其所以。观其所由。察其所安。人焉廋哉，人焉廋哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì qí suǒ yǐ guān qí suǒ yóu chá qí suǒ ān rén yān sōu zāi rén yān sōu zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"See what a man does. 2. \"Mark his motives. 3. \"Examine in what things he rests. 4. \"How can a man conceal his character? 5. How can a man conceal his character?\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-011","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，温故而知新，可以为师矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wēn gù ér zhī xīn kě yǐ wéi shī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a man keeps cherishing his old knowledge, so as continually to be acquiring new, he may be a teacher of others.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-012","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子不器。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bú qì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The accomplished scholar is not a utensil.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-013","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子贡问君子，子曰，先行其言，而后从之。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn jūn zǐ zǐ yuē xiān xíng qí yán ér hòu cóng zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked what constituted the superior man. The Master said, \"He acts before he speaks, and afterwards speaks according to his actions.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-014","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子周而不比，小人比而不周。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ zhōu ér bú bì xiǎo rén bì ér bù zhōu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is catholic and no partisan. The mean man is partisan and not catholic.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-015","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，学而不思则罔，思而不学则殆。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xué ér bù sī zé wǎng sī ér bù xué zé dài","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Learning without thought is labour lost; thought without learning is perilous.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-016","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，攻乎异端，斯害也已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gōng hū yì duān sī hài yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The study of strange doctrines is injurious indeed!\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-017","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，由，诲女知之乎，知之为知之，不知为不知，是知也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yóu huì rǔ zhī zhī hū zhī zhī wéi zhī zhī bù zhī wéi bù zhī shì zhì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Yu, shall I teach you what knowledge is? When you know a thing, to hold that you know it; and when you do not know a thing, to allow that you do not know it;—this is knowledge.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-018","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子张学干禄。子曰，多闻阙疑，愼言其余，则寡尤，多见阙殆，愼行其余，则寡悔，言寡尤，行寡悔，禄在其中矣。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng xué gān lù zǐ yuē duō wén quē yí shèn yán qí yú zé guǎ yóu duō jiàn quē dài shèn xíng qí yú zé guǎ huǐ yán guǎ yóu xíng guǎ huǐ lù zài qí zhōng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang was learning with a view to official emolument. 2. The Master said, \"Hear much and put aside the points of which you stand in doubt, while you speak cautiously at the same time of the others:—then you will afford few occasions for blame. See much and put aside the things which seem perilous, while you are cautious at the same time in carrying the others into practice:—then you will have few occasions for repentance. When one gives few occasions for blame in his words, and few occasions for repentance in his conduct, he is in the way to get emolument.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-019","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"哀公问曰，何为则民服。孔子对曰，擧直错诸枉，则民服，擧枉错诸直，则民不服。","pinyin":"āi gōng wèn yuē hé wéi zé mín fú kǒng zǐ duì yuē jǔ zhí cuò zhū wǎng zé mín fú jǔ wǎng cuò zhū zhí zé mín bù fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Duke Ai asked, saying, \"What should be done in order to secure the submission of the people?\" Confucius replied, \"Advance the upright and set aside the crooked, then the people will submit. Advance the crooked and set aside the upright, then the people will not submit.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-020","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"季康子问使民敬忠以劝，如之何。子曰，临之以庄，则敬，孝慈，则忠，擧善而教不能，则劝。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ wèn shǐ mín jìng zhōng yǐ quàn rú zhī hé zǐ yuē lín zhī yǐz huāng zé jìng xiào cí zé zhōng jǔ shàn ér jiào bù néng zé quàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang asked how to cause the people to reverence their ruler, to be faithful to him, and to go on to nerve themselves to virtue. The Master said, \"Let him preside over them with gravity;—then they will reverence him. Let him be filial and kind to all;—then they will be faithful to him. Let him advance the good and teach the incompetent;—then they will eagerly seek to be virtuous.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-021","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"或谓孔子曰，子奚不为政。子曰，书云孝乎，惟孝友于兄弟，施于有政，是亦为政，奚其为为政。","pinyin":"huò wèi kǒng zǐ yuē zǐ xī bù wéi zhèng zǐ yuē shū yún xiào hū wéi xiào yǒu yú xiōng dì shī yú yǒu zhèng shì yì wéi zhèng xī qí wéi wéi zhèng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Some one addressed Confucius, saying, \"Sir, why are you not engaged in the government?\" 2. The Master said, \"What does the Shu-ching say of filial piety?—'You are filial, you discharge your brotherly duties. These qualities are displayed in government.' This then also constitutes the exercise of government. Why must there be that —making one be in the government?\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-022","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人而无信，不知其可也，大车无𫐐，小车无𫐄，其何以行之哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén ér wú xìn bù zhī qí kě yě dà jū wú ní xiǎo jū wú yuè qí hé yǐ xíng zhī zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I do not know how a man without truthfulness is to get on. How can a large carriage be made to go without the cross-bar for yoking the oxen to, or a small carriage without the arrangement for yoking the horses?\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-023","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子张问十世，可知也。子曰，殷因于夏礼，所损益，可知也，周因于殷礼，所损益，可知也，其或继周者，虽百世，可知也。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn shí shì kě zhī yě zǐ yuē yīn yīn yú xià lǐ suǒ sǔn yì kě zhī yě zhōu yīn yú yīn lǐ suǒ sǔn yì kě zhī yě qí huò jì zhōu zhě suī bǎi shì kě zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang asked whether the affairs of ten ages after could be known. 2. Confucius said, \"The Yin dynasty followed the regulations of the Hsia: wherein it took from or added to them may be known. The Chau dynasty has followed the regulations of Yin: wherein it took from or added to them may be known. Some other may follow the Chau, but though it should be at the distance of a hundred ages, its affairs may be known.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zheng-024","bookSlug":"wei-zheng","bookNumber":2,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，非其鬼而祭之，谄也。见义不为，无勇也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fēi qí guǐ ér jì zhī chǎn yě jiàn yì bù wéi wú yǒng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"For a man to sacrifice to a spirit which does not belong to him is flattery. 2. \"To see what is right and not to do it is want of courage.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zheng"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"ba-yi","number":3,"zhTitle":"八佾","enTitle":"Pa Yih","chapterCount":26,"sentences":[{"id":"ba-yi-001","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"孔子谓季氏，八佾舞于庭，是可忍也，孰不可忍也。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ wèi jì shì bā yì wǔ yú tíng shì kě rěn yě shú bù kě rěn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said of the head of the Chi family, who had eight rows of pantomimes in his area, \"If he can bear to do this, what may he not bear to do?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-002","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"三家者，以雍彻。子曰，相维辟公，天子穆穆，奚取于三家之堂。","pinyin":"sān jiā zhě yǐ yōng chè zǐ yuē xiàng wéi bì gōng tiān zǐ mù mù xī qǔ yú sān jiā zhī táng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The three families used the yung ode, while the vessels were being removed, at the conclusion of the sacrifice. The Master said, \"'Assisting are the princes;—the son of heaven looks profound and grave:'—what application can these words have in the hall of the three families?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-003","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人而不仁，如礼何，人而不仁，如乐何。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén ér bù rén rú lǐ hé rén ér bù rén rú yuè hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a man be without the virtues proper to humanity, what has he to do with the rites of propriety? If a man be without the virtues proper to humanity, what has he to do with music?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-004","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"林放问礼之本。子曰，大哉问。礼，与其奢也，宁俭，丧，与其易也，宁戚。","pinyin":"lín fàng wèn lǐ zhī běn zǐ yuē dà zāi wèn lǐ yǔ qí shē yě nìng jiǎn sāng yǔ qí yì yě nìng qī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Lin Fang asked what was the first thing to be attended to in ceremonies. 2. The Master said, \"A great question indeed! 3. \"In festive ceremonies, it is better to be sparing than extravagant. In the ceremonies of mourning, it is better that there be deep sorrow than a minute attention to observances.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-005","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，夷狄之有君，不如诸夏之亡也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yí dí zhī yǒu jūn bù rú zhū xià zhī wú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The rude tribes of the east and north have their princes, and are not like the States of our great land which are without them.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-006","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"季氏旅于泰山。子谓冉有曰，女弗能救与。对曰，不能。子曰，呜呼，曾谓泰山，不如林放乎。","pinyin":"jì shì lǚ yú tài shān zǐ wèi rǎn yǒu yuē rǔ fú néng jiù yú duì yuē bù néng zǐ yuē wū hū zēng wèi tài shān bù rú lín fàng hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The chief of the Chi family was about to sacrifice to the T'ai mountain. The Master said to Zan Yu, \"Can you not save him from this?\" He answered, \"I cannot.\" Confucius said, \"Alas! will you say that the T'ai mountain is not so discerning as Lin Fang?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-007","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子无所争，必也射乎，揖让而升，下而飮，其争也君子。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ wú suǒ zhēng bì yě shè hū yī ràng ér shēng xià ér yǐn qí zhēng yě jūn zǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The student of virtue has no contentions. If it be said he cannot avoid them, shall this be in archery? But he bows complaisantly to his competitors; thus he ascends the hall, descends, and exacts the forfeit of drinking. In his contention, he is still the Chun-tsze.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-008","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子夏问曰，巧笑倩兮，美目盼兮，素以为绚兮。何谓也。子曰，绘事后素。曰，礼后乎。子曰，起予者商也，始可与言诗已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ xià wèn yuē qiǎo xiào qiàn xī měi mù pàn xī sù yǐ wéi xuàn xī hé wèi yě zǐ yuē huì shì hòu sù yuē lǐ hòu hū zǐ yuē qǐ yú zhě shāng yě shǐ kě yǔ yán shī yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-hsia asked, saying, \"What is the meaning of the passage—'The pretty dimples of her artful smile! The well-defined black and white of her eye! The plain ground for the colours?'\" 2. The Master said, \"The business of laying on the colours follows (the preparation of) the plain ground.\" 3. \"Ceremonies then are a subsequent thing?\" The Master said, \"It is Shang who can bring out my meaning. Now I can begin to talk about the odes with him.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-009","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，夏礼吾能言之，杞不足征也，殷礼吾能言之，宋不足征也，文献不足故也，足，则吾能征之矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xià lǐ wú néng yán zhī qǐ bù zú zhēng yě yīn lǐ wú néng yán zhī sòng bù zú zhēng yě wén xiàn bù zú gù yě zú zé wú néng zhēng zhī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I could describe the ceremonies of the Hsia dynasty, but Chi cannot sufficiently attest my words. I could describe the ceremonies of the Yin dynasty, but Sung cannot sufficiently attest my words. (They cannot do so) because of the insufficiency of their records and wise men. If those were sufficient, I could adduce them in support of my words.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-010","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，禘，自既灌而往者，吾不欲观之矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dì zì jì guàn ér wǎng zhě wú bú yù guān zhī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"At the great sacrifice, after the pouring out of the libation, I have no wish to look on.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-011","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"或问禘之说。子曰，不知也，知其说者，之于天下也，其如示诸斯乎。指其掌。","pinyin":"huò wèn dì zhī shuō zǐ yuē bù zhī yě zhī qí shuō zhě zhī yú tiān xià yě qí rú shì zhū sī hū zhǐ qí zhǎng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Some one asked the meaning of the great sacrifice. The Master said, \"I do not know. He who knew its meaning would find it as easy to govern the kingdom as to look on this;—pointing to his palm.","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-012","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"祭如在，祭神如神在。子曰，吾不与祭，如不祭。","pinyin":"jì rú zài jì shén rú shén zài zǐ yuē wú bú yù jì rú bú jì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. He sacrificed to the dead, as if they were present. He sacrificed to the spirits, as if the spirits were present. 2. The Master said, \"I consider my not being present at the sacrifice, as if I did not sacrifice.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-013","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"王孙贾问曰，与其媚于奥，宁媚于灶，何谓也。子曰，不然，获罪于天，无所祷也。","pinyin":"wáng sūn jiǎ wèn yuē yǔ qí mèi yú ào nìng mèi yú zào hé wèi yě zǐ yuē bù rán huò zuì yú tiān wú suǒ dǎo yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Wang-sun Chia asked, saying, \"What is the meaning of the saying, 'It is better to pay court to the furnace than to the south-west corner?'\" 2. The Master said, \"Not so. He who offends against Heaven has none to whom he can pray.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-014","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，周监于二代，郁郁乎文哉，吾从周。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhōu jiàn yú èr dài yù yù hū wén zāi wú cóng zhōu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Chau had the advantage of viewing the two past dynasties. How complete and elegant are its regulations! I follow Chau.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-015","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子入大庙，每事问。或曰，孰谓鄹人之子知礼乎，入大庙，每事问。子闻之曰，是礼也。","pinyin":"zǐ rù tài miào měi shì wèn huò yuē shú wèi zōu rén zhī zǐ zhī lǐ hū rù tài miào měi shì wèn zǐ wén zhī yuē shì lǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master, when he entered the grand temple, asked about everything. Some one said, \"Who will say that the son of the man of Tsau knows the rules of propriety! He has entered the grand temple and asks about everything.\" The Master heard the remark, and said, \"This is a rule of propriety.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-016","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，射不主皮，为力不同科，古之道也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shè bù zhǔ pí wèi lì bù tóng kē gǔ zhī dào yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In archery it is not going through the leather which is the principal thing;—because people's strength is not equal. This was the old way.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-017","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子贡欲去吿朔之饩羊。子曰，赐也，尔爱其羊，我爱其礼。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yù qù gù shuò zhī xì yáng zǐ yuē cì yě ěr ài qí yáng wǒ ài qí lǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung wished to do away with the offering of a sheep connected with the inauguration of the first day of each month. 2. The Master said, \"Ts'ze, you love the sheep; I love the ceremony.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-018","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，事君尽礼，人以为谄也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì jūn jìn lǐ rén yǐ wéi chǎn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The full observance of the rules of propriety in serving one's prince is accounted by people to be flattery.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-019","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"定公问君使臣，臣事君，如之何。孔子对曰，君使臣以礼，臣事君以忠。","pinyin":"dìng gōng wèn jūn shǐ chén chén shì jūn rú zhī hé kǒng zǐ duì yuē jūn shǐ chén yǐ lǐ chén shì jūn yǐ zhōng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Duke Ting asked how a prince should employ his ministers, and how ministers should serve their prince. Confucius replied, \"A prince should employ his minister according to the rules of propriety; ministers should serve their prince with faithfulness.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-020","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子曰，关睢乐而不淫，哀而不伤。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē guān jū lè ér bù yín āi ér bù shāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The Kwan Tsu is expressive of enjoyment without being licentious, and of grief without being hurtfully excessive.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-021","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"哀公问社于宰我。宰我对曰，夏后氏以松，殷人以柏，周人以栗，曰，使民战栗。子闻之曰，成事不说，遂事不谏，既往不咎。","pinyin":"āi gōng wèn shè yú zǎi wǒ zǎi wǒ duì yuē xià hòu shì yǐ sōng yīn rén yǐ bǎi zhōu rén yǐ lì yuē shǐ mín zhàn lì zǐ wén zhī yuē chéng shì bù shuō suì shì bú jiàn jì wǎng bú jiù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ai asked Tsai Wo about the altars of the spirits of the land. Tsai Wo replied, \"The Hsia sovereign planted the pine tree about them; the men of the Yin planted the cypress; and the men of the Chau planted the chestnut tree, meaning thereby to cause the people to be in awe.\" 2. When the Master heard it, he said, \"Things that are done, it is needless to speak about; things that have had their course, it is needless to remonstrate about; things that are past, it is needless to blame.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-022","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，管仲之器小哉。或曰，管仲俭乎。曰，管氏有三归，官事不摄，焉得俭。然则管仲知礼乎。曰，邦君树塞门，管氏亦树塞门，邦君为两君之好，有反坫，管氏亦有反坫，管氏而知礼，孰不知礼。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē guǎn zhòng zhī qì xiǎo zāi huò yuē guǎn zhòng jiǎn hū yuē guǎn shì yǒu sān guī guān shì bú shè yān dé jiǎn rán zé guǎn zhòng zhī lǐ hū yuē bāng jūn shù sè mén guǎn shì yì shù sè mén bāng jūn wèi liǎng jūn zhī hào yǒu fǎn diàn guǎn shì yì yǒu fǎn diàn guǎn shì ér zhī lǐ shú bù zhī lǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Small indeed was the capacity of Kwan Chung!\" 2. Some one said, \"Was Kwan Chung parsimonious?\" \"Kwan,\" was the reply, \"had the San Kwei, and his officers performed no double duties; how can he be considered parsimonious?\" 3. \"Then, did Kwan Chung know the rules of propriety?\" The Master said, \"The princes of States have a screen intercepting the view at their gates. Kwan had likewise a screen at his gate. The princes of States on any friendly meeting between two of them, had a stand on which to place their inverted cups. Kwan had also such a stand. If Kwan knew the rules of propriety, who does not know them?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-023","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子语鲁大师乐曰，乐其可知也，始作，翕如也，从之，纯如也，缴如也，绎如也，以成。","pinyin":"zǐ yù lǔ tài shī yuè yuē yuè qí kě zhī yě shǐ zuò xì rú yě zòng zhī chún rú yě jiǎo rú yě yì rú yě yǐ chéng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master instructing the grand music-master of Lu said, \"How to play music may be known. At the commencement of the piece, all the parts should sound together. As it proceeds, they should be in harmony while severally distinct and flowing without break, and thus on to the conclusion.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-024","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"仪封人请见，曰，君子之至于斯也，吾未尝不得见也。从者见之，出曰，二三子，何患于丧乎，天下之无道也久矣，天将以夫子为木铎。","pinyin":"yí fēng rén qǐng xiàn yuē jūn zǐ zhī zhì yú sī yě wú wèi cháng bù dé jiàn yě zòng zhě xiàn zhī chū yuē èr sān zǐ hé huàn yú sàng hū tiān xià zhī wú dào yě jiǔ yǐ tiān jiāng yǐ fū zǐ wéi mù duó","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The border warden at Yi requested to be introduced to the Master, saying, \"When men of superior virtue have come to this, I have never been denied the privilege of seeing them.\" The followers of the sage introduced him, and when he came out from the interview, he said, \"My friends, why are you distressed by your master's loss of office? The kingdom has long been without the principles of truth and right; Heaven is going to use your master as a bell with its wooden tongue.\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-025","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子谓韶，尽美矣，又尽善也，谓武，尽美矣，未尽善也。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi sháo jìn měi yǐ yòu jìn shàn yě wèi wǔ jìn měi yǐ wèi jìn shàn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said of the Shao that it was perfectly beautiful and also perfectly good. He said of the Wu that it was perfectly beautiful but not perfectly good.","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ba-yi-026","bookSlug":"ba-yi","bookNumber":3,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，居上不宽，为礼不敬，临丧不哀，吾何以观之哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jū shàng bù kuān wéi lǐ bú jìng lín sāng bù āi wú hé yǐ guān zhī zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"High station filled without indulgent generosity; ceremonies performed without reverence; mourning conducted without sorrow;—wherewith should I contemplate such ways?\"","themes":["analects","ba-yi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"li-ren","number":4,"zhTitle":"里仁","enTitle":"Le Jin","chapterCount":26,"sentences":[{"id":"li-ren-001","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，里仁为美，择不处仁，焉得知。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē lǐ rén wéi měi zé bù chǔ rén yān dé zhì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"It is virtuous manners which constitute the excellence of a neighborhood. If a man in selecting a residence, do not fix on one where such prevail, how can he be wise?\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-002","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不仁者，不可以久处约，不可以长处乐，仁者安仁，知者利仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bù rén zhě bù kě yǐ jiǔ chǔ yuē bù kě yǐ cháng chǔ lè rén zhě ān rén zhì zhě lì rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Those who are without virtue cannot abide long either in a condition of poverty and hardship, or in a condition of enjoyment. The virtuous rest in virtue; the wise desire virtue.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-003","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，惟仁者，能好人，能恶人。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wéi rén zhě néng hào rén néng wù rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"It is only the (truly) virtuous man, who can love, or who can hate, others.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-004","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子曰，苟志于仁矣，无恶也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǒu zhì yú rén yǐ wú wù yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If the will be set on virtue, there will be no practice of wickedness.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-005","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，富与贵，是人之所欲也，不以其道得之，不处也。贫与贱，是人之所恶也，不以其道得之，不去也。君子去仁，恶乎成名。君子无终食之间违仁，造次必于是，顚沛必于是。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fù yǔ guì shì rén zhī suǒ yù yě bù yǐ qí dào dé zhī bù chǔ yě pín yǔ jiàn shì rén zhī suǒ wù yě bù yǐ qí dào dé zhī bú qù yě jūn zǐ qù rén wū hū chéng míng jūn zǐ wú zhōng shí zhī jiān wéi rén zào cì bì yú shì diān pèi bì yú shì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Riches and honours are what men desire. If it cannot be obtained in the proper way, they should not be held. Poverty and meanness are what men dislike. If it cannot be avoided in the proper way, they should not be avoided. 2. \"If a superior man abandon virtue, how can he fulfil the requirements of that name? 3. \"The superior man does not, even for the space of a single meal, act contrary to virtue. In moments of haste, he cleaves to it. In seasons of danger, he cleaves to it.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-006","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，我未见好仁者，恶不仁者，好仁者，无以尙之，恶不仁者，其为仁矣，不使不仁者，加乎其身。有能一日用其力于仁矣乎，我未见力不足者。盖有之矣，我未之见也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wǒ wèi jiàn hào rén zhě wù bù rén zhě hào rén zhě wú yǐ shàng zhī wù bù rén zhě qí wéi rén yǐ bù shǐ bù rén zhě jiā hū qí shēn yǒu néng yí rì yòng qí lì yú rén yǐ hū wǒ wèi jiàn lì bù zú zhě gài yǒu zhī yǐ wǒ wèi zhī jiàn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"I have not seen a person who loved virtue, or one who hated what was not virtuous. He who loved virtue, would esteem nothing above it. He who hated what is not virtuous, would practise virtue in such a way that he would not allow anything that is not virtuous to approach his person. 2. \"Is any one able for one day to apply his strength to virtue? I have not seen the case in which his strength would be insufficient. 3. \"Should there possibly be any such case, I have not seen it.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-007","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人之过也，各于其党，观过，斯知仁矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén zhī guò yě gè yú qí dǎng guān guò sī zhī rén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The faults of men are characteristic of the class to which they belong. By observing a man's faults, it may be known that he is virtuous.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-008","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，朝闻道，夕死，可矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhāo wén dào xī sǐ kě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a man in the morning hear the right way, he may die in the evening without regret.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-009","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，士志于道，而耻恶衣恶食者，未足与议也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì zhì yú dào ér chǐ è yī è shí zhě wèi zú yǔ yì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A scholar, whose mind is set on truth, and who is ashamed of bad clothes and bad food, is not fit to be discoursed with.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-010","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子之于天下也，无适也，无莫也，义之与比。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ zhī yú tiān xià yě wú shì yě wú mò yě yì zhī yǔ bì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man, in the world, does not set his mind either for anything, or against anything; what is right he will follow.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-011","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子怀德，小人怀土，君子怀刑，小人怀惠。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ huái dé xiǎo rén huái tǔ jūn zǐ huái xíng xiǎo rén huái huì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man thinks of virtue; the small man thinks of comfort. The superior man thinks of the sanctions of law; the small man thinks of favours which he may receive.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-012","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，放于利而行，多怨。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fàng yú lì ér xíng duō yuàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said: \"He who acts with a constant view to his own advantage will be much murmured against.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-013","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，能以礼让为国乎，何有，不能以礼让为国，如礼何。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē néng yǐ lǐ ràng wéi guó hū hé yǒu bù néng yǐ lǐ ràng wéi guó rú lǐ hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a prince is able to govern his kingdom with the complaisance proper to the rules of propriety, what difficulty will he have? If he cannot govern it with that complaisance, what has he to do with the rules of propriety?\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-014","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不患无位，患所以立，不患莫己知，求为可知也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú huàn wú wèi huàn suǒ yǐ lì bú huàn mò jǐ zhī qiú wéi kě zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A man should say, I am not concerned that I have no place, I am concerned how I may fit myself for one. I am not concerned that I am not known, I seek to be worthy to be known.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-015","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，参乎，吾道一以贯之。曾子曰，唯。子出，门人问曰，何谓也。曾子曰，夫子之道，忠恕而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shēn hū wú dào yī yǐ guàn zhī zēng zǐ yuē wéi zǐ chū mén rén wèn yuē hé wèi yě zēng zǐ yuē fū zǐ zhī dào zhōng shù ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Shan, my doctrine is that of an all-pervading unity.\" The disciple Tsang replied, \"Yes.\" 2. The Master went out, and the other disciples asked, saying, \"What do his words mean?\" Tsang said, \"The doctrine of our master is to be true to the principles of our nature and the benevolent exercise of them to others,—this and nothing more.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-016","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子喻于义，小人喻于利。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yù yú yì xiǎo rén yù yú lì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The mind of the superior man is conversant with righteousness; the mind of the mean man is conversant with gain.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-017","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，见贤思齐焉，见不贤而内自省也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jiàn xián sī qí yān jiàn bù xián ér nèi zì xǐng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When we see men of worth, we should think of equalling them; when we see men of a contrary character, we should turn inwards and examine ourselves.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-018","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，事父母几谏，见志不从，又敬不违，劳而不怨。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì fù mǔ jī jiàn jiàn zhì bù cóng yòu jìng bù wéi láo ér bú yuàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In serving his parents, a son may remonstrate with them, but gently; when he sees that they do not incline to follow his advice, he shows an increased degree of reverence, but does not abandon his purpose; and should they punish him, he does not allow himself to murmur.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-019","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，父母在，不远游，游必有方。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fù mǔ zài bù yuǎn yóu yóu bì yǒu fāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"While his parents are alive, the son may not go abroad to a distance. If he does go abroad, he must have a fixed place to which he goes.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-020","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子曰，三年无改于父之道，可谓孝矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sān nián wú gǎi yú fù zhī dào kě wèi xiào yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If the son for three years does not alter from the way of his father, he may be called filial.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-021","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，父母之年，不可不知也，一则以喜，一则以惧。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fù mǔ zhī nián bù kě bù zhī yě yì zé yǐ xǐ yì zé yǐ jù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The years of parents may by no means not be kept in the memory, as an occasion at once for joy and for fear.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-022","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，古者言之不出，耻躬之不逮也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǔ zhě yán zhī bù chū chǐ gōng zhī bú dài yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The reason why the ancients did not readily give utterance to their words, was that they feared lest their actions should not come up to them.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-023","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，以约，失之者鲜矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǐ yuē shī zhī zhě xiǎn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The cautious seldom err.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-024","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子欲讷于言，而敏于行。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yù nè yú yán ér mǐn yú xíng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man wishes to be slow in his speech and earnest in his conduct.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-025","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，德不孤，必有邻。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dé bù gū bì yǒu lín","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Virtue is not left to stand alone. He who practises it will have neighbors.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"li-ren-026","bookSlug":"li-ren","bookNumber":4,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子游曰，事君数，斯辱矣，朋友数，斯疏矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu yuē shì jūn shuò sī rǔ yǐ péng yǒu shuò sī shū yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-yu said, \"In serving a prince, frequent remonstrances lead to disgrace. Between friends, frequent reproofs make the friendship distant.\"","themes":["analects","li-ren"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"gong-ye-chang","number":5,"zhTitle":"公冶长","enTitle":"Kung-ye Ch'ang","chapterCount":27,"sentences":[{"id":"gong-ye-chang-001","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子谓公冶长，可妻也，虽在缧绁之中，非其罪也。以其子妻之。子谓南容，邦有道，不废，邦无道，免于刑戮。以其兄之子妻之。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi gōng yě cháng kě qì yě suī zài léi xiè zhī zhōng fēi qí zuì yě yǐ qí zǐ qì zhī zǐ wèi nán róng bāng yǒu dào bú fèi bāng wú dào miǎn yú xíng lù yǐ qí xiōng zhī zǐ qì zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said of Kung-ye Ch'ang that he might be wived; although he was put in bonds, he had not been guilty of any crime. Accordingly, he gave him his own daughter to wife. 2. Of Nan Yung he said that if the country were well governed he would not be out of office, and if it were ill-governed, he would escape punishment and disgrace. He gave him the daughter of his own elder brother to wife.","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-002","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子谓子贱，君子哉若人。鲁无君子者，斯焉取斯。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi zǐ jiàn jūn zǐ zāi ruò rén lǔ wú jūn zǐ zhě sī yān qǔ sī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said of Tsze-chien, \"Of superior virtue indeed is such a man! If there were not virtuous men in Lu, how could this man have acquired this character?\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-003","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子贡问曰，赐也何如。子曰，女器也。曰，何器也。曰，瑚琏也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yuē cì yě hé rú zǐ yuē rǔ qì yě yuē hé qì yě yuē hú liǎn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked, \"What do you say of me, Ts'ze? The Master said, \"You are a utensil.\" \"What utensil?\" \"A gemmed sacrificial utensil.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-004","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"或曰，雍也仁，而不佞。子曰，焉用佞，御人以口给，屡憎于人，不知其仁，焉用佞。","pinyin":"huò yuē yōng yě rén ér bú nìng zǐ yuē yān yòng nìng yù rén yǐ kǒu jǐ lǚ zēng yú rén bù zhī qí rén yān yòng nìng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Some one said, \"Yung is truly virtuous, but he is not ready with his tongue.\" 2. The Master said, \"What is the good of being ready with the tongue? They who encounter men with smartnesses of speech for the most part procure themselves hatred. I know not whether he be truly virtuous, but why should he show readiness of the tongue?\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-005","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子使漆雕开仕。对曰，吾斯之未能信。子说。","pinyin":"zǐ shǐ qī diāo kāi shì duì yuē wú sī zhī wèi néng xìn zǐ yuè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master was wishing Ch'i-tiao K'ai to enter on official employment. He replied, \"I am not yet able to rest in the assurance of THIS.\" The Master was pleased.","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-006","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，道不行，乘桴浮于海，从我者，其由与。子路闻之喜。子曰，由也，好勇过我，无所取材。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dào bù xíng chéng fú fú yú hǎi zòng wǒ zhě qí yóu yú zǐ lù wén zhī xǐ zǐ yuē yóu yě hào yǒng guò wǒ wú suǒ qǔ cái","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"My doctrines make no way. I will get upon a raft, and float about on the sea. He that will accompany me will be Yu, I dare say.\" Tsze-lu hearing this was glad, upon which the Master said, \"Yu is fonder of daring than I am. He does not exercise his judgment upon matters.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-007","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"孟武伯问子路仁乎。子曰，不知也。又问。子曰，由也，千乘之国，可使治其赋也，不知其仁也。求也何如。子曰，求也，千室之邑，百乘之家，可使为之宰也，不知其仁也。赤也何如。子曰，赤也，束带立于朝，可使与宾客言也，不知其仁也。","pinyin":"mèng wǔ bó wèn zǐ lù rén hū zǐ yuē bù zhī yě yòu wèn zǐ yuē yóu yě qiān shèng zhī guó kě shǐ zhì qí fù yě bù zhī qí rén yě qiú yě hé rú zǐ yuē qiú yě qiān shì zhī yì bǎi shèng zhī jiā kě shǐ wéi zhī zǎi yě bù zhī qí rén yě chì yě hé rú zǐ yuē chì yě shù dài lì yú cháo kě shǐ yǔ bīn kè yán yě bù zhī qí rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Mang Wu asked about Tsze-lu, whether he was perfectly virtuous. The Master said, \"I do not know.\" 2. He asked again, when the Master replied, \"In a kingdom of a thousand chariots, Yu might be employed to manage the military levies, but I do not know whether he be perfectly virtuous.\" 3. \"And what do you say of Ch'iu?\" The Master replied, \"In a city of a thousand families, or a clan of a hundred chariots, Ch'iu might be employed as governor, but I do not know whether he is perfectly virtuous.\" 4. \"What do you say of Ch'ih?\" The Master replied, \"With his sash girt and standing in a court, Ch'ih might be employed to converse with the visitors and guests, but I do not know whether he is perfectly virtuous.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-008","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子谓子贡曰，女与回也，孰愈。对曰，赐也，何敢望回，回也，闻一以知十，赐也，闻一以知二。子曰，弗如也，吾与女，弗如也。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi zǐ gòng yuē rǔ yǔ huí yě shú yù duì yuē cì yě hé gǎn wàng huí huí yě wén yī yǐ zhī shí cì yě wén yī yǐ zhī èr zǐ yuē fú rú yě wú yǔ rǔ fú rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said to Tsze-kung, \"Which do you consider superior, yourself or Hui?\" 2. Tsze-kung replied, \"How dare I compare myself with Hui? Hui hears one point and knows all about a subject; I hear one point, and know a second.\" 3. The Master said, \"You are not equal to him. I grant you, you are not equal to him.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-009","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"宰予昼寝。子曰，朽木不可雕也，粪土之墙，不可杇也，于予与何诛。子曰，始吾于人也，听其言而信其行，今吾于人也，听其言而观其行，于予与改是。","pinyin":"zǎi yú zhòu qǐn zǐ yuē xiǔ mù bù kě diāo yě fèn tǔ zhī qiáng bù kě wū yě yú yú yú hé zhū zǐ yuē shǐ wú yú rén yě tīng qí yán ér xìn qí xíng jīn wú yú rén yě tīng qí yán ér guān qí xíng yú yú yú gǎi shì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsai Yu being asleep during the daytime, the Master said, \"Rotten wood cannot be carved; a wall of dirty earth will not receive the trowel. This Yu!—what is the use of my reproving him?\" 2. The Master said, \"At first, my way with men was to hear their words, and give them credit for their conduct. Now my way is to hear their words, and look at their conduct. It is from Yu that I have learned to make this change.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-010","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾未见刚者。或对曰，申枨。子曰，枨也欲，焉得刚。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú wèi jiàn gāng zhě huò duì yuē shēn chéng zǐ yuē chéng yě yù yān dé gāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I have not seen a firm and unbending man.\" Some one replied, \"There is Shan Ch'ang.\" \"Ch'ang,\" said the Master, \"is under the influence of his passions; how can he be pronounced firm and unbending?\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-011","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，我不欲人之加诸我也，吾亦欲无加诸人。子曰，赐也，非尔所及也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē wǒ bú yù rén zhī jiā zhū wǒ yě wú yì yù wú jiā zhū rén zǐ yuē cì yě fēi ěr suǒ jí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung said, \"What I do not wish men to do to me, I also wish not to do to men.\" The Master said, \"Ts'ze, you have not attained to that.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-012","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，夫子之文章，可得而闻也，夫子之言性与天道，不可得而闻也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē fū zǐ zhī wén zhāng kě dé ér wén yě fū zǐ zhī yán xìng yǔ tiān dào bù kě dé ér wén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung said, \"The Master's personal displays of his principles and ordinary descriptions of them may be heard. His discourses about man's nature, and the way of Heaven, cannot be heard.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-013","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子路有闻，未之能行，唯恐有闻。","pinyin":"zǐ lù yǒu wén wèi zhī néng xíng wéi kóng yòu wén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When Tsze-lu heard anything, if he had not yet succeeded in carrying it into practice, he was only afraid lest he should hear something else.","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-014","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子贡问曰，孔文子，何以谓之文也。子曰，敏而好学，不耻下问，是以谓之文也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yuē kǒng wén zǐ hé yǐ wèi zhī wén yě zǐ yuē mǐn ér hào xué bù chǐ xià wèn shì yǐ wèi zhī wén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked, saying, \"On what ground did Kung-wan get that title of Wan?\" The Master said, \"He was of an active nature and yet fond of learning, and he was not ashamed to ask and learn of his inferiors!—On these grounds he has been styled Wan.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-015","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子谓子产，有君子之道四焉，其行己也恭，其事上也敬，其养民也惠，其使民也义。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi zǐ chǎn yǒu jūn zǐ zhī dào sì yān qí xíng jǐ yě gōng qí shì shàng yě jìng qí yǎng mín yě huì qí shǐ mín yě yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said of Tsze-ch'an that he had four of the characteristics of a superior man:—in his conduct of himself, he was humble; in serving his superiors, he was respectful; in nourishing the people, he was kind; in ordering the people, he was just.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-016","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，晏平仲善与人交，久而敬之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yàn píng zhòng shàn yǔ rén jiāo jiǔ ér jìng zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Yen P'ing knew well how to maintain friendly intercourse. The acquaintance might be long, but he showed the same respect as at first.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-017","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，藏文仲，居蔡，山节藻棁，何如其知也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zāng wén zhòng jū cài shān jié zǎo zhuó hé rú qí zhì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Tsang Wan kept a large tortoise in a house, on the capitals of the pillars of which he had hills made, and with representations of duckweed on the small pillars above the beams supporting the rafters.—Of what sort was his wisdom?\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-018","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子张问曰，令尹子文三仕为令尹，无喜色，三已之，无愠色。旧令尹之政，必以吿新令尹，何如。子曰，忠矣。曰，仁矣乎。曰，未知，焉得仁。崔子弑齐君，陈文子有马十乘，弃而违之。至于他邦，则曰，犹吾大夫崔子也，违之，之一邦，则又曰，犹吾大夫崔子也，违之，何如。子曰，淸矣。曰，仁矣乎。曰，未知，焉得仁。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn yuē lìng yǐn zǐ wén sān shì wéi lìng yǐn wú xǐ sè sān yǐ zhī wú yùn sè jiù lìng yǐn zhī zhèng bì yǐ gào xīn lìng yǐn hé rú zǐ yuē zhōng yǐ yuē rén yǐ hū yuē wèi zhì yān dé rén cuī zǐ shì qí jūn chén wén zǐ yǒu mǎ shí shèng qì ér wéi zhī zhì yú tā bāng zé yuē yóu wú dà fū cuī zǐ yě wéi zhī zhī yì bāng zé yòu yuē yóu wú dà fū cuī zǐ yě wéi zhī hé rú zǐ yuē qīng yǐ yuē rén yǐ hū yuē wèi zhì yān dé rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang asked, saying, \"The minister Tsze-wan thrice took office, and manifested no joy in his countenance. Thrice he retired from office, and manifested no displeasure. He made it a point to inform the new minister of the way in which he had conducted the government;—what do you say of him?\" The Master replied. \"He was loyal.\" \"Was he perfectly virtuous?\" \"I do not know. How can he be pronounced perfectly virtuous?\" 2. Tsze-chang proceeded, \"When the officer Ch'ui killed the prince of Ch'i, Ch'an Wan, though he was the owner of forty horses, abandoned them and left the country. Coming to another State, he said, 'They are here like our great officer, Ch'ui,' and left it. He came to a second State, and with the same observation left it also;—what do you say of him?\" The Master replied, \"He was pure.\" \"Was he perfectly virtuous?\" \"I do not know. How can he be pronounced perfectly virtuous?\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-019","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"季文子三思而后行。子闻之曰，再，斯可矣。","pinyin":"jì wén zǐ sān sī ér hòu xíng zǐ wén zhī yuē zài sī kě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi Wan thought thrice, and then acted. When the Master was informed of it, he said, \"Twice may do.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-020","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子曰，宁武子，邦有道，则知，邦无道，则愚，其知可及也，其愚不可及也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē nìng wǔ zǐ bāng yǒu dào zé zhì bāng wú dào zé yú qí zhì kě jí yě qí yú bù kě jí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When good order prevailed in his country, Ning Wu acted the part of a wise man. When his country was in disorder, he acted the part of a stupid man. Others may equal his wisdom, but they cannot equal his stupidity.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-021","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子在陈曰，归与归与，吾党之小子狂简，斐然成章，不知所以裁之。","pinyin":"zǐ zài chén yuē guī yú guī yú wú dǎng zhī xiǎo zǐ kuáng jiǎn fěi rán chéng zhāng bù zhī suǒ yǐ cái zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When the Master was in Ch'an, he said, \"Let me return! Let me return! The little children of my school are ambitious and too hasty. They are accomplished and complete so far, but they do not know how to restrict and shape themselves.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-022","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，伯夷叔齐，不念旧恶，怨是用希。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bó yí shū qí bú niàn jiù è yuàn shì yòng xī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Po-i and Shu-ch'i did not keep the former wickednesses of men in mind, and hence the resentments directed towards them were few.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-023","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，孰谓微生高直，或乞醯焉，乞诸邻而与之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shú wèi wēi shēng gāo zhí huò qǐ xī yān qǐ zhū lín ér yǔ zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Who says of Wei-shang Kao that he is upright? One begged some vinegar of him, and he begged it of a neighbor and gave it to the man.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-024","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，巧言令色，足恭，左丘明耻之，丘亦耻之，匿怨而友其人，左丘明耻之，丘亦耻之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qiǎo yán lìng sè zú gōng zuǒ qiū míng chǐ zhī mǒu yì chǐ zhī nì yuàn ér yǒu qí rén zuǒ qiū míng chǐ zhī mǒu yì chǐ zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Fine words, an insinuating appearance, and excessive respect;—Tso Ch'iu-ming was ashamed of them. I also am ashamed of them. To conceal resentment against a person, and appear friendly with him;—Tso Ch'iu-ming was ashamed of such conduct. I also am ashamed of it.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-025","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"颜渊季路侍。子曰，盍各言尔志。子路曰，愿车马，衣轻裘，与朋友共，蔽之而无憾。颜渊曰，愿无伐善，无施劳。子路曰，愿闻子之志。子曰，老者安之，朋友信之，少者怀之。","pinyin":"yán yuān jì lù shì zǐ yuē hé gè yán ěr zhì zǐ lù yuē yuàn jū mǎ yī qīng qiú yǔ péng yǒu gòng bì zhī ér wú hàn yán yuān yuē yuàn wú fá shàn wú shī láo zǐ lù yuē yuàn wén zǐ zhī zhì zǐ yuē lǎo zhě ān zhī péng yǒu xìn zhī shào zhě huái zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yen Yuan and Chi Lu being by his side, the Master said to them, \"Come, let each of you tell his wishes.\" 2. Tsze-lu said, \"I should like, having chariots and horses, and light fur dresses, to share them with my friends, and though they should spoil them, I would not be displeased.\" 3. Yen Yuan said, \"I should like not to boast of my excellence, nor to make a display of my meritorious deeds.\" 4. Tsze-lu then said, \"I should like, sir, to hear your wishes.\" The Master said, \"They are, in regard to the aged, to give them rest; in regard to friends, to show them sincerity; in regard to the young, to treat them tenderly.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-026","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，已矣乎，吾未见能见其过，而内自讼者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǐ yǐ hū wú wèi jiàn néng jiàn qí guò ér nèi zì sòng zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"It is all over! I have not yet seen one who could perceive his faults, and inwardly accuse himself.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"gong-ye-chang-027","bookSlug":"gong-ye-chang","bookNumber":5,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，十室之邑，必有忠信，如丘者焉，不如丘之好学也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shí shì zhī yì bì yǒu zhōng xìn rú mǒu zhě yān bù rú mǒu zhī hào xué yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In a hamlet of ten families, there may be found one honourable and sincere as I am, but not so fond of learning.\"","themes":["analects","gong-ye-chang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"yong-ye","number":6,"zhTitle":"雍也","enTitle":"Yung Yey","chapterCount":28,"sentences":[{"id":"yong-ye-001","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，雍也可使南面。仲弓问子桑伯子。子曰，可也，简。仲弓曰，居敬而行简，以临其民，不亦可乎，居简而行简，无乃大简乎。子曰，雍之言然。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yōng yě kě shǐ nán miàn zhòng gōng wèn zǐ sāng bó zǐ zǐ yuē kě yě jiǎn zhòng gōng yuē jū jìng ér xíng jiǎn yǐ lín qí mín bú yì kě hū jū jiǎn ér xíng jiǎn wú nǎi tài jiǎn hū zǐ yuē yōng zhī yán rán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"There is Yung!—He might occupy the place of a prince.\" 2. Chung-kung asked about Tsze-sang Po-tsze. The Master said, \"He may pass. He does not mind small matters.\" 3. Chung-kung said, \"If a man cherish in himself a reverential feeling of the necessity of attention to business, though he may be easy in small matters in his government of the people, that may be allowed. But if he cherish in himself that easy feeling, and also carry it out in his practice, is not such an easy mode of procedure excessive?\" 4. The Master said, \"Yung's words are right.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-002","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"哀公问弟子孰为好学。孔子对曰，有颜回者好学，不迁怒，不贰过，不幸短命死矣，今也则亡，未闻好学者也。","pinyin":"āi gōng wèn dì zǐ shú wéi hào xué kǒng zǐ duì yuē yǒu yán huí zhě hào xué bù qiān nù bú èr guò bú xìng duǎn mìng sǐ yǐ jīn yě zé wú wèi wén hào xué zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ai asked which of the disciples loved to learn. 2.Confucius replied to him, \"There was Yen Hui; he loved to learn. He did not transfer his anger; he did not repeat a fault. Unfortunately, his appointed time was short and he died; and now there is not such another. I have not yet heard of any one who loves to learn as he did.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-003","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子华使于齐，冉子为其母请粟。子曰，与之釜。请益。曰，与之庾。冉子与之粟五秉。子曰，赤之适齐也，乘肥马，衣轻裘，吾闻之也，君子周急，不继富。原思为之宰，与之粟九百，辞。子曰，毋，以与尔邻里乡党乎。","pinyin":"zǐ huá shǐ yú qí rǎn zǐ wèi qí mǔ qǐng sù zǐ yuē yǔ zhī fǔ qǐng yì yuē yǔ zhī yǔ rǎn zǐ yǔ zhī sù wǔ bǐng zǐ yuē chì zhī shì qí yě chéng féi mǎ yì qīng qiú wú wén zhī yě jūn zǐ zhōu jí bú jì fù yuán sī wéi zhī zǎi yǔ zhī sù jiǔ bǎi cí zǐ yuē wú yǐ yǔ ěr lín lǐ xiāng dǎng hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-hwa being employed on a mission to Ch'i, the disciple Zan requested grain for his mother. The Master said, \"Give her a fu.\" Yen requested more. \"Give her an yu,\" said the Master. Yen gave her five ping. 2. The Master said, \"When Ch'ih was proceeding to Ch'i, he had fat horses to his carriage, and wore light furs. I have heard that a superior man helps the distressed, but does not add to the wealth of the rich.\" 3. Yuan Sze being made governor of his town by the Master, he gave him nine hundred measures of grain, but Sze declined them. 4. The Master said, \"Do not decline them. May you not give them away in the neighborhoods, hamlets, towns, and villages?\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-004","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子谓仲弓曰，犁牛之子骍且角，虽欲勿用，山川其舍诸。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi zhòng gōng yuē lí niú zhī zǐ xīng qiě jiǎo suī yù wù yòng shān chuān qí shě zhū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master, speaking of Chung-kung, said, \"If the calf of a brindled cow be red and horned, although men may not wish to use it, would the spirits of the mountains and rivers put it aside?\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-005","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，回也，其心三月不违仁，其余，则日月至焉而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē huí yě qí xīn sān yuè bù wéi rén qí yú zé rì yuè zhì yān ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Such was Hui that for three months there would be nothing in his mind contrary to perfect virtue. The others may attain to this on some days or in some months, but nothing more.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-006","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"季康子问仲由，可使从政也与。子曰，由也果，于从政乎何有。曰，赐也，可使从政也与。曰，赐也达，于从政乎何有。曰，求也，可使从政也与。曰，求也艺，于从政乎何有。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ wèn zhòng yóu kě shǐ cóng zhèng yě yú zǐ yuē yóu yě guǒ yú cóng zhèng hū hé yǒu yuē cì yě kě shǐ cóng zhèng yě yú yuē cì yě dá yú cóng zhèng hū hé yǒu yuē qiú yě kě shǐ cóng zhèng yě yú yuē qiú yě yì yú cóng zhèng hū hé yǒu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang asked about Chung-yu, whether he was fit to be employed as an officer of government. The Master said, \"Yu is a man of decision; what difficulty would he find in being an officer of government?\" K'ang asked, \"Is Ts'ze fit to be employed as an officer of government?\" and was answered, \"Ts'ze is a man of intelligence; what difficulty would he find in being an officer of government?\" And to the same question about Ch'iu the Master gave the same reply, saying, \"Ch'iu is a man of various ability.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-007","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"季氏使闵子骞为费宰，闵子骞曰，善为我辞焉，如有复我者，则吾必在汶上矣。","pinyin":"jì shì shǐ mǐn zǐ qiān wéi bì zǎi mǐn zǐ qiān yuē shàn wèi wǒ cí yān rú yǒu fù wǒ zhě zé wú bì zài wèn shàng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The chief of the Chi family sent to ask Min Tsze-ch'ien to be governor of Pi. Min Tsze-ch'ien said, \"Decline the offer for me politely. If any one come again to me with a second invitation, I shall be obliged to go and live on the banks of the Wan.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-008","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"伯牛有疾，子问之，自牖执其手，曰，亡之，命矣夫，斯人也，而有斯疾也，斯人也，而有斯疾也。","pinyin":"bó niú yǒu jí zǐ wèn zhī zì yǒu zhí qí shǒu yuē wú zhī mìng yǐ fú sī rén yě ér yǒu sī jí yě sī rén yě ér yǒu sī jí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Po-niu being ill, the Master went to ask for him. He took hold of his hand through the window, and said, \"It is killing him. It is the appointment of Heaven, alas! That such a man should have such a sickness! That such a man should have such a sickness!\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-009","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，贤哉回也，一箪食，一瓢飮，在陋巷，人不堪其忧，回也不改其乐，贤哉回也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xián zāi huí yě yì dān sì yì piáo yǐn zài lòu xiàng rén bù kān qí yōu huí yě bù gǎi qí lè xián zāi huí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Admirable indeed was the virtue of Hui! With a single bamboo dish of rice, a single gourd dish of drink, and living in his mean narrow lane, while others could not have endured the distress, he did not allow his joy to be affected by it. Admirable indeed was the virtue of Hui!\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-010","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"冉求曰，非不说子之道，力不足也。子曰，力不足者，中道而废，今女画。","pinyin":"rǎn qiú yuē fēi bú yuè zǐ zhī dào lì bù zú yě zǐ yuē lì bù zú zhě zhōng dào ér fèi jīn rǔ huà","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Yen Ch'iu said, \"It is not that I do not delight in your doctrines, but my strength is insufficient.\" The Master said, \"Those whose strength is insufficient give over in the middle of the way but now you limit yourself.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-011","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子谓子夏曰，女为君子儒，无为小人儒。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi zǐ xià yuē rǔ wéi jūn zǐ rú wú wéi xiǎo rén rú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said to Tsze-hsia, \"Do you be a scholar after the style of the superior man, and not after that of the mean man.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-012","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子游为武城宰，子曰，女得人焉耳乎。曰，有澹台灭明者，行不由径，非公事，未尝至于偃之室也。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu wéi wǔ chéng zǎi zǐ yuē rǔ dé rén yān ěr hū yuē yǒu tán tái miè míng zhě xíng bù yóu jìng fēi gōng shì wèi cháng zhì yú yǎn zhī shì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-yu being governor of Wu-ch'ang, the Master said to him, \"Have you got good men there?\" He answered, \"There is Tan-t'ai Mieh-ming, who never in walking takes a short cut, and never comes to my office, excepting on public business.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-013","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，孟之反不伐，奔而殿，将入门，策其马，曰，非敢后也，马不进也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mèng zhī fǎn bù fá bēn ér diàn jiāng rù mén cè qí mǎ yuē fēi gǎn hòu yě mǎ bú jìn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Mang Chih-fan does not boast of his merit. Being in the rear on an occasion of flight, when they were about to enter the gate, he whipped up his horse, saying, 'It is not that I dare to be last. My horse would not advance.'\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-014","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不有祝𬶍之佞，而有宋朝之美，难乎免于今之世矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bù yǒu zhù tuó zhī nìng ér yǒu sòng zhāo zhī měi nán hū miǎn yú jīn zhī shì yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Without the specious speech of the litanist T'o and the beauty of the prince Chao of Sung, it is difficult to escape in the present age.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-015","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，谁能出不由户，何莫由斯道也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shuí néng chū bù yóu hù hé mò yóu sī dào yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Who can go out but by the door? How is it that men will not walk according to these ways?\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-016","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，质胜文则野，文胜质则史，文质彬彬，然后君子。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì shèng wén zé yě wén shèng zhì zé shǐ wén zhì bīn bīn rán hòu jūn zǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Where the solid qualities are in excess of accomplishments, we have rusticity; where the accomplishments are in excess of the solid qualities, we have the manners of a clerk. When the accomplishments and solid qualities are equally blended, we then have the man of virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-017","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人之生也直，罔之生也，幸而免。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén zhī shēng yě zhí wǎng zhī shēng yě xìng ér miǎn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Man is born for uprightness. If a man lose his uprightness, and yet live, his escape from death is the effect of mere good fortune.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-018","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，知之者，不如好之者，好之者，不如乐之者。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhī zhī zhě bù rú hào zhī zhě hào zhī zhě bù rú lè zhī zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"They who know the truth are not equal to those who love it, and they who love it are not equal to those who delight in it.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-019","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，中人以上，可以语上也，中人以下，不可以语上也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhōng rén yǐ shàng kě yǐ yù shàng yě zhōng rén yǐ xià bù kě yǐ yù shàng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"To those whose talents are above mediocrity, the highest subjects may be announced. To those who are below mediocrity, the highest subjects may not be announced.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-020","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"樊迟问知。子曰，务民之义，敬鬼神而远之，可谓知矣。问仁。曰，仁者先难而后获，可谓仁矣。","pinyin":"fán chí wèn zhì zǐ yuē wù mín zhī yì jìng guǐ shén ér yuǎn zhī kě wèi zhì yǐ wèn rén yuē rén zhě xiān nán ér hòu huò kě wèi rén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Fan Ch'ih asked what constituted wisdom. The Master said, \"To give one's self earnestly to the duties due to men, and, while respecting spiritual beings, to keep aloof from them, may be called wisdom.\" He asked about perfect virtue. The Master said, \"The man of virtue makes the difficulty to be overcome his first business, and success only a subsequent consideration;—this may be called perfect virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-021","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，知者乐水，仁者乐山，知者动，仁者静，知者乐，仁者寿。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì zhě yào shuǐ rén zhě yào shān zhì zhě dòng rén zhě jìng zhì zhě lè rén zhě shòu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The wise find pleasure in water; the virtuous find pleasure in hills. The wise are active; the virtuous are tranquil. The wise are joyful; the virtuous are long-lived.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-022","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，齐一变，至于鲁，鲁一变，至于道。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qí yí biàn zhì yú lǔ lǔ yí biàn zhì yú dào","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Ch'i, by one change, would come to the State of Lu. Lu, by one change, would come to a State where true principles predominated.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-023","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，觚不觚，觚哉觚哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gū bù gū gū zāi gū zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A cornered vessel without corners.—A strange cornered vessel! A strange cornered vessel!\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-024","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"宰我问曰，仁者虽吿之曰，井有仁焉，其从之也。子曰，何为其然也，君子可逝也，不可陷也，可欺也，不可罔也。","pinyin":"zǎi wǒ wèn yuē rén zhě suī gào zhī yuē jǐng yǒu rén yān qí cóng zhī yě zǐ yuē hé wéi qí rán yě jūn zǐ kě shì yě bù kě xiàn yě kě qī yě bù kě wǎng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsai Wo asked, saying, \"A benevolent man, though it be told him,—\"There is a man in the well\" will go in after him, I suppose.\" Confucius said, \"Why should he do so?\" A superior man may be made to go to the well, but he cannot be made to go down into it. He may be imposed upon, but he cannot be fooled.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-025","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子博学于文，约之以礼，亦可以弗畔矣夫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bó xué yú wén yuē zhī yǐ lǐ yì kě yǐ fú pàn yǐ fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man, extensively studying all learning, and keeping himself under the restraint of the rules of propriety, may thus likewise not overstep what is right.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-026","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子见南子，子路不说，夫子矢之，曰，予所否者，天厌之，天厌之。","pinyin":"zǐ jiàn nán zǐ zǐ lù bú yuè fū zǐ shǐ zhī yuē yú suǒ fǒu zhě tiān yàn zhī tiān yàn zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master having visited Nan-tsze, Tsze-lu was displeased, on which the Master swore, saying, \"Wherein I have done improperly, may Heaven reject me, may Heaven reject me!\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-027","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，中庸之为德也，其至矣乎，民鲜久矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhōng yōng zhī wéi dé yě qí zhì yǐ hū mín xiǎn jiǔ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Perfect is the virtue which is according to the Constant Mean! Rare for a long time has been its practise among the people.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yong-ye-028","bookSlug":"yong-ye","bookNumber":6,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，如有博施于民，而能济众，何如，可谓仁乎。子曰，何事于仁，必也圣乎，尧舜其犹病诸。夫仁者，己欲立而立人，己欲达而达人。能近取譬，可谓仁之方也已。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē rú yǒu bó shī yú mín ér néng jì zhòng hé rú kě wèi rén hū zǐ yuē hé shì yú rén bì yě shèng hū yáo shùn qí yóu bìng zhū fú rén zhě jǐ yù lì ér lì rén jǐ yù dá ér dá rén néng jìn qǔ pì kě wèi rén zhī fāng yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung said, \"Suppose the case of a man extensively conferring benefits on the people, and able to assist all, what would you say of him? Might he be called perfectly virtuous?\" The Master said, \"Why speak only of virtue in connexion with him? Must he not have the qualities of a sage? Even Yao and Shun were still solicitous about this. 2. \"Now the man of perfect virtue, wishing to be established himself, seeks also to establish others; wishing to be enlarged himself, he seeks also to enlarge others. 3. \"To be able to judge of others by what is nigh in ourselves;—this may be called the art of virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yong-ye"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"shu-er","number":7,"zhTitle":"述而","enTitle":"Shu R.","chapterCount":37,"sentences":[{"id":"shu-er-001","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，述而不作，信而好古，窃比于我老彭。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shù ér bú zuò xìn ér hào gǔ qiè bǐ yú wǒ lǎo péng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A transmitter and not a maker, believing in and loving the ancients, I venture to compare myself with our old P'ang.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-002","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，默而识之，学而不厌，诲人不倦，何有于我哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mò ér zhì zhī xué ér bú yàn huì rén bú juàn hé yǒu yú wǒ zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The silent treasuring up of knowledge; learning without satiety; and instructing others without being wearied:—which one of these things belongs to me?\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-003","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，德之不修，学之不讲，闻义不能徒，不善不能改，是吾忧也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dé zhī bù xiū xué zhī bù jiǎng wén yì bù néng xǐ bú shàn bù néng gǎi shì wú yōu yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The leaving virtue without proper cultivation; the not thoroughly discussing what is learned; not being able to move towards righteousness of which a knowledge is gained; and not being able to change what is not good:—these are the things which occasion me solicitude.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-004","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子之燕居，申申如也，夭夭如也。","pinyin":"zǐ zhī yàn jū shēn shēn rú yě yāo yāo rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When the Master was unoccupied with business, his manner was easy, and he looked pleased.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-005","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，甚矣吾衰也，久矣，吾不复梦见周公。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shèn yǐ wú shuāi yě jiǔ yǐ wú bú fù mèng jiàn zhōu gōng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Extreme is my decay. For a long time, I have not dreamed, as I was wont to do, that I saw the duke of Chau.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-006","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，志于道。据于德。依于仁。游于艺。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì yú dào jù yú dé yī yú rén yóu yú yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Let the will be set on the path of duty. 2. \"Let every attainment in what is good be firmly grasped. 3. \"Let perfect virtue be accorded with. 4. \"Let relaxation and enjoyment be found in the polite arts.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-007","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，自行束修以上，吾未尝无诲焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zì xíng shù xiū yǐ shàng wú wèi cháng wú huì yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"From the man bringing his bundle of dried flesh for my teaching upwards, I have never refused instruction to any one.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-008","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不愤不启，不悱不发，擧一隅，不以三隅反，则不复也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú fèn bù qǐ bù fěi bù fā jǔ yì yú bù yǐ sān yú fǎn zé bú fù yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I do not open up the truth to one who is not eager to get knowledge, nor help out any one who is not anxious to explain himself. When I have presented one corner of a subject to any one, and he cannot from it learn the other three, I do not repeat my lesson.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-009","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子食于有丧者之侧，未尝饱也。子于是日哭，则不歌。","pinyin":"zǐ shí yú yǒu sāng zhě zhī cè wèi cháng bǎo yě zǐ yú shì rì kū zé bù gē","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When the Master was eating by the side of a mourner, he never ate to the full. 2. He did not sing on the same day in which he had been weeping.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-010","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子谓颜渊曰，用之则行，舍之则藏，惟我与尔有是夫。子路曰，子行三军则谁与。子曰，暴虎冯河，死而无悔者，吾不与也，必也临事而惧，好谋而成者也。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi yán yuān yuē yòng zhī zé xíng shě zhī zé cáng wéi wǒ yǔ ěr yǒu shì fú zǐ lù yuē zǐ xíng sān jūn zé shuí yǔ zǐ yuē bào hǔ píng hé sǐ ér wú huǐ zhě wú bù yǔ yě bì yě lín shì ér jù hào móu ér chéng zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said to Yen Yuan, \"When called to office, to undertake its duties; when not so called, to lie retired;—it is only I and you who have attained to this.\" 2. Tsze-lu said, \"If you had the conduct of the armies of a great State, whom would you have to act with you?\" 3. The Master said, \"I would not have him to act with me, who will unarmed attack a tiger, or cross a river without a boat, dying without any regret. My associate must be the man who proceeds to action full of solicitude, who is fond of adjusting his plans, and then carries them into execution.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-011","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，富而可求也，虽执鞭之士，吾亦为之，如不可求，从吾所好。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fù ér kě qiú yě suī zhí biān zhī shì wú yì wéi zhī rú bù kě qiú cóng wú suǒ hào","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If the search for riches is sure to be successful, though I should become a groom with whip in hand to get them, I will do so. As the search may not be successful, I will follow after that which I love.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-012","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子之所愼，齐，战，疾。","pinyin":"zǐ zhī suǒ shèn zhāi zhàn jí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The things in reference to which the Master exercised the greatest caution were —fasting, war, and sickness.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-013","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子在齐闻韶，三月不知肉味，曰，不图为乐之至于斯也。","pinyin":"zǐ zài qí wén sháo sān yuè bù zhī ròu wèi yuē bù tú wéi yuè zhī zhì yú sī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When the Master was in Ch'i, he heard the Shao, and for three months did not know the taste of flesh. \"I did not think\" he said, \"that music could have been made so excellent as this.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-014","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"冉有曰，夫子为衞君乎。子贡曰，诺，吾将问之。入曰，伯夷叔齐，何人也。曰，古之贤人也。曰，怨乎。曰，求仁而得仁，又何怨。出曰，夫子不为也。","pinyin":"rǎn yǒu yuē fū zǐ wèi wèi jūn hū zǐ gòng yuē nuò wú jiāng wèn zhī rù yuē bó yí shū qí hé rén yě yuē gǔ zhī xián rén yě yuē yuàn hū yuē qiú rén ér dé rén yòu hé yuàn chū yuē fū zǐ bú wèi yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yen Yu said, \"Is our Master for the ruler of Wei?\" Tsze-kung said, \"Oh! I will ask him.\" 2. He went in accordingly, and said, \"What sort of men were Po-i and Shu-ch'i?\" \"They were ancient worthies,\" said the Master. \"Did they have any repinings because of their course?\" The Master again replied, \"They sought to act virtuously, and they did so; what was there for them to repine about?\" On this, Tsze-kung went out and said, \"Our Master is not for him.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-015","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，饭疏食飮水，曲肱而枕之，乐亦在其中矣，不义而富且贵，于我如浮云。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fàn shū sì yǐn shuǐ qū gōng ér zhèn zhī lè yì zài qí zhōng yǐ bú yì ér fù qiě guì yú wǒ rú fú yún","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"With coarse rice to eat, with water to drink, and my bended arm for a pillow;—I have still joy in the midst of these things. Riches and honours acquired by unrighteousness, are to me as a floating cloud.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-016","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，加我数年，五十以学易，可以无大过矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jiā wǒ shù nián wǔ shí yǐ xué yì kě yǐ wú dà guò yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If some years were added to my life, I would give fifty to the study of the Yi, and then I might come to be without great faults.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-017","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子所雅言，诗，书，执礼，皆雅言也。","pinyin":"zǐ suǒ yǎ yán shī shū zhí lǐ jiē yǎ yán yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master's frequent themes of discourse were—the Odes, the History, and the maintenance of the Rules of Propriety. On all these he frequently discoursed.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-018","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"叶公问孔子于子路，子路不对。子曰，女奚不曰，其为人也，发愤忘食，乐以忘忧，不知老之将至云尔。","pinyin":"shè gōng wèn kǒng zǐ yú zǐ lù zǐ lù bú duì zǐ yuē rǔ xī bù yuē qí wéi rén yě fā fèn wàng shí lè yǐ wàng yōu bù zhī lǎo zhī jiāng zhì yún ěr","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke of Sheh asked Tsze-lu about Confucius, and Tsze-lu did not answer him. 2. The Master said, \"Why did you not say to him,—He is simply a man, who in his eager pursuit (of knowledge) forgets his food, who in the joy of its attainment forgets his sorrows, and who does not perceive that old age is coming on?\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-019","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，我非生而知之者，好古，敏以求之者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wǒ fēi shēng ér zhī zhī zhě hào gǔ mǐn yǐ qiú zhī zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I am not one who was born in the possession of knowledge; I am one who is fond of antiquity, and earnest in seeking it there.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-020","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子不语，怪，力，乱，神。","pinyin":"zǐ bù yǔ guài lì luàn shén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The subjects on which the Master did not talk, were—extraordinary things, feats of strength, disorder, and spiritual beings.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-021","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，三人行，必有我师焉，择其善者而从之，其不善者而改之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sān rén xíng bì yǒu wǒ shī yān zé qí shàn zhě ér cóng zhī qí bú shàn zhě ér gǎi zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When I walk along with two others, they may serve me as my teachers. I will select their good qualities and follow them, their bad qualities and avoid them.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-022","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，天生德于予，桓魋其如予何。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē tiān shēng dé yú yú huán tuí qí rú yú hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Heaven produced the virtue that is in me. Hwan T'ui—what can he do to me?\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-023","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，二三子，以我为隐乎。吾无隐乎尔。吾无行而不与二三子者，是丘也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē èr sān zǐ yǐ wǒ wéi yǐn hū wú wú yǐn hū ěr wú wú xíng ér bù yǔ èr sān zǐ zhě shì mǒu yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Do you think, my disciples, that I have any concealments? I conceal nothing from you. There is nothing which I do that is not shown to you, my disciples;—that is my way.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-024","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子以四教，文，行，忠，信。","pinyin":"zǐ yǐ sì jiào wén xíng zhōng xìn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"There were four things which the Master taught,—letters, ethics, devotion of soul, and truthfulness.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-025","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，圣人吾不得而见之矣，得见君子者，斯可矣。子曰，善人吾不得而见之矣，得见有恒者，斯可矣。亡而为有，虚而为盈，约而为泰，难乎有恒矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shèng rén wú bù dé ér jiàn zhī yǐ dé jiàn jūn zǐ zhě sī kě yǐ zǐ yuē shàn rén wú bù dé ér jiàn zhī yǐ dé jiàn yǒu héng zhě sī kě yǐ wú ér wéi yǒu xū ér wéi yíng yuē ér wéi tài nán hū yǒu héng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"A sage it is not mine to see; could I see a man of real talent and virtue, that would satisfy me.\" 2. The Master said, \"A good man it is not mine to see; could I see a man possessed of constancy, that would satisfy me. 3. \"Having not and yet affecting to have, empty and yet affecting to be full, straitened and yet affecting to be at ease:—it is difficult with such characteristics to have constancy.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-026","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子钓而不纲，弋不射宿。","pinyin":"zǐ diào ér bù gāng yì bú shè sù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master angled,—but did not use a net. He shot,—but not at birds perching.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-027","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，盖有不知而作之者，我无是也。多闻，择其善者而从之，多见而识之，知之次也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gài yǒu bù zhī ér zuò zhī zhě wǒ wú shì yě duō wén zé qí shàn zhě ér cóng zhī duō jiàn ér zhì zhī zhī zhī cì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"There may be those who act without knowing why. I do not do so. Hearing much and selecting what is good and following it; seeing much and keeping it in memory:—this is the second style of knowledge.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-028","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"互乡难与言，童子见，门人惑。子曰，与其进也，不与其退也，唯何甚，人洁己以进，与其洁也，不保其往也。","pinyin":"hù xiāng nán yǔ yán tóng zǐ xiàn mén rén huò zǐ yuē yǔ qí jìn yě bù yǔ qí tuì yě wéi hé shèn rén jié jǐ yǐ jìn yǔ qí jié yě bù bǎo qí wǎng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. It was difficult to talk (profitably and reputably) with the people of Hu-hsiang, and a lad of that place having had an interview with the Master, the disciples doubted. 2. The Master said, \"I admit people's approach to me without committing myself as to what they may do when they have retired. Why must one be so severe? If a man purify himself to wait upon me, I receive him so purified, without guaranteeing his past conduct.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-029","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":29,"classicalChinese":"子曰，仁远乎哉，我欲仁，斯仁至矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén yuǎn hū zāi wǒ yù rén sī rén zhì yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Is virtue a thing remote? I wish to be virtuous, and lo! virtue is at hand.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-030","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":30,"classicalChinese":"陈司败问昭公知礼乎。孔子曰，知礼。孔子退，揖巫马期而进之，曰，吾闻君子不党，君子亦党乎，君取于吴为同姓，谓之吴孟子，君而知礼，孰不知礼。巫马期以吿。子曰，丘也幸，苟有过，人必知之。","pinyin":"chén sī bài wèn zhāo gōng zhī lǐ hū kǒng zǐ yuē zhī lǐ kǒng zǐ tuì yī wū mǎ qī ér jìn zhī yuē wú wén jūn zǐ bù dǎng jūn zǐ yì dǎng hū jūn qǔ yú wú wéi tóng xìng wèi zhī wú mèng zǐ jūn ér zhī lǐ shú bù zhī lǐ wū mǎ qī yǐ gào zǐ yuē mǒu yě xìng gǒu yǒu guò rén bì zhī zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The minister of crime of Ch'an asked whether the duke Chao knew propriety, and Confucius said, \"He knew propriety.\" 2. Confucius having retired, the minister bowed to Wu-ma Ch'i to come forward, and said, \"I have heard that the superior man is not a partisan. May the superior man be a partisan also? The prince married a daughter of the house of Wu, of the same surname with himself, and called her,—'The elder Tsze of Wu.' If the prince knew propriety, who does not know it?\" 3. Wu-ma Ch'i reported these remarks, and the Master said, \"I am fortunate! If I have any errors, people are sure to know them.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-031","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":31,"classicalChinese":"子与人歌，而善，必使反之，而后和之。","pinyin":"zǐ yǔ rén gē ér shàn bì shǐ fǎn zhī ér hòu hè zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When the Master was in company with a person who was singing, if he sang well, he would make him repeat the song, while he accompanied it with his own voice.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-032","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":32,"classicalChinese":"子曰，文，莫吾犹人也，躬行君子，则吾未之有得。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wén mò wú yóu rén yě gōng xíng jūn zǐ zé wú wèi zhī yǒu dé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In letters I am perhaps equal to other men, but the character of the superior man, carrying out in his conduct what he professes, is what I have not yet attained to.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-033","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":33,"classicalChinese":"子曰，若圣与仁，则吾岂敢，抑为之不厌，诲人不倦，则可谓云尔已矣。公西华曰，正唯弟子不能学也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē ruò shèng yǔ rén zé wú qǐ gǎn yì wéi zhī bú yàn huì rén bú juàn zé kě wèi yún ěr yǐ yǐ gōng xī huá yuē zhèng wéi dì zǐ bù néng xué yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The sage and the man of perfect virtue;—how dare I rank myself with them? It may simply be said of me, that I strive to become such without satiety, and teach others without weariness.\" Kung-hsi Hwa said, \"This is just what we, the disciples, cannot imitate you in.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-034","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":34,"classicalChinese":"子疾病。子路请祷。子曰，有诸。子路对曰，有之，诔曰，祷尔于上下神祇。子曰，丘之祷久矣。","pinyin":"zǐ jí bìng zǐ lù qǐng dǎo zǐ yuē yǒu zhū zǐ lù duì yuē yǒu zhī lěi yuē dǎo ěr yú shàng xià shén qí zǐ yuē mǒu zhī dǎo jiǔ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master being very sick, Tsze-lu asked leave to pray for him. He said, \"May such a thing be done?\" Tsze-lu replied, \"It may. In the Eulogies it is said, 'Prayer has been made for thee to the spirits of the upper and lower worlds.'\" The Master said, \"My praying has been for a long time.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-035","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":35,"classicalChinese":"子曰，奢则不孙，俭则固，与其不孙也，宁固。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shē zé bú xùn jiǎn zé gù yǔ qí bú xùn yě nìng gù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Extravagance leads to insubordination, and parsimony to meanness. It is better to be mean than to be insubordinate.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-036","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":36,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子坦荡荡，小人长戚戚。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ tǎn dàng dàng xiǎo rén cháng qī qī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is satisfied and composed; the mean man is always full of distress.\"","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"shu-er-037","bookSlug":"shu-er","bookNumber":7,"sentenceNumber":37,"classicalChinese":"子温而厉，威而不猛，恭而安。","pinyin":"zǐ wēn ér lì wēi ér bù měng gōng ér ān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master was mild, and yet dignified; majestic, and yet not fierce; respectful, and yet easy.","themes":["analects","shu-er"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"tai-bo","number":8,"zhTitle":"泰伯","enTitle":"T'ai-po","chapterCount":21,"sentences":[{"id":"tai-bo-001","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，泰伯其可谓至德也已矣，三以天下让，民无得而称焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē tài bó qí kě wèi zhì dé yě yǐ yǐ sān yǐ tiān xià ràng mín wú dé ér chēng yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"T'ai-po may be said to have reached the highest point of virtuous action. Thrice he declined the kingdom, and the people in ignorance of his motives could not express their approbation of his conduct.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-002","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，恭而无礼则劳，愼而无礼则葸，勇而无礼则乱，直而无礼则绞。君子笃于亲，则民兴于仁，故旧不遗，则民不偷。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gōng ér wú lǐ zé láo shèn ér wú lǐ zé xǐ yǒng ér wú lǐ zé luàn zhí ér wú lǐ zé jiǎo jūn zǐ dǔ yú qīn zé mín xīng yú rén gù jiù bù yí zé mín bù tōu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Respectfulness, without the rules of propriety, becomes laborious bustle; carefulness, without the rules of propriety, becomes timidity; boldness, without the rules of propriety, becomes insubordination; straightforwardness, without the rules of propriety, becomes rudeness. 2. \"When those who are in high stations perform well all their duties to their relations, the people are aroused to virtue. 3. When old friends are not neglected by them, the people are preserved from meanness.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-003","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"曾子有疾，召门弟子曰，启予足，启予手，诗云，战战兢兢，如临深渊，如履薄冰，而今而后，吾知免夫，小子。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yǒu jí zhào mén dì zǐ yuē qǐ yú zú qǐ yú shǒu shī yún zhàn zhàn jīng jīng rú lín shēn yuān rú lǚ bó bīng ér jīn ér hòu wú zhī miǎn fú xiǎo zǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang being ill, he called to him the disciples of his school, and said, \"Uncover my feet, uncover my hands. It is said in the Book of Poetry, 'We should be apprehensive and cautious, as if on the brink of a deep gulf, as if treading on thin ice,' and so have I been. Now and hereafter, I know my escape from all injury to my person, O ye, my little children.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-004","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"曾子有疾，孟敬子问之。曾子言曰，鸟之将死，其鸣也哀，人之将死，其言也善。君子所贵乎道者三，动容貌，斯远暴慢矣，正颜色，斯近信笑，出辞气，斯远鄙倍矣，笾豆之事，则有司存。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yǒu jí mèng jìng zǐ wèn zhī zēng zǐ yán yuē niǎo zhī jiāng sǐ qí míng yě āi rén zhī jiāng sǐ qí yán yě shàn jūn zǐ suǒ guì hū dào zhě sān dòng róng mào sī yuǎn bào màn yǐ zhèng yán sè sī jìn xìn yǐ chū cí qì sī yuǎn bǐ bèi yǐ biān dòu zhī shì zé yǒu sī cún","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The philosopher Tsang being ill, Meng Chang went to ask how he was. 2. Tsang said to him, \"When a bird is about to die, its notes are mournful; when a man is about to die, his words are good. 3. \"There are three principles of conduct which the man of high rank should consider specially important:—that in his deportment and manner he keep from violence and heedlessness; that in regulating his countenance he keep near to sincerity; and that in his words and tones he keep far from lowness and impropriety. As to such matters as attending to the sacrificial vessels, there are the proper officers for them.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-005","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，以能问于不能，以多问于寡，有若无，实若虚，犯而不校，昔者吾友，尝从事于斯矣。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē yǐ néng wèn yú bù néng yǐ duō wèn yú guǎ yǒu ruò wú shí ruò xū fàn ér bú jiào xī zhě wú yǒu cháng cóng shì yú sī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"Gifted with ability, and yet putting questions to those who were not so; possessed of much, and yet putting questions to those possessed of little; having, as though he had not; full, and yet counting himself as empty; offended against, and yet entering into no altercation; formerly I had a friend who pursued this style of conduct.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-006","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，可以托六尺之孤，可以寄百里之命，临大节，而不可夺也，君子人与，君子人也。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē kě yǐ tuō liù chǐ zhī gū kě yǐ jì bǎi lǐ zhī mìng lín dà jié ér bù kě duó yě jūn zǐ rén yú jūn zǐ rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"Suppose that there is an individual who can be entrusted with the charge of a young orphan prince, and can be commissioned with authority over a state of a hundred li, and whom no emergency however great can drive from his principles:—is such a man a superior man? He is a superior man indeed.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-007","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，士，不可以不弘毅，任重而道远。仁以为己任，不亦重乎，死而后已，不亦远乎。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē shì bù kě yǐ bù hóng yì rèn zhòng ér dào yuǎn rén yǐ wéi jǐ rèn bú yì zhòng hū sǐ ér hòu yǐ bú yì yuǎn hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The philosopher Tsang said, \"The officer may not be without breadth of mind and vigorous endurance. His burden is heavy and his course is long. 2. \"Perfect virtue is the burden which he considers it is his to sustain;—is it not heavy? Only with death does his course stop;—is it not long?","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-008","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，兴于诗。立于礼。成于乐。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xīng yú shī lì yú lǐ chéng yú yuè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"It is by the Odes that the mind is aroused. 2. \"It is by the Rules of Propriety that the character is established. 3. \"It is from Music that the finish is received.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-009","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，民可使由之，不可使知之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mín kě shǐ yóu zhī bù kě shǐ zhī zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The people may be made to follow a path of action, but they may not be made to understand it.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-010","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，好勇疾贫，乱也，人而不仁，疾之已甚，乱也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē hào yǒng jí pín luàn yě rén ér bù rén jí zhī yǐ shèn luàn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The man who is fond of daring and is dissatisfied with poverty, will proceed to insubordination. So will the man who is not virtuous, when you carry your dislike of him to an extreme.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-011","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，如有周公之才之美，使骄且吝，其余不足观也已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rú yǒu zhōu gōng zhī cái zhī měi shǐ jiāo qiě lìn qí yú bù zú guān yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Though a man have abilities as admirable as those of the Duke of Chau, yet if he be proud and niggardly, those other things are really not worth being looked at.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-012","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，三年学，不至于谷，不易得也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sān nián xué bú zhì yú gǔ bú yì dé yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"It is not easy to find a man who has learned for three years without coming to be good.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-013","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，笃信好学，守死善道。危邦不入，乱邦不居，天下有道则见，无道则隐。邦有道，贫且贱焉，耻也，邦无道，富且贵焉，耻也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dǔ xìn hào xué shǒu sǐ shàn dào wēi bāng bú rù luàn bāng bù jū tiān xià yǒu dào zé xiàn wú dào zé yǐn bāng yǒu dào pín qiě jiàn yān chǐ yě bāng wú dào fù qiě guì yān chǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"With sincere faith he unites the love of learning; holding firm to death, he is perfecting the excellence of his course. 2. \"Such an one will not enter a tottering State, nor dwell in a disorganized one. When right principles of government prevail in the kingdom, he will show himself; when they are prostrated, he will keep concealed. 3. \"When a country is well-governed, poverty and a mean condition are things to be ashamed of. When a country is ill-governed, riches and honour are things to be ashamed of.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-014","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不在其位，不谋其政。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú zài qí wèi bù móu qí zhèng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who is not in any particular office, has nothing to do with plans for the administration of its duties.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-015","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，师挚之始，关睢之乱，洋洋乎盈耳哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shī zhì zhī shǐ guān jū zhī luàn yáng yáng hū yíng ěr zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When the music master Chih first entered on his office, the finish of the Kwan Tsu was magnificent;—how it filled the ears!\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-016","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，狂而不直，侗而不愿，悾悾而不信，吾不知之矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē kuáng ér bù zhí tóng ér bú yuàn kōng kōng ér bú xìn wú bù zhī zhī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Ardent and yet not upright; stupid and yet not attentive; simple and yet not sincere:—such persons I do not understand.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-017","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，学如不及，犹恐失之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xué rú bù jí yóu kǒng shī zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Learn as if you could not reach your object, and were always fearing also lest you should lose it.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-018","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，巍巍乎，舜禹之有天下也，而不与焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wēi wēi hū shùn yǔ zhī yǒu tiān xià yě ér bú yù yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"How majestic was the manner in which Shun and Yu held possession of the empire, as if it were nothing to them!\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-019","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，大哉，尧之为君也，巍巍乎，唯天为大，唯尧则之，荡荡乎，民无能名焉。巍巍乎，其有成功也，焕乎，其有文章。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dà zāi yáo zhī wéi jūn yě wēi wēi hū wéi tiān wéi dà wéi yáo zé zhī dàng dàng hū mín wú néng míng yān wēi wēi hū qí yǒu chéng gōng yě huàn hū qí yǒu wén zhāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Great indeed was Yao as a sovereign! How majestic was he! It is only Heaven that is grand, and only Yao corresponded to it. How vast was his virtue! The people could find no name for it. 2. \"How majestic was he in the works which he accomplished! How glorious in the elegant regulations which he instituted!\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-020","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"舜有臣五人，而天下治。武王曰，予有乱臣十人。孔子曰，才难，不其然乎，唐虞之际，于斯为盛，有妇人焉，九人而已。三分天下有其二，以服事殷，周之德，其可谓至德也已矣。","pinyin":"shùn yǒu chén wǔ rén ér tiān xià zhì wǔ wáng yuē yú yǒu luàn chén shí rén kǒng zǐ yuē cái nán bù qí rán hū táng yú zhī jì yú sī wéi shèng yǒu fù rén yān jiǔ rén ér yǐ sān fēn tiān xià yǒu qí èr yǐ fú shì yīn zhōu zhī dé qí kě wèi zhì dé yě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Shun had five ministers, and the empire was well-governed. 2. King Wu said, \"I have ten able ministers.\" 3. Confucius said, \"Is not the saying that talents are difficult to find, true? Only when the dynasties of T'ang and Yu met, were they more abundant than in this of Chau, yet there was a woman among them. The able ministers were no more than nine men. 4. \"King Wan possessed two of the three parts of the empire, and with those he served the dynasty of Yin. The virtue of the house of Chau may be said to have reached the highest point indeed.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"tai-bo-021","bookSlug":"tai-bo","bookNumber":8,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，禹吾无间然矣，菲飮食，而致孝乎鬼神，恶衣服，而致美乎黻冕，卑宫室，而尽力乎沟恤，禹吾无间然矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǔ wú wú jiàn rán yǐ fěi yǐn shí ér zhì xiào hū guǐ shén è yī fú ér zhì měi hū fú miǎn bēi gōng shì ér jìn lì hū gōu xù yǔ wú wú jiàn rán yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I can find no flaw in the character of Yu. He used himself coarse food and drink, but displayed the utmost filial piety towards the spirits. His ordinary garments were poor, but he displayed the utmost elegance in his sacrificial cap and apron. He lived in a low mean house, but expended all his strength on the ditches and water-channels. I can find nothing like a flaw in Yu.\"","themes":["analects","tai-bo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"zi-han","number":9,"zhTitle":"子罕","enTitle":"Tsze Han","chapterCount":30,"sentences":[{"id":"zi-han-001","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子罕言，利，与命，与仁。","pinyin":"zǐ hǎn yán lì yǔ mìng yǔ rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The subjects of which the Master seldom spoke were—profitableness, and also the appointments of Heaven, and perfect virtue.","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-002","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"达巷党人曰，大哉孔子，博学而无所成名。子闻之，谓门弟子曰，吾何执，执御乎，执射乎，吾执御矣。","pinyin":"dá xiàng dǎng rén yuē dà zāi kǒng zǐ bó xué ér wú suǒ chéng míng zǐ wén zhī wèi mén dì zǐ yuē wú hé zhí zhí yù hū zhí shè hū wú zhí yù yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. A man of the village of Ta-hsiang said, \"Great indeed is the philosopher K'ung! His learning is extensive, and yet he does not render his name famous by any particular thing.\" 2. The Master heard the observation, and said to his disciples, \"What shall I practise? Shall I practise charioteering, or shall I practise archery? I will practise charioteering.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-003","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，麻冕，礼也，今也纯，俭，吾从众。拜下，礼也。今拜乎上，泰也，虽违众，吾从下。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē má miǎn lǐ yě jīn yě chún jiǎn wú cóng zhòng bài xià lǐ yě jīn bài hū shàng tài yě suī wéi zhòng wú cóng xià","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"The linen cap is that prescribed by the rules of ceremony, but now a silk one is worn. It is economical, and I follow the common practice. 2. \"The rules of ceremony prescribe the bowing below the hall, but now the practice is to bow only after ascending it. That is arrogant. I continue to bow below the hall, though I oppose the common practice.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-004","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子绝四，毋意，毋必，毋固，毋我。","pinyin":"zǐ jué sì wú yì wú bì wú gù wú wǒ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"There were four things from which the Master was entirely free. He had no foregone conclusions, no arbitrary predeterminations, no obstinacy, and no egoism.","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-005","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子畏于匡。曰，文王既没，文不在兹乎。天之将丧斯文也，后死者不得与于斯文也，天之未丧斯文也，匡人其如予何。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi yú kuāng yuē wén wáng jì mò wén bú zài zī hū tiān zhī jiāng sàng sī wén yě hòu sǐ zhě bù dé yù yú sī wén yě tiān zhī wèi sàng sī wén yě kuāng rén qí rú yú hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master was put in fear in K'wang. 2. He said, \"After the death of King Wan, was not the cause of truth lodged here in me? 3. \"If Heaven had wished to let this cause of truth perish, then I, a future mortal, should not have got such a relation to that cause. While Heaven does not let the cause of truth perish, what can the people of K'wang do to me?\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-006","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"大宰问于子贡，曰，夫子圣者与，何其多能也。子贡曰，固天纵之将圣，又多能也。子闻之曰，大宰知我乎，吾少也贱，故多能，鄙事，君子多乎哉，不多也。牢曰，子云，吾不试，故艺。","pinyin":"tài zǎi wèn yú zǐ gòng yuē fū zǐ shèng zhě yú hé qí duō néng yě zǐ gòng yuē gù tiān zòng zhī jiāng shèng yòu duō néng yě zǐ wén zhī yuē tài zǎi zhī wǒ hū wú shào yě jiàn gù duō néng bǐ shì jūn zǐ duō hū zāi bù duō yě láo yuē zǐ yún wú bú shì gù yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. A high officer asked Tsze-kung, saying, \"May we not say that your Master is a sage? How various is his ability!\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"Certainly Heaven has endowed him unlimitedly. He is about a sage. And, moreover, his ability is various.\" 3. The Master heard of the conversation and said, \"Does the high officer know me? When I was young, my condition was low, and therefore I acquired my ability in many things, but they were mean matters. Must the superior man have such variety of ability? He does not need variety of ability.\" 4. Lao said, \"The Master said, 'Having no official employment, I acquired many arts.'\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-007","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾有知乎哉，无知也，有鄙夫问于我，空空如也，我叩其两端而竭焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú yǒu zhī hū zāi wú zhī yě yǒu bǐ fū wèn yú wǒ kōng kōng rú yě wǒ kòu qí liǎng duān ér jié yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Am I indeed possessed of knowledge? I am not knowing. But if a mean person, who appears quite empty-like, ask anything of me, I set it forth from one end to the other, and exhaust it.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-008","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，凤鸟不至，河不出图，吾已矣乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fèng niǎo bú zhì hé bù chū tú wú yǐ yǐ fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The fang bird does not come; the river sends forth no map:—it is all over with me!\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-009","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子见齐衰者，冕衣裳者，与瞽者，见之，虽少必作，过之必趋。","pinyin":"zǐ jiàn zī cuī zhě miǎn yī cháng zhě yǔ gǔ zhě jiàn zhī suī shào bì zuò guò zhī bì qū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When the Master saw a person in a mourning dress, or any one with the cap and upper and lower garments of full dress, or a blind person, on observing them approaching, though they were younger than himself, he would rise up, and if he had to pass by them, he would do so hastily.","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-010","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"颜渊喟然叹曰，仰之弥高，钻之弥坚，瞻之在前，忽焉在后。夫子循循然善诱人，博我以文，约我以礼。欲罢不能，既竭吾才，如有所立卓尔，虽欲从之，末由也已。","pinyin":"yán yuān kuì rán tàn yuē yǎng zhī mí gāo zuān zhī mí jiān zhān zhī zài qián hū yān zài hòu fū zǐ xún xún rán shàn yòu rén bó wǒ yǐ wén yuē wǒ yǐ lǐ yù bà bù néng jì jié wú cái rú yǒu suǒ lì zhuó ěr suī yù cóng zhī mò yóu yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yen Yuan, in admiration of the Master's doctrines, sighed and said, \"I looked up to them, and they seemed to become more high; I tried to penetrate them, and they seemed to become more firm; I looked at them before me, and suddenly they seemed to be behind. 2. \"The Master, by orderly method, skilfully leads men on. He enlarged my mind with learning, and taught me the restraints of propriety. 3. \"When I wish to give over the study of his doctrines, I cannot do so, and having exerted all my ability, there seems something to stand right up before me; but though I wish to follow and lay hold of it, I really find no way to do so.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-011","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子疾病，子路使门人为臣。病间曰，久矣哉，由之行诈也，无臣而为有臣，吾谁欺，欺天乎。且予与其死于臣之手也，无宁死于二三子之手乎，且予纵不得大葬，予死于道路乎。","pinyin":"zǐ jí bìng zǐ lù shǐ mén rén wéi chén bìng jiān yuē jiǔ yǐ zāi yóu zhī xíng zhà yě wú chén ér wéi yǒu chén wú shuí qī qī tiān hū qiě yú yǔ qí sǐ yú chén zhī shǒu yě wú nìng sǐ yú èr sān zǐ zhī shǒu hū qiě yú zòng bù dé dà zàng yú sǐ yú dào lù hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master being very ill, Tsze-lu wished the disciples to act as ministers to him. 2. During a remission of his illness, he said, \"Long has the conduct of Yu been deceitful! By pretending to have ministers when I have them not, whom should I impose upon? Should I impose upon Heaven? 3. \"Moreover, than that I should die in the hands of ministers, is it not better that I should die in the hands of you, my disciples? And though I may not get a great burial, shall I die upon the road?\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-012","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，有美玉于斯，韫匵而藏诸，求善贾而沽诸。子曰，沽之哉，沽之哉，我待贾者也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē yǒu měi yù yú sī yùn dú ér cáng zhū qiú shàn gǔ ér gū zhū zǐ yuē gū zhī zāi gū zhī zāi wǒ dài gǔ zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung said, \"There is a beautiful gem here. Should I lay it up in a case and keep it? or should I seek for a good price and sell it?\" The Master said, \"Sell it! Sell it! But I would wait for one to offer the price.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-013","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子欲居九夷。或曰，陋，如之何。子曰，君子居之，何陋之有。","pinyin":"zǐ yù jū jiǔ yí huò yuē lòu rú zhī hé zǐ yuē jūn zǐ jū zhī hé lòu zhī yǒu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master was wishing to go and live among the nine wild tribes of the east. 2. Some one said, \"They are rude. How can you do such a thing?\" The Master said, \"If a superior man dwelt among them, what rudeness would there be?\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-014","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾自衞反鲁，然后乐正，雅颂各得其所。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú zì wèi fǎn lǔ rán hòu yuè zhèng yǎ sòng gè dé qí suǒ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I returned from Wei to Lu, and then the music was reformed, and the pieces in the Royal songs and Praise songs all found their proper places.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-015","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，出则事公卿，入则事父兄，丧事不敢不勉，不为酒困，何有于我哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē chū zé shì gōng qīng rù zé shì fù xiōng sāng shì bù gǎn bù miǎn bù wéi jiǔ kùn hé yǒu yú wǒ zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Abroad, to serve the high ministers and nobles; at home, to serve one's father and elder brothers; in all duties to the dead, not to dare not to exert one's self; and not to be overcome of wine:—which one of these things do I attain to?\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-016","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子在川上曰，逝者如斯夫，不舍昼夜。","pinyin":"zǐ zài chuān shàng yuē shì zhě rú sī fú bù shě zhòu yè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master standing by a stream, said, \"It passes on just like this, not ceasing day or night!\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-017","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾未见好德，如好色者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú wèi jiàn hào dé rú hào sè zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I have not seen one who loves virtue as he loves beauty.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-018","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，譬如为山，未成一篑，止，吾止也，譬如平地，虽覆一篑，进，吾往也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē pì rú wéi shān wèi chéng yí kuì zhǐ wú zhǐ yě pì rú píng dì suī fù yí kuì jìn wú wǎng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The prosecution of learning may be compared to what may happen in raising a mound. If there want but one basket of earth to complete the work, and I stop, the stopping is my own work. It may be compared to throwing down the earth on the level ground. Though but one basketful is thrown at a time, the advancing with it is my own going forward.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-019","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，语之而不惰者，其回也与。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yù zhī ér bú duò zhě qí huí yě yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Never flagging when I set forth anything to him;—ah! that is Hui.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-020","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子谓颜渊曰，惜乎，吾见其进也，未见其止也。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi yán yuān yuē xī hū wú jiàn qí jìn yě wèi jiàn qí zhǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said of Yen Yuan, \"Alas! I saw his constant advance. I never saw him stop in his progress.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-021","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，苗而不秀者，有矣夫，秀而不实者，有矣夫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē miáo ér bú xiù zhě yǒu yǐ fú xiù ér bù shí zhě yǒu yǐ fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"There are cases in which the blade springs, but the plant does not go on to flower! There are cases where it flowers, but no fruit is subsequently produced!\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-022","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，后生可畏，焉知来者之不如今也，四十五十而无闻焉，斯亦不足畏也已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē hòu shēng kě wèi yān zhī lái zhě zhī bù rú jīn yě sì shí wǔ shí ér wú wén yān sī yì bù zú wèi yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A youth is to be regarded with respect. How do we know that his future will not be equal to our present? If he reach the age of forty or fifty, and has not made himself heard of, then indeed he will not be worth being regarded with respect.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-023","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，法语之言，能无从乎，改之为贵，巽与之言，能无说乎，绎之为贵，说而不绎，从而不改，吾末如之何也已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē fǎ yǔ zhī yán néng wú cóng hū gǎi zhī wéi guì xùn yǔ zhī yán néng wú yuè hū yì zhī wéi guì yuè ér bú yì cóng ér bù gǎi wú mò rú zhī hé yě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Can men refuse to assent to the words of strict admonition? But it is reforming the conduct because of them which is valuable. Can men refuse to be pleased with words of gentle advice? But it is unfolding their aim which is valuable. If a man be pleased with these words, but does not unfold their aim, and assents to those, but does not reform his conduct, I can really do nothing with him.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-024","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，主忠信，毋友不如己者，过则勿惮改。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhǔ zhōng xìn wú yǒu bù rú jǐ zhě guò zé wù dàn gǎi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Hold faithfulness and sincerity as first principles. Have no friends not equal to yourself. When you have faults, do not fear to abandon them.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-025","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，三军可夺师也，匹夫不可夺志也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sān jūn kě duó shuài yě pǐ fū bù kě duó zhì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The commander of the forces of a large state may be carried off, but the will of even a common man cannot be taken from him.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-026","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，衣敝缊袍，与衣孤貉者立，而不耻者，其由也与。不忮不求，何用不臧。子路终身诵之，子曰，是道也，何足以臧。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yì bì yùn páo yǔ yì hú hé zhě lì ér bù chǐ zhě qí yóu yě yú bú zhì bù qiú hé yòng bù zāng zǐ lù zhōng shēn sòng zhī zǐ yuē shì dào yě hé zú yǐ zāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Dressed himself in a tattered robe quilted with hemp, yet standing by the side of men dressed in furs, and not ashamed;—ah! it is Yu who is equal to this! 2. \"'He dislikes none, he covets nothing;—what can he do but what is good!'\" 3. Tsze-lu kept continually repeating these words of the ode, when the Master said, \"Those things are by no means sufficient to constitute (perfect) excellence.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-027","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，岁寒，然后知松柏之后雕也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē suì hán rán hòu zhī sōng bǎi zhī hòu diāo yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When the year becomes cold, then we know how the pine and the cypress are the last to lose their leaves.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-028","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"子曰，知者不惑，仁者不忧，勇者不惧。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì zhě bú huò rén zhě bù yōu yǒng zhě bú jù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The wise are free from perplexities; the virtuous from anxiety; and the bold from fear.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-029","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":29,"classicalChinese":"子曰，可与共学，未可与适道，可与适道，未可与立，可与立，未可与权。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē kě yǔ gòng xué wèi kě yǔ shì dào kě yǔ shì dào wèi kě yǔ lì kě yǔ lì wèi kě yǔ quán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"There are some with whom we may study in common, but we shall find them unable to go along with us to principles. Perhaps we may go on with them to principles, but we shall find them unable to get established in those along with us. Or if we may get so established along with them, we shall find them unable to weigh occurring events along with us.\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-han-030","bookSlug":"zi-han","bookNumber":9,"sentenceNumber":30,"classicalChinese":"唐棣之华，偏其反而，岂不尔思，室是远而。子曰，未之思也，夫何远之有。","pinyin":"táng dì zhī huā piān qí fǎn ér qǐ bù ěr sī shì shì yuǎn ér zǐ yuē wèi zhī sī yě fú hé yuǎn zhī yǒu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. How the flowers of the aspen-plum flutter and turn! Do I not think of you? But your house is distant. 2. The Master said, \"It is the want of thought about it. How is it distant?\"","themes":["analects","zi-han"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"xiang-dang","number":10,"zhTitle":"乡党","enTitle":"Heang Tang","chapterCount":18,"sentences":[{"id":"xiang-dang-001","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"孔子于乡党，恂恂如也，似不能言者。其在宗庙朝廷，便便言，唯谨尔。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yú xiāng dǎng xún xún rú yě sì bù néng yán zhě qí zài zōng miào cháo tíng pián pián yán wéi jǐn ěr","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Confucius, in his village, looked simple and sincere, and as if he were not able to speak. 2. When he was in the prince's ancestorial temple, or in the court, he spoke minutely on every point, but cautiously.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-002","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"朝，与下大夫言，侃侃如也，与上大夫言，訚訚如也。君在，踧踖如也，与与如也。","pinyin":"cháo yǔ xià dà fū yán kǎn kǎn rú yě yǔ shàng dà fū yán yín yín rú yě jūn zài cù jí rú yě yú yú rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When he was waiting at court, in speaking with the great officers of the lower grade, he spake freely, but in a straightforward manner; in speaking with those of the higher grade, he did so blandly, but precisely. 2. When the ruler was present, his manner displayed respectful uneasiness; it was grave, but self-possessed.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-003","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"君召使摈，色勃如也，足躩如也。揖所与立，左右手，衣前后，檐如也。趋进，翼如也。宾退，必复命曰，宾不顾矣。","pinyin":"jūn zhào shǐ bìn sè bó rú yě zú jué rú yě yī suǒ yǔ lì zuǒ yòu shǒu yī qián hòu chān rú yě qū jìn yì rú yě bīn tuì bì fù mìng yuē bīn bú gù yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When the prince called him to employ him in the reception of a visitor, his countenance appeared to change, and his legs to move forward with difficulty. 2. He inclined himself to the other officers among whom he stood, moving his left or right arm, as their position required, but keeping the skirts of his robe before and behind evenly adjusted. 3. He hastened forward, with his arms like the wings of a bird. 4. When the guest had retired, he would report to the prince, \"The visitor is not turning round any more.\"","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-004","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"入公门，鞠躬如也，如不容。立不中门，行不履阈。过位，色勃如也，足躩如也，其言似不足者。摄齐升堂，鞠躬如也，屛气似不息者。出，降一等，逞颜色，怡怡如也，没阶，趋进，翼如也，复其位，踧踖如也。","pinyin":"rù gōng mén jú gōng rú yě rú bù róng lì bù zhōng mén xíng bù lǚ yù guò wèi sè bó rú yě zú jué rú yě qí yán sì bù zú zhě shè zī shēng táng jú gōng rú yě bǐng qì sì bù xī zhě chū jiàng yì děng chěng yán sè yí yí rú yě mò jiē qū jìn yì rú yě fù qí wèi cù jí rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When he entered the palace gate, he seemed to bend his body, as if it were not sufficient to admit him. 2. When he was standing, he did not occupy the middle of the gate-way; when he passed in or out, he did not tread upon the threshold. 3. When he was passing the vacant place of the prince, his countenance appeared to change, and his legs to bend under him, and his words came as if he hardly had breath to utter them. 4. He ascended the reception hall, holding up his robe with both his hands, and his body bent; holding in his breath also, as if he dared not breathe. 5. When he came out from the audience, as soon as he had descended one step, he began to relax his countenance, and had a satisfied look. When he had got to the bottom of the steps, he advanced rapidly to his place, with his arms like wings, and on occupying it, his manner still showed respectful uneasiness.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-005","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"执圭，鞠躬如也，如不胜，上如揖，下如授，勃如战色，足蹜蹜如有循。享礼，有容色。私觌，愉愉如也。","pinyin":"zhí guī jú gōng rú yě rú bù shēng shàng rú yī xià rú shòu bó rú zhàn sè zú sù sù rú yǒu xún xiǎng lǐ yǒu róng sè sī dí yú yú rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When he was carrying the scepter of his ruler, he seemed to bend his body, as if he were not able to bear its weight. He did not hold it higher than the position of the hands in making a bow, nor lower than their position in giving anything to another. His countenance seemed to change, and look apprehensive, and he dragged his feet along as if they were held by something to the ground. 2. In presenting the presents with which he was charged, he wore a placid appearance. 3. At his private audience, he looked highly pleased.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-006","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"君子不以绀緅饰。红紫不以为亵服。当暑袗𫄨绤，必表而出之。缁衣羔裘，素衣麑裘，黄衣狐裘。亵裘长，短右袂。必有寝衣，长一身有半。狐貉之厚以居。去丧，无所不佩。非帷裳，必杀之。羔裘玄冠不以吊。吉月，必朝服而朝。","pinyin":"jūn zǐ bù yǐ gàn zōu shì hóng zǐ bù yǐ wéi xiè fú dāng shǔ zhěn chī xì bì biǎo ér chū zhī zī yī gāo qiú sù yī ní qiú huáng yī hú qiú xiè qiú cháng duǎn yòu mèi bì yǒu qǐn yī cháng yì shēn yòu bàn hú hé zhī hòu yǐ jū qù sāng wú suǒ bú pèi fēi wéi cháng bì shài zhī gāo qiú xuán guān bù yǐ diào jí yuè bì cháo fú ér cháo","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The superior man did not use a deep purple, or a puce colour, in the ornaments of his dress. 2. Even in his undress, he did not wear anything of a red or reddish colour. 3. In warm weather, he had a single garment either of coarse or fine texture, but he wore it displayed over an inner garment. 4. Over lamb's fur he wore a garment of black; over fawn's fur one of white; and over fox's fur one of yellow. 5. The fur robe of his undress was long, with the right sleeve short. 6. He required his sleeping dress to be half as long again as his body. 7. When staying at home, he used thick furs of the fox or the badger. 8. When he put off mourning, he wore all the appendages of the girdle. 9. His under-garment, except when it was required to be of the curtain shape, was made of silk cut narrow above and wide below. 10. He did not wear lamb's fur or a black cap, on a visit of condolence. 11. On the first day of the month he put on his court robes, and presented himself at court.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-007","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"齐，必有明衣，布。齐必变食，居必迁坐。","pinyin":"zhāi bì yǒu míng yī bù zhāi bì biàn shí jū bì qiān zuò","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When fasting, he thought it necessary to have his clothes brightly clean and made of linen cloth. 2. When fasting, he thought it necessary to change his food, and also to change the place where he commonly sat in the apartment.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-008","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"食不厌精，脍不厌细。食饐而餲，鱼馁而肉败，不食，色恶不食，臭恶不食，失饪不食，不时不食。割不正不食，不得其酱不食。肉虽多，不使胜食气，惟酒无量，不及乱。沽酒市脯不食。不撤姜食。不多食。祭于公，不宿肉。祭肉不出三日，出三日，不食之矣。食不语，寝不言。虽疏食菜羹，瓜祭，必齐如也。","pinyin":"sì bù yān jīng kuài bù yān xì sì yì ér ài yú něi ér ròu bài bù shí sè è bù shí xiù è bù shí shī rèn bù shí bù shí bù shí gē bú zhèng bù shí bù dé qí jiàng bù shí ròu suī duō bù shǐ shèng sì qì wéi jiǔ wú liàng bù jí luàn gū jiǔ shì fǔ bù shí bú chè jiāng shí bù duō shí jì yú gōng bú sù ròu jì ròu bù chū sān rì chū sān rì bù shí zhī yǐ shí bù yǔ qǐn bù yán suī shū sì cài gēng guā jì bì zhāi rú yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. He did not dislike to have his rice finely cleaned, nor to have his minced meat cut quite small. 2. He did not eat rice which had been injured by heat or damp and turned sour, nor fish or flesh which was gone. He did not eat what was discoloured, or what was of a bad flavour, nor anything which was ill-cooked, or was not in season. 3. He did not eat meat which was not cut properly, nor what was served without its proper sauce. 4. Though there might be a large quantity of meat, he would not allow what he took to exceed the due proportion for the rice. It was only in wine that he laid down no limit for himself, but he did not allow himself to be confused by it. 5. He did not partake of wine and dried meat bought in the market. 6. He was never without ginger when he ate. 7. He did not eat much. 8. When he had been assisting at the prince's sacrifice, he did not keep the flesh which he received overnight. The flesh of his family sacrifice he did not keep over three days. If kept over three days, people could not eat it. 9. When eating, he did not converse. When in bed, he did not speak. 10. Although his food might be coarse rice and vegetable soup, he would offer a little of it in sacrifice with a grave, respectful air.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-009","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"席不正不坐。","pinyin":"xí bú zhèng bú zuò","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"If his mat was not straight, he did not sit on it.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-010","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"乡人飮酒，杖者出，斯出矣。乡人傩，朝服而立于阼阶。","pinyin":"xiāng rén yǐn jiǔ zhàng zhě chū sī chū yǐ xiāng rén nuó cháo fú ér lì yú zuò jiē","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When the villagers were drinking together, on those who carried staffs going out, he went out immediately after. 2. When the villagers were going through their ceremonies to drive away pestilential influences, he put on his court robes and stood on the eastern steps.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-011","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"问人于他邦，再拜而送之。康子馈药，拜而受之，曰，丘未达，不敢尝。","pinyin":"wèn rén yú tā bāng zài bài ér sòng zhī kāng zǐ kuì yào bài ér shòu zhī yuē mǒu wèi dá bù gǎn cháng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When he was sending complimentary inquiries to any one in another State, he bowed twice as he escorted the messenger away. 2. Chi K'ang having sent him a present of physic, he bowed and received it, saying, \"I do not know it. I dare not taste it.\"","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-012","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"厩焚，子退朝，曰，伤人乎，不问马。","pinyin":"jiù fén zǐ tuì cháo yuē shāng rén hū bú wèn mǎ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The stable being burned down, when he was at court, on his return he said, \"Has any man been hurt?\" He did not ask about the horses.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-013","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"君赐食，必正席，先尝之，君赐腥，必熟而荐之，君赐生，必畜之。侍食于君，君祭，先饭。疾，君视之，东首，加朝服拖绅。君命召，不俟驾行矣。","pinyin":"jūn cì shí bì zhèng xí xiān cháng zhī jūn cì xīng bì shóu ér jiàn zhī jūn cì shēng bì xù zhī shì shí yú jūn jūn jì xiān fàn jí jūn shì zhī dōng shǒu jiā cháo fú tuō shēn jūn mìng zhào bú sì jià xíng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When the prince sent him a gift of cooked meat, he would adjust his mat, first taste it, and then give it away to others. When the prince sent him a gift of undressed meat, he would have it cooked, and offer it to the spirits of his ancestors. When the prince sent him a gift of a living animal, he would keep it alive. 2. When he was in attendance on the prince and joining in the entertainment, the prince only sacrificed. He first tasted everything. 3. When he was ill and the prince came to visit him, he had his head to the east, made his court robes be spread over him, and drew his girdle across them. 4. When the prince's order called him, without waiting for his carriage to be yoked, he went at once.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-014","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"入大庙每事问。","pinyin":"rù tài miào měi shì wèn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When he entered the ancestral temple of the State, he asked about everything.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-015","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"朋友死，无所归，曰，于我殡。朋友之馈，虽车马，非祭肉不拜。","pinyin":"péng yǒu sǐ wú suǒ guī yuē yú wǒ bìn péng yǒu zhī kuì suī jū mǎ fēi jì ròu bú bài","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When any of his friends died, if he had no relations who could be depended on for the necessary offices, he would say, \"I will bury him.\" 2. When a friend sent him a present, though it might be a carriage and horses, he did not bow. 3. The only present for which he bowed was that of the flesh of sacrifice.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-016","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"寝不尸，居不容。见齐衰者，虽狎必变，见冕者，与瞽者，虽亵必以貌。凶服者式之，式负版者。有盛馔，必变色而作。迅雷，风烈，必变。","pinyin":"qǐn bù shī jū bú kè jiàn zī cuī zhě suī xiá bì biàn jiàn mián zhě yǔ gǔ zhě suī xiè bì yǐ mào xiōng fú zhě shì zhī shì fù bǎn zhě yǒu shèng zhuàn bì biàn sè ér zuò xùn léi fēng liè bì biàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. In bed, he did not lie like a corpse. At home, he did not put on any formal deportment. 2. When he saw any one in a mourning dress, though it might be an acquaintance, he would change countenance; when he saw any one wearing the cap of full dress, or a blind person, though he might be in his undress, he would salute them in a ceremonious manner. 3. To any person in mourning he bowed forward to the crossbar of his carriage; he bowed in the same way to any one bearing the tables of population. 4. When he was at an entertainment where there was an abundance of provisions set before him, he would change countenance and rise up. 5. On a sudden clap of thunder, or a violent wind, he would change countenance.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-017","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"升车，必正立，执绥。车中，不内顾，不疾言，不亲指。","pinyin":"shēng jū bì zhèng lì zhí suī jū zhōng bú nèi gù bù jí yán bù qīn zhǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When he was about to mount his carriage, he would stand straight, holding the cord. 2. When he was in the carriage, he did not turn his head quite round, he did not talk hastily, he did not point with his hands.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xiang-dang-018","bookSlug":"xiang-dang","bookNumber":10,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"色斯擧矣，翔而后集。曰，山梁雌雉，时哉时哉。子路共之，三嗅而作。","pinyin":"sè sī jǔ yǐ xiáng ér hòu jí yuē shān liáng cí zhì shí zāi shí zāi zǐ lù gǒng zhī sān xiù ér zuò","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Seeing the countenance, it instantly rises. It flies round, and by and by settles. 2. The Master said, \"There is the hen-pheasant on the hill bridge. At its season! At its season!\" Tsze-lu made a motion to it. Thrice it smelt him and then rose.","themes":["analects","xiang-dang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"xian-jin","number":11,"zhTitle":"先进","enTitle":"Hsien Tsin","chapterCount":25,"sentences":[{"id":"xian-jin-001","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子曰，先进于礼乐，野人也，后进于礼乐，君子也，如用之，则吾从先进。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xiān jìn yú lǐ yuè yě rén yě hòu jìn yú lǐ yuè jūn zǐ yě rú yòng zhī zé wú cóng xiān jìn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"The men of former times, in the matters of ceremonies and music were rustics, it is said, while the men of these latter times, in ceremonies and music, are accomplished gentlemen. 2. \"If I have occasion to use those things, I follow the men of former times.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-002","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，从我于陈蔡者，皆不及门也。德行，颜渊，闵子骞，冉伯牛，仲弓。言语，宰我，子贡。政事，冉有，季路。文学，子游，子夏。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zòng wǒ yú chén cài zhě jiē bù jí mén yě dé xìng yán yuān mǐn zǐ qiān rǎn bó niú zhòng gōng yán yǔ zǎi wǒ zǐ gòng zhèng shì rǎn yǒu jì lù wén xué zǐ yóu zǐ xià","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Of those who were with me in Ch'an and Ts'ai, there are none to be found to enter my door.\" 2. Distinguished for their virtuous principles and practice, there were Yen Yuan, Min Tsze-ch'ien, Zan Po-niu, and Chung-kung; for their ability in speech, Tsai Wo and Tsze-kung; for their administrative talents, Zan Yu and Chi Lu; for their literary acquirements, Tsze-yu and Tsze-hsia.","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-003","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，回也，非助我者也，于吾言，无所不说。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē huí yě fēi zhù wǒ zhě yě yú wú yán wú suǒ bú yuè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Hui gives me no assistance. There is nothing that I say in which he does not delight.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-004","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子曰，孝哉闵子骞，人不间于其父母昆弟之言。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xiào zāi mǐn zǐ qiān rén bú jiàn yú qí fù mǔ kūn dì zhī yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Filial indeed is Min Tsze-ch'ien! Other people say nothing of him different from the report of his parents and brothers.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-005","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"南容三复白圭，孔子以其兄之子妻之。","pinyin":"nán róng sān fù bái guī kǒng zǐ yǐ qí xiōng zhī zǐ qì zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Nan Yung was frequently repeating the lines about a white scepter stone. Confucius gave him the daughter of his elder brother to wife.","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-006","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"季康子问弟子孰为好学。孔子对曰，有颜回者好学，不幸短命死矣，今也则亡。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ wèn dì zǐ shú wéi hào xué kǒng zǐ duì yuē yǒu yán huí zhě hào xué bú xìng duǎn mìng sǐ yǐ jīn yě zé wú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang asked which of the disciples loved to learn. Confucius replied to him, \"There was Yen Hui; he loved to learn. Unfortunately his appointed time was short, and he died. Now there is no one who loves to learn, as he did.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-007","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"颜渊死，颜路请子之车，以为之椁。子曰，才不才，亦各言其子也，鲤也死，有棺而无椁，吾不徒行以为之椁，以吾从大夫之后，不可徒行也。","pinyin":"yán yuān sǐ yán lù qǐng zǐ zhī jū yǐ wéi zhī guǒ zǐ yuē cái bù cái yì gè yán qí zǐ yě lǐ yě sǐ yǒu guān ér wú guǒ wú bù tú xíng yǐ wéi zhī guǒ yǐ wú cóng dà fū zhī hòu bù kě tú xíng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When Yen Yuan died, Yen Lu begged the carriage of the Master to sell and get an outer shell for his son's coffin. 2. The Master said, \"Every one calls his son his son, whether he has talents or has not talents. There was Li; when he died, he had a coffin but no outer shell. I would not walk on foot to get a shell for him, because, having followed in the rear of the great officers, it was not proper that I should walk on foot.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-008","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"颜渊死，子曰，噫，天丧予，天丧予。","pinyin":"yán yuān sǐ zǐ yuē yī tiān sàng yú tiān sàng yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"When Yen Yuan died, the Master said, \"Alas! Heaven is destroying me! Heaven is destroying me!\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-009","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"颜渊死，子哭之恸，从者曰，子恸矣。曰，有恸乎。非夫人之为恸而谁为。","pinyin":"yán yuān sǐ zǐ kū zhī tòng zòng zhě yuē zǐ tòng yǐ yuē yǒu tòng hū fēi fú rén zhī wèi tòng ér shuí wèi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When Yen Yuan died, the Master bewailed him exceedingly, and the disciples who were with him said, \"Master, your grief is excessive?\" 2. \"Is it excessive?\" said he. 3. \"If I am not to mourn bitterly for this man, for whom should I mourn?\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-010","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"颜渊死，门人欲厚葬之。子曰，不可。门人厚葬之。子曰，回也，视予犹父也，予不得视犹子也，非我也，夫二三子也。","pinyin":"yán yuān sǐ mén rén yù hòu zàng zhī zǐ yuē bù kě mén rén hòu zàng zhī zǐ yuē huí yě shì yú yóu fù yě yú bù dé shì yóu zǐ yě fēi wǒ yě fú èr sān zǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When Yen Yuan died, the disciples wished to give him a great funeral, and the Master said, \"You may not do so.\" 2. The disciples did bury him in great style. 3. The Master said, \"Hui behaved towards me as his father. I have not been able to treat him as my son. The fault is not mine; it belongs to you, O disciples.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-011","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"季路问事鬼神。子曰，未能事人，焉能事鬼。敢问死。曰，未知生，焉知死。","pinyin":"jì lù wèn shì guǐ shén zǐ yuē wèi néng shì rén yān néng shì guǐ gǎn wèn sǐ yuē wèi zhī shēng yān zhī sǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi Lu asked about serving the spirits of the dead. The Master said, \"While you are not able to serve men, how can you serve their spirits?\" Chi Lu added, \"I venture to ask about death?\" He was answered, \"While you do not know life, how can you know about death?\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-012","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"闵子侍侧，訚訚如也，子路行行如也，冉有，子贡，侃侃如也。子乐。若由也，不得其死然。","pinyin":"mǐn zǐ shì cè yín yín rú yě zǐ lù hàng hàng rú yě rǎn yǒu zǐ gòng kǎn kǎn rú yě zǐ lè ruò yóu yě bù dé qí sǐ rán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The disciple Min was standing by his side, looking bland and precise; Tsze-lu, looking bold and soldierly; Zan Yu and Tsze-kung, with a free and straightforward manner. The Master was pleased. 2. He said, \"Yu, there!—he will not die a natural death.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-013","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"鲁人为长府。闵子骞曰，仍旧贯，如之何，何必改作。子曰，夫人不言，言必有中。","pinyin":"lǔ rén wéi cháng fǔ mǐn zǐ qiān yuē réng jiù guàn rú zhī hé hé bì gǎi zuò zǐ yuē fú rén bù yán yán bì yǒu zhòng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Some parties in Lu were going to take down and rebuild the Long Treasury. 2. Min Tsze-ch'ien said, \"Suppose it were to be repaired after its old style;—why must it be altered and made anew?\" 3. The Master said, \"This man seldom speaks; when he does, he is sure to hit the point.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-014","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，由之瑟，奚为于丘之门。门人不敬子路。子曰，由也，升堂矣，未入于室也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yóu zhī sè xī wéi yú mǒu zhī mén mén rén bú jìng zǐ lù zǐ yuē yóu yě shēng táng yǐ wèi rù yú shì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"What has the lute of Yu to do in my door?\" 2. The other disciples began not to respect Tsze-lu. The Master said, \"Yu has ascended to the hall, though he has not yet passed into the inner apartments.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-015","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子贡问师与商也孰贤。子曰，师也过，商也不及。曰，然则师愈与。子曰，过犹不及。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn shī yǔ shāng yě shú xián zǐ yuē shī yě guò shāng yě bù jí yuē rán zé shī yù yú zǐ yuē guò yóu bù jí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung asked which of the two, Shih or Shang, was the superior. The Master said, \"Shih goes beyond the due mean, and Shang does not come up to it.\" 2. \"Then,\" said Tsze-kung, \"the superiority is with Shih, I suppose.\" 3. The Master said, \"To go beyond is as wrong as to fall short.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-016","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"季氏富于周公，而求也为之聚敛而附益之。子曰，非吾徒也，小子，鸣鼓而攻之可也。","pinyin":"jì shì fù yú zhōu gōng ér qiú yě wèi zhī jù liǎn ér fù yì zhī zǐ yuē fēi wú tú yě xiǎo zǐ míng gǔ ér gōng zhī kě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The head of the Chi family was richer than the duke of Chau had been, and yet Ch'iu collected his imposts for him, and increased his wealth. 2. The Master said, \"He is no disciple of mine. My little children, beat the drum and assail him.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-017","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"柴也愚。参也鲁。师也辟。由也喭。","pinyin":"chái yě yú shēn yě lǔ shī yě pì yóu yě yàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Ch'ai is simple. 2. Shan is dull. 3. Shih is specious. 4. Yu is coarse.","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-018","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，回也其庶乎屡空。赐不受命，而货殖焉，亿则屡中。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē huí yě qí shù hū lǚ kōng cì bú shòu mìng ér huò zhí yān yì zé lǚ zhòng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"There is Hui! He has nearly attained to perfect virtue. He is often in want. 2. \"Ts'ze does not acquiesce in the appointments of Heaven, and his goods are increased by him. Yet his judgments are often correct.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-019","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子张问善人之道。子曰，不践迹，亦不入于室。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn shàn rén zhī dào zǐ yuē bú jiàn jī yì bú rù yú shì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang asked what were the characteristics of the good man. The Master said, \"He does not tread in the footsteps of others, but moreover, he does not enter the chamber of the sage.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-020","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子曰，论笃是与，君子者乎，色庄者乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē lùn dǔ shì yǔ jūn zǐ zhě hū sèz huān gzhě hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If, because a man's discourse appears solid and sincere, we allow him to be a good man, is he really a superior man? or is his gravity only in appearance?\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-021","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子路问闻斯行诸。子曰，有父兄在，如之何其闻斯行之。冉有问闻斯行诸。子曰，闻斯行之。公西华曰，由也问闻斯行诸，子曰，有父兄在，求也问闻斯行诸，子曰，闻斯行之，赤也惑，敢问。子曰，求也退，故进之，由也兼人，故退之。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn wén sī xíng zhū zǐ yuē yǒu fù xiōng zài rú zhī hé qí wén sī xíng zhī rǎn yǒu wèn wén sī xíng zhū zǐ yuē wén sī xíng zhī gōng xī huá yuē yóu yě wèn wén sī xíng zhū zǐ yuē yǒu fù xiōng zài qiú yě wèn wén sī xíng zhū zǐ yuē wén sī xíng zhī chì yě huò gǎn wèn zǐ yuē qiú yě tuì gù jìn zhī yóu yě jiān rén gù tuì zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu asked whether he should immediately carry into practice what he heard. The Master said, \"There are your father and elder brothers to be consulted;—why should you act on that principle of immediately carrying into practice what you hear?\" Zan Yu asked the same, whether he should immediately carry into practice what he heard, and the Master answered, \"Immediately carry into practice what you hear.\" Kung-hsi Hwa said, \"Yu asked whether he should carry immediately into practice what he heard, and you said, 'There are your father and elder brothers to be consulted.' Ch'iu asked whether he should immediately carry into practice what he heard, and you said, 'Carry it immediately into practice.' I, Ch'ih, am perplexed, and venture to ask you for an explanation.\" The Master said, \"Ch'iu is retiring and slow; therefore, I urged him forward. Yu has more than his own share of energy; therefore I kept him back.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-022","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子畏于匡，颜渊后，子曰，吾以女为死矣。曰，子在，回何敢死。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi yú kuāng yán yuān hòu zǐ yuē wú yǐ rǔ wéi sǐ yǐ yuē zǐ zài huí hé gǎn sǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master was put in fear in K'wang and Yen Yuan fell behind. The Master, on his rejoining him, said, \"I thought you had died.\" Hui replied, \"While you were alive, how should I presume to die?\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-023","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"季子然问仲由冉求，可谓大臣与。子曰，吾以子为异之问，曾由与求之问。所谓大臣者，以道事君，不可则止。今由与求也，可谓具臣矣。曰，然则从之者与。子曰，弑父与君，亦不从也。","pinyin":"jì zǐ rán wèn zhòng yóu rǎn qiú kě wèi dà chén yú zǐ yuē wú yǐ zǐ wéi yì zhī wèn zēng yóu yǔ qiú zhī wèn suǒ wèi dà chén zhě yǐ dào shì jūn bù kě zé zhǐ jīn yóu yǔ qiú yě kě wèi jù chén yǐ yuē rán zé cóng zhī zhě yú zǐ yuē shì fù yǔ jūn yì bù cóng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Chi Tsze-zan asked whether Chung Yu and Zan Ch'iu could be called great ministers. 2. The Master said, \"I thought you would ask about some extraordinary individuals, and you only ask about Yu and Ch'iu! 3. \"What is called a great minister, is one who serves his prince according to what is right, and when he finds he cannot do so, retires. 4. \"Now, as to Yu and Ch'iu, they may be called ordinary ministers.\" 5. Tsze-zan said, \"Then they will always follow their chief;—will they?\" 6. The Master said, \"In an act of parricide or regicide, they would not follow him.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-024","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子路使子羔为费宰。子曰，贼夫人之子。子路曰，有民人焉，有社稷焉，何必读书，然后为学。子曰，是故恶夫佞者。","pinyin":"zǐ lù shǐ zǐ gāo wéi bì zǎi zǐ yuē zéi fú rén zhī zǐ zǐ lù yuē yǒu mín rén yān yǒu shè jì yān hé bì dú shū rán hòu wéi xué zǐ yuē shì gù wù fú nìng zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu got Tsze-kao appointed governor of Pi. 2. The Master said, \"You are injuring a man's son.\" 3. Tsze-lu said, \"There are (there) common people and officers; there are the altars of the spirits of the land and grain. Why must one read books before he can be considered to have learned?\" 4. The Master said, \"It is on this account that I hate your glib-tongued people.\"","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-jin-025","bookSlug":"xian-jin","bookNumber":11,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子路，曾皙，冉有，公西华，侍坐。子曰，以吾一日长乎尔，毋吾以也。居则曰，不吾知也，如或知尔，则何以哉。子路率尔而对曰，千乘之国，摄乎大国之闲，加之以师旅，因之以饥馑，由也为之，比及三年，可使有勇，且知方也。夫子哂之。","pinyin":"zǐ lù zēng xī rǎn yǒu gōng xī huá shì zuò zǐ yuē yǐ wú yí rì zhǎng hū ěr wú wú yǐ yě jū zé yuē bù wú zhī yě rú huò zhī ěr zé hé yǐ zāi zǐ lù cù ěr ér duì yuē qiān shèng zhī guó shè hū dà guó zhī jiān jiā zhī yǐ shī lǚ yīn zhī yǐ jī jǐn yóu yě wéi zhī bì jí sān nián kě shǐ yǒu yǒng qiě zhī fāng yě fū zǐ shěn zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu, Tsang Hsi, Zan Yu, and Kung-hsi Hwa were sitting by the Master. 2. He said to them, \"Though I am a day or so older than you, do not think of that. 3. \"From day to day you are saying, 'We are not known.' If some ruler were to know you, what would you like to do?\" 4. Tsze-lu hastily and lightly replied, \"Suppose the case of a State of ten thousand chariots; let it be straitened between other large States; let it be suffering from invading armies; and to this let there be added a famine in corn and in all vegetables:—if I were intrusted with the government of it, in three years\" time I could make the people to be bold, and to recognise the rules of righteous conduct.\" The Master smiled at him. 5. Turning to Yen Yu, he said, \"Ch'iu, what are your wishes?\" Ch'iu replied, \"Suppose a state of sixty or seventy li square, or one of fifty or sixty, and let me have the government of it;—in three years\" time, I could make plenty to abound among the people. As to teaching them the principles of propriety, and music, I must wait for the rise of a superior man to do that.\" 6. \"What are your wishes, Ch'ih,\" said the Master next to Kung-hsi Hwa. Ch'ih replied, \"I do not say that my ability extends to these things, but I should wish to learn them. At the services of the ancestral temple, and at the audiences of the princes with the sovereign, I should like, dressed in the dark square-made robe and the black linen cap, to act as a small assistant.\" 7. Last of all, the Master asked Tsang Hsi, \"Tien, what are your wishes?\" Tien, pausing as he was playing on his lute, while it was yet twanging, laid the instrument aside, and rose. \"My wishes,\" he said, \"are different from the cherished purposes of these three gentlemen.\" \"What harm is there in that?\" said the Master; \"do you also, as well as they, speak out your wishes.\" Tien then said, \"In this, the last month of spring, with the dress of the season all complete, along with five or six young men who have assumed the cap, and six or seven boys, I would wash in the I, enjoy the breeze among the rain altars, and return home singing.\" The Master heaved a sigh and said, \"I give my approval to Tien.\" 8. The three others having gone out, Tsang Hsi remained behind, and said, \"What do you think of the words of these three friends?\" The Master replied, \"They simply told each one his wishes.\" 9. Hsi pursued, \"Master, why did you smile at Yu?\" 10. He was answered, \"The management of a State demands the rules of propriety. His words were not humble; therefore I smiled at him.\" 11. Hsi again said, \"But was it not a State which Ch'iu proposed for himself?\" The reply was, \"Yes; did you ever see a territory of sixty or seventy li or one of fifty or sixty, which was not a State?\" 12. Once more, Hsi inquired, \"And was it not a State which Ch'ih proposed for himself?\" The Master again replied, \"Yes; who but princes have to do with ancestral temples, and with audiences but the sovereign? If Ch'ih were to be a small assistant in these services, who could be a great one?","themes":["analects","xian-jin"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"yan-yuan","number":12,"zhTitle":"颜渊","enTitle":"Yen Yuan","chapterCount":24,"sentences":[{"id":"yan-yuan-001","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"颜渊问仁。子曰，克己复礼为仁，一日克己复礼，天下归仁焉，为仁由己，而由人乎哉。颜渊曰，请问其目。子曰，非礼勿视，非礼勿听，非礼勿言，非礼勿动。颜渊曰，回虽不敏，请事斯语矣。","pinyin":"yán yuān wèn rén zǐ yuē kè jǐ fù lǐ wéi rén yí rì kè jǐ fù lǐ tiān xià guī rén yān wéi rén yóu jǐ ér yóu rén hū zāi yán yuān yuē qǐng wèn qí mù zǐ yuē fēi lǐ wù shì fēi lǐ wù tīng fēi lǐ wù yán fēi lǐ wù dòng yán yuān yuē huí suī bù mǐn qǐng shì sī yǔ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yen Yuan asked about perfect virtue. The Master said, \"To subdue one's self and return to propriety, is perfect virtue. If a man can for one day subdue himself and return to propriety, all under heaven will ascribe perfect virtue to him. Is the practice of perfect virtue from a man himself, or is it from others?\" 2. Yen Yuan said, \"I beg to ask the steps of that process.\" The Master replied, \"Look not at what is contrary to propriety; listen not to what is contrary to propriety; speak not what is contrary to propriety; make no movement which is contrary to propriety.\" Yen Yuan then said, \"Though I am deficient in intelligence and vigour, I will make it my business to practise this lesson.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-002","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"仲弓问仁。子曰，出门如见大宾，使民如承大祭，己所不欲，勿施于人，在邦无怨，在家无怨。仲弓曰，雍虽不敏，请事斯语矣。","pinyin":"zhòng gōng wèn rén zǐ yuē chū mén rú jiàn dà bīn shǐ mín rú chéng dà jì jǐ suǒ bú yù wù shī yú rén zài bāng wú yuàn zài jiā wú yuàn zhòng gōng yuē yōng suī bù mǐn qǐng shì sī yǔ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chung-kung asked about perfect virtue. The Master said, \"It is, when you go abroad, to behave to every one as if you were receiving a great guest; to employ the people as if you were assisting at a great sacrifice; not to do to others as you would not wish done to yourself; to have no murmuring against you in the country, and none in the family.\" Chung-kung said, \"Though I am deficient in intelligence and vigour, I will make it my business to practise this lesson.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-003","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"司马牛问仁。子曰，仁者其言也讱。曰，其言也讱，斯谓之仁矣乎。子曰，为之难，言之得无讱乎。","pinyin":"sī mǎ niú wèn rén zǐ yuē rén zhě qí yán yě rèn yuē qí yán yě rèn sī wèi zhī rén yǐ hū zǐ yuē wéi zhī nán yán zhī dé wú rèn hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Sze-ma Niu asked about perfect virtue. 2. The Master said, \"The man of perfect virtue is cautious and slow in his speech.\" 3. \"Cautious and slow in his speech!\" said Niu;—\"is this what is meant by perfect virtue?\" The Master said, \"When a man feels the difficulty of doing, can he be other than cautious and slow in speaking?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-004","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"司马牛问君子。子曰，君子不忧不惧。曰，不忧不惧，斯谓之君子矣乎。子曰，内省不疚，夫何忧何惧。","pinyin":"sī mǎ niú wèn jūn zǐ zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bù yōu bú jù yuē bù yōu bú jù sī wèi zhī jūn zǐ yǐ hū zǐ yuē nèi xǐng bú jiù fú hé yōu hé jù","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Sze-ma Niu asked about the superior man. The Master said, \"The superior man has neither anxiety nor fear.\" 2. \"Being without anxiety or fear!\" said Nui;—\"does this constitute what we call the superior man?\" 3. The Master said, \"When internal examination discovers nothing wrong, what is there to be anxious about, what is there to fear?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-005","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"司马牛忧曰，人皆有兄弟，我独亡。子夏曰，商闻之矣。死生有命，富贵在天。君子敬而无失，与人恭而有礼，四海之内，皆兄弟也，君子何患乎无兄弟也。","pinyin":"sī mǎ niú yōu yuē rén jiē yǒu xiōng dì wǒ dú wú zǐ xià yuē shāng wén zhī yǐ sǐ shēng yǒu mìng fù guì zài tiān jūn zǐ jìng ér wú shī yǔ rén gōng ér yǒu lǐ sì hǎi zhī nèi jiē xiōng dì yě jūn zǐ hé huàn hū wú xiōng dì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Sze-ma Niu, full of anxiety, said, \"Other men all have their brothers, I only have not.\" 2. Tsze-hsia said to him, \"There is the following saying which I have heard:— 3. \"'Death and life have their determined appointment; riches and honours depend upon Heaven.' 4. \"Let the superior man never fail reverentially to order his own conduct, and let him be respectful to others and observant of propriety:—then all within the four seas will be his brothers. What has the superior man to do with being distressed because he has no brothers?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-006","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子张问明。子曰，浸润之谮，肤受之愬，不行焉，可谓明也已矣，浸润之谮，肤受之愬，不行焉，可谓远也已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn míng zǐ yuē jìn rùn zhī zèn fū shòu zhī sù bù xíng yān kě wèi míng yě yǐ yǐ jìn rùn zhī zèn fū shòu zhī sù bù xíng yān kě wèi yuǎn yě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang asked what constituted intelligence. The Master said, \"He with whom neither slander that gradually soaks into the mind, nor statements that startle like a wound in the flesh, are successful, may be called intelligent indeed. Yea, he with whom neither soaking slander, nor startling statements, are successful, may be called farseeing.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-007","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子贡问政。子曰足食，足兵，民信之矣。子贡曰，必不得已而去，于斯三者何先。曰，去兵。子贡曰，必不得已而去，于斯二者何先。曰，去食，自古皆有死，民无信不立。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn zhèng zǐ yuē zú shí zú bīng mín xìn zhī yǐ zǐ gòng yuē bì bù dé yǐ ér qù yú sī sān zhě hé xiān yuē qù bīng zǐ gòng yuē bì bù dé yǐ ér qù yú sī èr zhě hé xiān yuē qù shí zì gǔ jiē yǒu sǐ mín wú xìn bú lì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung asked about government. The Master said, \"The requisites of government are that there be sufficiency of food, sufficiency of military equipment, and the confidence of the people in their ruler.\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"If it cannot be helped, and one of these must be dispensed with, which of the three should be foregone first?\" \"The military equipment,\" said the Master. 3. Tsze-kung again asked, \"If it cannot be helped, and one of the remaining two must be dispensed with, which of them should be foregone?\" The Master answered, \"Part with the food. From of old, death has been the lot of all men; but if the people have no faith in their rulers, there is no standing for the state.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-008","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"棘子成曰，君子质而已矣，何以文为。子贡曰，惜乎，夫子之说，君子也，驷不及舌。文犹质也，质犹文也，虎豹之鞹，犹犬羊之鞹。","pinyin":"jí zǐ chéng yuē jūn zǐ zhì ér yǐ yǐ hé yǐ wén wéi zǐ gòng yuē xī hū fū zǐ zhī shuō jūn zǐ yě sì bù jí shé wén yóu zhì yě zhì yóu wén yě hǔ bào zhī kuò yóu quǎn yáng zhī kuò","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Chi Tsze-ch'ang said, \"In a superior man it is only the substantial qualities which are wanted;—why should we seek for ornamental accomplishments?\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"Alas! Your words, sir, show you to be a superior man, but four horses cannot overtake the tongue. 3. Ornament is as substance; substance is as ornament. The hide of a tiger or a leopard stripped of its hair, is like the hide of a dog or a goat stripped of its hair.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-009","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"哀公问于有若曰年饥，用不足，如之何。有若对曰，盍彻乎。曰，二，吾犹不足，如之何其彻也。对曰，百姓足，君孰与不足，百姓不足，君孰与足。","pinyin":"āi gōng wèn yú yǒu ruò yuē nián jī yòng bù zú rú zhī hé yǒu ruò duì yuē hé chè hū yuē èr wú yóu bù zú rú zhī hé qí chè yě duì yuē bǎi xìng zú jūn shú yǔ bù zú bǎi xìng bù zú jūn shú yǔ zú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ai inquired of Yu Zo, saying, \"The year is one of scarcity, and the returns for expenditure are not sufficient;—what is to be done?\" 2. Yu Zo replied to him, \"Why not simply tithe the people?\" 3. \"With two tenths, said the duke, \"I find it not enough;—how could I do with that system of one tenth?\" 4. Yu Zo answered, \"If the people have plenty, their prince will not be left to want alone. If the people are in want, their prince cannot enjoy plenty alone.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-010","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子张问崇德，辨惑。子曰，主忠信，徒义，崇德也。爱之欲其生，恶之欲其死，既欲其生，又欲其死，是惑也。诚不以富亦祇以异。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn chóng dé biàn huò zǐ yuē zhǔ zhōng xìn xǐ yì chóng dé yě ài zhī yù qí shēng wù zhī yù qí sǐ jì yù qí shēng yòu yù qí sǐ shì huò yě chéng bù yǐ fù yì zhī yǐ yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang having asked how virtue was to be exalted, and delusions to be discovered, the Master said, \"Hold faithfulness and sincerity as first principles, and be moving continually to what is right;—this is the way to exalt one's virtue. 2. \"You love a man and wish him to live; you hate him and wish him to die. Having wished him to live, you also wish him to die. This is a case of delusion. 3. \"'It may not be on account of her being rich, yet you come to make a difference.'\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-011","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"齐景公问政于孔子。孔子对曰，君君，臣臣，父父，子子。公曰，善哉，信如君不君，臣不臣，父不父，子不子，虽有粟，吾得而食诸。","pinyin":"qí jǐng gōng wèn zhèng yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ duì yuē jūn jūn chén chén fù fù zǐ zǐ gōng yuē shàn zāi xìn rú jūn bù jūn chén bù chén fù bú fù zǐ bù zǐ suī yǒu sù wú dé ér shí zhū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ching, of Ch'i, asked Confucius about government. 2. Confucius replied, \"There is government, when the prince is prince, and the minister is minister; when the father is father, and the son is son.\" 3. \"Good!\" said the duke; \"if, indeed; the prince be not prince, the minister not minister, the father not father, and the son not son, although I have my revenue, can I enjoy it?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-012","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，片言可以折狱者，其由也与。子路无宿诺。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē piàn yán kě yǐ zhé yù zhě qí yóu yě yú zǐ lù wú sù nuò","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Ah! it is Yu, who could with half a word settle litigations!\" 2. Tsze-lu never slept over a promise.","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-013","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，听讼，吾犹人也，必也，使无讼乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē tīng sòng wú yóu rén yě bì yě shǐ wú sòng hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In hearing litigations, I am like any other body. What is necessary, however, is to cause the people to have no litigations.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-014","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子张问政。子曰，居之无倦，行之以忠。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn zhèng zǐ yuē jū zhī wú juàn xíng zhī yǐ zhōng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang asked about government. The Master said, \"The art of governing is to keep its affairs before the mind without weariness, and to practise them with undeviating consistency.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-015","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，博学于文，约之以礼，亦可以弗畔矣夫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bó xué yú wén yuē zhī yǐ lǐ yì kě yǐ fú pàn yǐ fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"By extensively studying all learning, and keeping himself under the restraint of the rules of propriety, one may thus likewise not err from what is right.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-016","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子成人之美，不成人之恶，小人反是。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ chéng rén zhī měi bù chéng rén zhī è xiǎo rén fǎn shì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man seeks to perfect the admirable qualities of men, and does not seek to perfect their bad qualities. The mean man does the opposite of this.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-017","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"季康子问政于孔子。孔子对曰，政者正也，子帅以正，孰敢不正。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ wèn zhèng yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ duì yuē zhèng zhě zhèng yě zǐ shuài yǐ zhèng shú gǎn bú zhèng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang asked Confucius about government. Confucius replied, \"To govern means to rectify. If you lead on the people with correctness, who will dare not to be correct?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-018","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"季康子患盗，问于孔子。孔子对曰，苟子之不欲，虽赏之不窃。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ huàn dào wèn yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ duì yuē gǒu zǐ zhī bú yù suī shǎng zhī bú qiè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang, distressed about the number of thieves in the state, inquired of Confucius how to do away with them. Confucius said, \"If you, sir, were not covetous, although you should reward them to do it, they would not steal.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-019","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"季康子问政于孔子，曰，如杀无道，以就有道，何如。孔子对曰，子为政，焉用杀，子欲善，而民善矣，君子之德风，小人之德草，草上之风必偃。","pinyin":"jì kāng zǐ wèn zhèng yú kǒng zǐ yuē rú shā wú dào yǐ jiù yǒu dào hé rú kǒng zǐ duì yuē zǐ wéi zhèng yān yòng shā zǐ yù shàn ér mín shàn yǐ jūn zǐ zhī dé fēng xiǎo rén zhī dé cǎo cǎo shàng zhī fēng bì yǎn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Chi K'ang asked Confucius about government, saying, \"What do you say to killing the unprincipled for the good of the principled?\" Confucius replied, \"Sir, in carrying on your government, why should you use killing at all? Let your evinced desires be for what is good, and the people will be good. The relation between superiors and inferiors, is like that between the wind and the grass. The grass must bend, when the wind blows across it.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-020","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子张问士何如，斯可谓之达矣。子曰，何哉，尔所谓达者。子张对曰，在邦必闻，在家必闻。子曰，是闻也，非达也。夫达也者，质直而好义，察言而观色，虑以下人，在邦必达，在家必达。夫闻也者，色取仁而行违，居之不疑，在邦必闻，在家必闻。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn shì hé rú sī kě wèi zhī dá yǐ zǐ yuē hé zāi ěr suǒ wèi dá zhě zǐ zhāng duì yuē zài bāng bì wén zài jiā bì wén zǐ yuē shì wén yě fēi dá yě fú dá yě zhě zhì zhí ér hào yì chá yán ér guān sè lǜ yǐ xià rén zài bāng bì dá zài jiā bì dá fú wén yě zhě sè qǔ rén ér xíng wéi jū zhī bù yí zài bāng bì wén zài jiā bì wén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang asked, \"What must the officer be, who may be said to be distinguished?\" 2. The Master said, \"What is it you call being distinguished?\" 3. Tsze-chang replied, \"It is to be heard of through the State, to be heard of throughout his clan.\" 4. The Master said, \"That is notoriety, not distinction. 5. \"Now the man of distinction is solid and straightforward, and loves righteousness. He examines people's words, and looks at their countenances. He is anxious to humble himself to others. Such a man will be distinguished in the country; he will be distinguished in his clan. 6. \"As to the man of notoriety, he assumes the appearance of virtue, but his actions are opposed to it, and he rests in this character without any doubts about himself. Such a man will be heard of in the country; he will be heard of in the clan.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-021","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"樊迟从游于舞雩之下。曰，敢问崇德，修慝，辨惑。子曰，善哉问。先事后得，非崇德与，攻其恶，无攻人之恶，非修慝与，一朝之忿，忘其身以及其亲，非惑与。","pinyin":"fán chí cóng yóu yú wǔ yú zhī xià yuē gǎn wèn chóng dé xiū tè biàn huò zǐ yuē shàn zāi wèn xiān shì hòu dé fēi chóng dé yú gōng qí è wú gōng rén zhī è fēi xiū tè yú yì zhāo zhī fèn wàng qí shēn yǐ jí qí qīn fēi huò yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Fan Ch'ih rambling with the Master under the trees about the rain altars, said, \"I venture to ask how to exalt virtue, to correct cherished evil, and to discover delusions.\" 2. The Master said, \"Truly a good question! 3. \"If doing what is to be done be made the first business, and success a secondary consideration;—is not this the way to exalt virtue? To assail one's own wickedness and not assail that of others;—is not this the way to correct cherished evil? For a morning's anger to disregard one's own life, and involve that of his parents;—is not this a case of delusion?\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-022","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"樊迟问仁。子曰，爱人。问知。子曰，知人。樊迟未达。子曰，擧直错诸枉，能使枉者直。樊迟退，见子夏曰，乡也，吾见于夫子而问知。子曰，擧直错诸枉，能使枉者直，何谓也。子夏曰，富哉言乎。舜有天下，选于众，擧皋陶，不仁者远矣，汤有天下，选于众，擧伊尹，不仁者远矣。","pinyin":"fán chí wèn rén zǐ yuē ài rén wèn zhì zǐ yuē zhī rén fán chí wèi dá zǐ yuē jǔ zhí cuò zhū wǎng néng shǐ wǎng zhě zhí fán chí tuì jiàn zǐ xià yuē xiàng yě wú xiàn yú fū zǐ ér wèn zhì zǐ yuē jǔ zhí cuò zhū wǎng néng shǐ wǎng zhě zhí hé wèi yě zǐ xià yuē fù zāi yán hū shùn yǒu tiān xià xuǎn yú zhòng jǔ gāo yáo bù rén zhě yuǎn yǐ tāng yǒu tiān xià xuǎn yú zhòng jǔ yī yǐn bù rén zhě yuǎn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Fan Ch'ih asked about benevolence. The Master said, \"It is to love all men.\" He asked about knowledge. The Master said, \"It is to know all men.\" 2. Fan Ch'ih did not immediately understand these answers. 3. The Master said, \"Employ the upright and put aside all the crooked;—in this way the crooked can be made to be upright.\" 4. Fan Ch'ih retired, and, seeing Tsze-hsia, he said to him, \"A Little while ago, I had an interview with our Master, and asked him about knowledge. He said, \"Employ the upright, and put aside all the crooked;—in this way, the crooked will be made to be upright.\" What did he mean?\" 5. Tsze-hsia said, \"Truly rich is his saying! 6. \"Shun, being in possession of the kingdom, selected from among all the people, and employed Kao-yao, on which all who were devoid of virtue disappeared. T'ang, being in possession of the kingdom, selected from among all the people, and employed I Yin, and all who were devoid of virtue disappeared.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-023","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子贡问友。子曰，忠吿而善道之，不可则止，毋自辱焉。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yǒu zǐ yuē zhōng gào ér shàn dào zhī bù kě zé zhǐ wú zì rǔ yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked about friendship. The Master said, \"Faithfully admonish your friend, and skillfully lead him on. If you find him impracticable, stop. Do not disgrace yourself.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yan-yuan-024","bookSlug":"yan-yuan","bookNumber":12,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，君子以文会友，以友辅仁。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yǐ wén huì yǒu yǐ yǒu fǔ rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"The superior man on grounds of culture meets with his friends, and by their friendship helps his virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yan-yuan"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"zi-lu","number":13,"zhTitle":"子路","enTitle":"Tsze-lu","chapterCount":30,"sentences":[{"id":"zi-lu-001","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子路问政。子曰，先之，劳之。请益。曰，无倦。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn zhèng zǐ yuē xiān zhī láo zhī qǐng yì yuē wú juàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu asked about government. The Master said, \"Go before the people with your example, and be laborious in their affairs.\" 2. He requested further instruction, and was answered, \"Be not weary (in these things).\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-002","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"仲弓为季氏宰，问政。子曰，先有司，赦小过，擧贤才。曰，焉知贤才而擧之。曰，擧尔所知，尔所不知，人其舍诸。","pinyin":"zhòng gōng wéi jì shì zǎi wèn zhèng zǐ yuē xiān yǒu sī shè xiǎo guò jǔ xián cái yuē yān zhī xián cái ér jǔ zhī yuē jǔ ěr suǒ zhī ěr suǒ bù zhī rén qí shě zhū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Chung-kung, being chief minister to the Head of the Chi family, asked about government. The Master said, \"Employ first the services of your various officers, pardon small faults, and raise to office men of virtue and talents.\" 2. Chung-kung said, \"How shall I know the men of virtue and talent, so that I may raise them to office?\" He was answered, \"Raise to office those whom you know. As to those whom you do not know, will others neglect them?\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-003","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子路曰，衞君待子而为政，子将奚先。子曰，必也，正名乎。子路曰，有是哉，子之迂也，奚其正。子曰，野哉，由也，君子于其所不知，盖阙如也。名不正，则言不顺，言不顺，则事不成。事不成，则礼乐不兴，礼乐不兴，则刑罚不中，刑罚不中，则民无所措手足。故君子名之必可言也，言之必可行也，君子于其言，无所茍而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ lù yuē wèi jūn dài zǐ ér wéi zhèng zǐ jiāng xī xiān zǐ yuē bì yě zhèng míng hū zǐ lù yuē yǒu shì zāi zǐ zhī yū yě xī qí zhèng zǐ yuē yě zāi yóu yě jūn zǐ yú qí suǒ bù zhī gài quē rú yě míng bú zhèng zé yán bú shùn yán bú shùn zé shì bù chéng shì bù chéng zé lǐ yuè bù xīng lǐ yuè bù xīng zé xíng fá bú zhòng xíng fá bú zhòng zé mín wú suǒ cuò shǒu zú gù jūn zǐ míng zhī bì kě yán yě yán zhī bì kě xíng yě jūn zǐ yú qí yán wú suǒ gǒu ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu said, \"The ruler of Wei has been waiting for you, in order with you to administer the government. What will you consider the first thing to be done?\" 2. The Master replied, \"What is necessary is to rectify names.\" 3. \"So, indeed!\" said Tsze-lu. \"You are wide of the mark! Why must there be such rectification?\" 4. The Master said, \"How uncultivated you are, Yu! A superior man, in regard to what he does not know, shows a cautious reserve. 5. \"If names be not correct, language is not in accordance with the truth of things. If language be not in accordance with the truth of things, affairs cannot be carried on to success. 6. \"When affairs cannot be carried on to success, proprieties and music will not flourish. When proprieties and music do not flourish, punishments will not be properly awarded. When punishments are not properly awarded, the people do not know how to move hand or foot. 7. \"Therefore a superior man considers it necessary that the names he uses may be spoken appropriately, and also that what he speaks may be carried out appropriately. What the superior man requires, is just that in his words there may be nothing incorrect.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-004","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"樊迟请学稼。子曰，吾不如老农。请学为圃。曰，吾不如老圃。樊迟出。子曰，小人哉，樊须也。上好礼，则民莫敢不敬，上好义，则民莫敢不服，上好信，则民莫敢不用情，夫如是，则四方之民，襁负其子而至矣，焉用稼。","pinyin":"fán chí qǐng xué jià zǐ yuē wú bù rú lǎo nóng qǐng xué wéi pǔ yuē wú bù rú lǎo pǔ fán chí chū zǐ yuē xiǎo rén zāi fán xū yě shàng hào lǐ zé mín mò gǎn bú jìng shàng hào yì zé mín mò gǎn bù fú shàng hào xìn zé mín mò gǎn bú yòng qíng fú rú shì zé sì fāng zhī mín qiǎng fù qí zǐ ér zhì yǐ yān yòng jià","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Fan Ch'ih requested to be taught husbandry. The Master said, \"I am not so good for that as an old husbandman.\" He requested also to be taught gardening, and was answered, \"I am not so good for that as an old gardener.\" 2. Fan Ch'ih having gone out, the Master said, \"A small man, indeed, is Fan Hsu! 3. If a superior love propriety, the people will not dare not to be reverent. If he love righteousness, the people will not dare not to submit to his example. If he love good faith, the people will not dare not to be sincere. Now, when these things obtain, the people from all quarters will come to him, bearing their children on their backs;—what need has he of a knowledge of husbandry?\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-005","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，诵诗三百，授之以政，不达，使于四方，不能专对，虽多，亦奚以为。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sòng shī sān bǎi shòu zhī yǐ zhèng bù dá shǐ yú sì fāng bù néng zhuān duì suī duō yì xī yǐ wéi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Though a man may be able to recite the three hundred odes, yet if, when intrusted with a governmental charge, he knows not how to act, or if, when sent to any quarter on a mission, he cannot give his replies unassisted, notwithstanding the extent of his learning, of what practical use is it?\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-006","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，其身正，不令而行，其身不正，虽令不从。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qí shēn zhèng bú lìng ér xíng qí shēn bú zhèng suī lìng bù cóng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When a prince's personal conduct is correct, his government is effective without the issuing of orders. If his personal conduct is not correct, he may issue orders, but they will not be followed.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-007","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，鲁衞之政，兄弟也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē lǔ wèi zhī zhèng xiōng dì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The governments of Lu and Wei are brothers.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-008","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子谓衞公子荆善居室，始有，曰，苟合矣，少有，曰，苟完矣，富有，曰，苟美矣。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi wèi gōng zǐ jīng shàn jū shì shǐ yǒu yuē gǒu hé yǐ shǎo yǒu yuē gǒu wán yǐ fù yǒu yuē gǒu měi yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said of Ching, a scion of the ducal family of Wei, that he knew the economy of a family well. When he began to have means, he said, \"Ha! here is a collection!\" When they were a little increased, he said, \"Ha! this is complete!\" When he had become rich, he said, \"Ha! this is admirable!\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-009","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子适衞，冉有仆。子曰，庶矣哉。冉有曰，既庶矣，又何加焉。曰，富之。曰，既富矣，又何加焉。曰，教之。","pinyin":"zǐ shì wèi rǎn yǒu pú zǐ yuē shù yǐ zāi rǎn yǒu yuē jì shù yǐ yòu hé jiā yān yuē fù zhī yuē jì fù yǐ yòu hé jiā yān yuē jiào zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. When the Master went to Wei, Zan Yu acted as driver of his carriage. 2. The Master observed, \"How numerous are the people!\" 3. Yu said, \"Since they are thus numerous, what more shall be done for them?\" \"Enrich them,\" was the reply. 4. \"And when they have been enriched, what more shall be done?\" The Master said, \"Teach them.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-010","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子曰，苟有用我者，朞月，而已可也，三年有成。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǒu yǒu yòng wǒ zhě jī yuè ér yǐ kě yě sān nián yǒu chéng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If there were (any of the princes) who would employ me, in the course of twelve months, I should have done something considerable. In three years, the government would be perfected.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-011","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，善人为邦百年，亦可以胜残去杀矣，诚哉是言也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shàn rén wéi bāng bǎi nián yì kě yǐ shèng cán qù shā yǐ chéng zāi shì yán yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"'If good men were to govern a country in succession for a hundred years, they would be able to transform the violently bad, and dispense with capital punishments.' True indeed is this saying!\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-012","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，如有王者，必世而后仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rú yǒu wáng zhě bì shì ér hòu rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a truly royal ruler were to arise, it would still require a generation, and then virtue would prevail.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-013","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，苟正其身矣，于从政乎何有，不能正其身，如正人何。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǒu zhèng qí shēn yǐ yú cóng zhèng hū hé yǒu bù néng zhèng qí shēn rú zhèng rén hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a minister make his own conduct correct, what difficulty will he have in assisting in government? If he cannot rectify himself, what has he to do with rectifying others?\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-014","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"冉子退朝，子曰，何晏也。对曰，有政。子曰，其事也，如有政，虽不吾以，吾其与闻之。","pinyin":"rǎn zǐ tuì cháo zǐ yuē hé yàn yě duì yuē yǒu zhèng zǐ yuē qí shì yě rú yǒu zhèng suī bù wú yǐ wú qí yù wén zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The disciple Zan returning from the court, the Master said to him, \"How are you so late?\" He replied, \"We had government business.\" The Master said, \"It must have been family affairs. If there had been government business, though I am not now in office, I should have been consulted about it.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-015","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"定公问一言而可以兴邦，有诸。孔子对曰，言不可以若是其几也。人之言曰，为君难，为臣不易。如知为君之难也，不几乎一言而兴邦乎。曰，一言而丧邦有诸。孔子对曰，言不可以若是其几也，人之言曰，予无乐乎为君，唯其言而莫予违也。如其善，而莫之违也，不亦善乎。如不善而莫之违也，不几乎一言而丧邦乎。","pinyin":"dìng gōng wèn yì yán ér kě yǐ xīng bāng yǒu zhū kǒng zǐ duì yuē yán bù kě yǐ ruò shì qí jī yě rén zhī yán yuē wéi jūn nán wéi chén bú yì rú zhī wéi jūn zhī nán yě bù jī hū yì yán ér xīng bāng hū yuē yì yán ér sàng bāng yǒu zhū kǒng zǐ duì yuē yán bù kě yǐ ruò shì qí jī yě rén zhī yán yuē yú wú lè hū wéi jūn wéi qí yán ér mò yú wéi yě rú qí shàn ér mò zhī wéi yě bú yì shàn hū rú bú shàn ér mò zhī wéi yě bù jǐ hū yì yán ér sàng bāng hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ting asked whether there was a single sentence which could make a country prosperous. Confucius replied, \"Such an effect cannot be expected from one sentence. 2. \"There is a saying, however, which people have—'To be a prince is difficult; to be a minister is not easy.' 3. \"If a ruler knows this,—the difficulty of being a prince,—may there not be expected from this one sentence the prosperity of his country?\" 4. The duke then said, \"Is there a single sentence which can ruin a country?\" Confucius replied, \"Such an effect as that cannot be expected from one sentence. There is, however, the saying which people have—'I have no pleasure in being a prince, but only in that no one can offer any opposition to what I say!' 5. \"If a ruler's words be good, is it not also good that no one oppose them? But if they are not good, and no one opposes them, may there not be expected from this one sentence the ruin of his country?\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-016","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"叶公问政。子曰，近者说，远者来。","pinyin":"shè gōng wèn zhèng zǐ yuē jìn zhě yuè yuǎn zhě lái","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke of Sheh asked about government. 2. The Master said, \"Good government obtains, when those who are near are made happy, and those who are far off are attracted.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-017","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子夏为莒父宰，问政。子曰，无欲速，无见小利。欲速则不达，见小利则大事不成。","pinyin":"zǐ xià wéi jǔ fǔ zǎi wèn zhèng zǐ yuē wú yù sù wú jiàn xiǎo lì yù sù zé bù dá jiàn xiǎo lì zé dà shì bù chéng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia, being governor of Chu-fu, asked about government. The Master said, \"Do not be desirous to have things done quickly; do not look at small advantages. Desire to have things done quickly prevents their being done thoroughly. Looking at small advantages prevents great affairs from being accomplished.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-018","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"叶公语孔子曰，吾党有直躬者，其父攘羊，而子证之。孔子曰，吾党之直者异于是，父为子隐，子为父隐，直在其中矣。","pinyin":"shè gōng yù kǒng zǐ yuē wú dǎng yǒu zhí gōng zhě qí fù ráng yáng ér zǐ zhèng zhī kǒng zǐ yuē wú dǎng zhī zhí zhě yì yú shì fù wèi zǐ yǐn zǐ wèi fù yǐn zhí zài qí zhōng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke of Sheh informed Confucius, saying, \"Among us here there are those who may be styled upright in their conduct. If their father have stolen a sheep, they will bear witness to the fact.\" 2. Confucius said, \"Among us, in our part of the country, those who are upright are different from this. The father conceals the misconduct of the son, and the son conceals the misconduct of the father. Uprightness is to be found in this.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-019","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"樊迟问仁。子曰，居处恭，执事敬，与人忠，虽之夷狄，不可弃也。","pinyin":"fán chí wèn rén zǐ yuē jū chǔ gōng zhí shì jìng yǔ rén zhōng suī zhī yí dí bù kě qì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Fan Ch'ih asked about perfect virtue. The Master said, \"It is, in retirement, to be sedately grave; in the management of business, to be reverently attentive; in intercourse with others, to be strictly sincere. Though a man go among rude, uncultivated tribes, these qualities may not be neglected.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-020","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子贡问曰，何如斯可谓之士矣。子曰，行己有耻，使于四方，不辱君命，可谓士矣。曰，敢问其次。曰，宗族称孝焉，乡党称弟焉。曰，敢问其次。曰，言必信，行必果，硁硁然，小人哉，抑亦可以为次矣。曰，今之从政者何如。子曰，噫，斗筲之人，何足算也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yuē hé rú sī kě wèi zhī shì yǐ zǐ yuē xíng jǐ yǒu chǐ shǐ yú sì fāng bù rǔ jūn mìng kě wèi shì yǐ yuē gǎn wèn qí cì yuē zōng zú chēng xiào yān xiāng dǎng chēng tì yān yuē gǎn wèn qí cì yuē yán bì xìn xíng bì guǒ kēng kēng rán xiǎo rén zāi yì yì kě yǐ wéi cì yǐ yuē jīn zhī cóng zhèng zhě hé rú zǐ yuē yī dǒu shāo zhī rén hé zú suàn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung asked, saying, \"What qualities must a man possess to entitle him to be called an officer? The Master said, \"He who in his conduct of himself maintains a sense of shame, and when sent to any quarter will not disgrace his prince's commission, deserves to be called an officer.\" 2. Tsze-kung pursued, \"I venture to ask who may be placed in the next lower rank?\" And he was told, \"He whom the circle of his relatives pronounce to be filial, whom his fellow-villagers and neighbours pronounce to be fraternal.\" 3. Again the disciple asked, \"I venture to ask about the class still next in order.\" The Master said, \"They are determined to be sincere in what they say, and to carry out what they do. They are obstinate little men. Yet perhaps they may make the next class.\" 4. Tsze-kung finally inquired, \"Of what sort are those of the present day, who engage in government?\" The Master said \"Pooh! they are so many pecks and hampers, not worth being taken into account.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-021","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不得中行而与之，必也狂狷乎，狂者进取，狷者有所不为也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bù dé zhōng xíng ér yǔ zhī bì yě kuáng juàn hū kuáng zhě jìn qǔ juàn zhě yǒu suǒ bù wéi yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Since I cannot get men pursuing the due medium, to whom I might communicate my instructions, I must find the ardent and the cautiously-decided. The ardent will advance and lay hold of truth; the cautiously-decided will keep themselves from what is wrong.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-022","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，南人有言曰，人而无恒，不可以作巫医，善夫。不恒其德，或承之羞。子曰，不占而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē nán rén yǒu yán yuē rén ér wú héng bù kě yǐ zuò wū yī shàn fú bù héng qí dé huò chéng zhī xiū zǐ yuē bù zhān ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"The people of the south have a saying—'A man without constancy cannot be either a wizard or a doctor.' Good! 2. \"Inconstant in his virtue, he will be visited with disgrace.\" 3. The Master said, \"This arises simply from not attending to the prognostication.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-023","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子和而不同，小人同而不和。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ hé ér bù tóng xiǎo rén tóng ér bù hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is affable, but not adulatory; the mean man is adulatory, but not affable.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-024","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子贡问曰，乡人皆好之，何如。子曰，未可也。乡人皆恶之，何如。子曰，未可也。不如乡人之善者好之，其不善者恶之。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yuē xiāng rén jiē hào zhī hé rú zǐ yuē wèi kě yě xiāng rén jiē wù zhī hé rú zǐ yuē wèi kě yě bù rú xiāng rén zhī shàn zhě hào zhī qí bú shàn zhě wù zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked, saying, \"What do you say of a man who is loved by all the people of his neighborhood?\" The Master replied, \"We may not for that accord our approval of him.\" \"And what do you say of him who is hated by all the people of his neighborhood?\" The Master said, \"We may not for that conclude that he is bad. It is better than either of these cases that the good in the neighborhood love him, and the bad hate him.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-025","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子易事而难说也，说之不以道，不说也，及其使人也，器之。小人难事而易说也，说之虽不以道，说也，及其使人也，求备焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yì shì ér nán yuè yě yuè zhī bù yǐ dào bú yuè yě jí qí shǐ rén yě qì zhī xiǎo rén nán shì ér yì yuè yě yuè zhī suī bù yǐ dào yuè yě jí qí shǐ rén yě qiú bèi yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is easy to serve and difficult to please. If you try to please him in any way which is not accordant with right, he will not be pleased. But in his employment of men, he uses them according to their capacity. The mean man is difficult to serve, and easy to please. If you try to please him, though it be in a way which is not accordant with right, he may be pleased. But in his employment of men, he wishes them to be equal to everything.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-026","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子泰而不骄，小人骄而不泰。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ tài ér bù jiāo xiǎo rén jiāo ér bú tài","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man has a dignified ease without pride. The mean man has pride without a dignified ease.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-027","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，刚，毅，木，讷，近仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gāng yì mù nè jìn rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The firm, the enduring, the simple, and the modest are near to virtue.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-028","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"子路问曰，何如斯可谓之士矣。子曰，切切，偲偲，怡怡如也，可谓士矣，朋友切切偲偲，兄弟怡怡。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn yuē hé rú sī kě wèi zhī shì yǐ zǐ yuē qiè qiè sī sī yí yí rú yě kě wèi shì yǐ péng yǒu qiè qiè sī sī xiōng dì yí yí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu asked, saying, \"What qualities must a man possess to entitle him to be called a scholar?\" The Master said, \"He must be thus,—earnest, urgent, and bland:—among his friends, earnest and urgent; among his brethren, bland.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-029","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":29,"classicalChinese":"子曰，善人教民七年，亦可以即戎矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shàn rén jiào mín qī nián yì kě yǐ jí róng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Let a good man teach the people seven years, and they may then likewise be employed in war.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-lu-030","bookSlug":"zi-lu","bookNumber":13,"sentenceNumber":30,"classicalChinese":"子曰，以不教民战，是谓弃之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǐ bú jiào mín zhàn shì wèi qì zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"To lead an uninstructed people to war, is to throw them away.\"","themes":["analects","zi-lu"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"xian-wen","number":14,"zhTitle":"宪问","enTitle":"Hsien Wan","chapterCount":47,"sentences":[{"id":"xian-wen-001","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"宪问耻。子曰，邦有道谷，邦无道谷，耻也。","pinyin":"xiàn wèn chǐ zǐ yuē bāng yǒu dào gǔ bāng wú dào gǔ chǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Hsien asked what was shameful. The Master said, \"When good government prevails in a state, to be thinking only of salary; and, when bad government prevails, to be thinking, in the same way, only of salary;—this is shameful.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-002","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"克，伐，怨，欲，不行焉，可以为仁矣。子曰，可以为难矣，仁则吾不知也。","pinyin":"kè fá yuàn yù bù xíng yān kě yǐ wéi rén yǐ zǐ yuē kě yǐ wéi nán yǐ rén zé wú bù zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. \"When the love of superiority, boasting, resentments, and covetousness are repressed, this may be deemed perfect virtue.\" 2. The Master said, \"This may be regarded as the achievement of what is difficult. But I do not know that it is to be deemed perfect virtue.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-003","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，士而怀居，不足以为士矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì ér huái jū bù zú yǐ wéi shì yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The scholar who cherishes the love of comfort is not fit to be deemed a scholar.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-004","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子曰，邦有道，危言危行，邦无道，危行言孙。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bāng yǒu dào wēi yán wēi xíng bāng wú dào wēi xíng yán xùn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When good government prevails in a state, language may be lofty and bold, and actions the same. When bad government prevails, the actions may be lofty and bold, but the language may be with some reserve.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-005","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子曰，有德者，必有言，有言者，不必有德，仁者，必有勇，勇者，不必有仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǒu dé zhě bì yǒu yán yǒu yán zhě bú bì yǒu dé rén zhě bì yǒu yǒng yǒng zhě bú bì yǒu rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The virtuous will be sure to speak correctly, but those whose speech is good may not always be virtuous. Men of principle are sure to be bold, but those who are bold may not always be men of principle.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-006","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"南宫适问于孔子曰，羿善射，奡荡舟，俱不得其死然，禹稷躬稼，而有天下。夫子不答。南宫适出。子曰，君子哉若人，尙德哉若人。","pinyin":"nán gōng kuò wèn yú kǒng zǐ yuē yì shàn shè ào dàng zhōu jù bù dé qí sǐ rán yǔ jì gōng jià ér yǒu tiān xià fū zǐ bù dá nán gōng kuò chū zǐ yuē jūn zǐ zāi ruò rén shàng dé zāi ruò rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Nan-kung Kwo, submitting an inquiry to Confucius, said, \"I was skillful at archery, and Ao could move a boat along upon the land, but neither of them died a natural death. Yu and Chi personally wrought at the toils of husbandry, and they became possessors of the kingdom.\" The Master made no reply; but when Nan-kung Kwo went out, he said, \"A superior man indeed is this! An esteemer of virtue indeed is this!\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-007","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子而不仁者有矣夫，未有小人而仁者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ ér bù rén zhě yǒu yǐ fú wèi yǒu xiǎo rén ér rén zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Superior men, and yet not always virtuous, there have been, alas! But there never has been a mean man, and, at the same time, virtuous.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-008","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，爱之，能勿劳乎，忠焉，能勿诲乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē ài zhī néng wù láo hū zhōng yān néng wù huì hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Can there be love which does not lead to strictness with its object? Can there be loyalty which does not lead to the instruction of its object?\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-009","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，为命，裨谌草创之，世叔讨论之，行人子羽修饰之，东里子产润色之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wéi mìng pí chén cǎoc huàn gzhī shì shū tǎo lùn zhī xíng rén zǐ yǔ xiū shì zhī dōng lí zǐ chǎn rùn sè zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In preparing the governmental notifications, P'i Shan first made the rough draught; Shi-shu examined and discussed its contents; Tsze-yu, the manager of Foreign intercourse, then polished the style; and, finally, Tsze-ch'an of Tung-li gave it the proper elegance and finish.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-010","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"或问子产，子曰，惠人也。问子西。曰，彼哉彼哉。问管仲。曰，人也，夺伯氏騈邑三百，饭疏食，没齿，无怨言。","pinyin":"huò wèn zǐ chǎn zǐ yuē huì rén yě wèn zǐ xī yuē bǐ zāi bǐ zāi wèn guǎn zhòng yuē rén yě duó bó shì pián yì sān bǎi fàn shū sì mò chǐ wú yuàn yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Some one asked about Tsze-ch'an. The Master said, \"He was a kind man.\" 2. He asked about Tsze-hsi. The Master said, \"That man! That man!\" 3. He asked about Kwan Chung. \"For him,\" said the Master, \"the city of Pien, with three hundred families, was taken from the chief of the Po family, who did not utter a murmuring word, though, to the end of his life, he had only coarse rice to eat.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-011","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，贫而无怨，难，富而无骄，易。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē pín ér wú yuàn nán fù ér wú jiāo yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"To be poor without murmuring is difficult. To be rich without being proud is easy.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-012","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，孟公绰，为赵魏老则优，不可以为滕薛大夫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mèng gōng chuò wéi zhào wèi lǎo zé yōu bù kě yǐ wéi téng xuē dà fū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Mang Kung-ch'o is more than fit to be chief officer in the families of Chao and Wei, but he is not fit to be great officer to either of the States Tang or Hsieh.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-013","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子路问成人。子曰，若臧武仲之知，公绰之不欲，卞庄子之勇，冉求之艺，文之以礼乐，亦可以为成人矣。曰，今之成人者，何必然。见利思义，见危授命，久要不忘平生之言，亦可以为成人矣。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn chéng rén zǐ yuē ruò zāng wǔ zhòng zhī zhì gōng chuò zhī bú yù biànz huān gzǐ zhī yǒng rǎn qiú zhī yì wén zhī yǐ lǐ yuè yì kě yǐ wéi chéng rén yǐ yuē jīn zhī chéng rén zhě hé bì rán jiàn lì sī yì jiàn wēi shòu mìng jiǔ yāo bú wàng píng shēng zhī yán yì kě yǐ wéi chéng rén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu asked what constituted a complete man. The Master said, \"Suppose a man with the knowledge of Tsang Wu-chung, the freedom from covetousness of Kung-ch'o, the bravery of Chwang of Pien, and the varied talents of Zan Ch'iu; add to these the accomplishments of the rules of propriety and music:—such a one might be reckoned a complete man.\" 2. He then added, \"But what is the necessity for a complete man of the present day to have all these things? The man, who in the","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-014","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子问公叔文子于公明贾曰，信乎，夫子不言，不笑，不取乎。公明贾对曰，以吿者过也。夫子时然后言，人不厌其言，乐然后笑，人不厌其笑，义然后取，人不厌其取。子曰，其然，岂其然乎。","pinyin":"zǐ wèn gōng shū wén zǐ yú gōng míng jiǎ yuē xìn hū fū zǐ bù yán bú xiào bù qǔ hū gōng míng jiǎ duì yuē yǐ gào zhě guò yě fū zǐ shí rán hòu yán rén bú yàn qí yán lè rán hòu xiào rén bú yàn qí xiào yì rán hòu qǔ rén bú yàn qí qǔ zǐ yuē qí rán qǐ qí rán hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master asked Kung-ming Chia about Kung-shu Wan, saying, \"Is it true that your master speaks not, laughs not, and takes not?\" 2. Kung-ming Chia replied, \"This has arisen from the reporters going beyond the truth.—My master speaks when it is the time to speak, and so men do not get tired of his speaking. He laughs when there is occasion to be joyful, and so men do not get tired of his laughing. He takes when it is consistent with righteousness to do so, and so men do not get tired of his taking.\" The Master said, \"So! But is it so with him?\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-015","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，臧武仲，以防求为后于鲁，虽曰不要君，吾不信也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zāng wǔ zhòng yǐ fáng qiú wéi hòu yú lǔ suī yuē bù yāo jūn wú bú xìn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Tsang Wu-chung, keeping possession of Fang, asked of the duke of Lu to appoint a successor to him in his family. Although it may be said that he was not using force with his sovereign, I believe he was.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-016","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，晋文公谲而不正，齐桓公正而不谲。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jìn wén gōng jué ér bú zhèng qí huán gōng zhèng ér bù jué","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The duke Wan of Tsin was crafty and not upright. The duke Hwan of Ch'i was upright and not crafty.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-017","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子路曰，桓公杀公子纠，召忽死之，管仲不死。曰，未仁乎。子曰，桓公九合诸侯，不以兵车，管仲之力也。如其仁。如其仁。","pinyin":"zǐ lù yuē huán gōng shā gōng zǐ jiū shào hū sǐ zhī guǎn zhòng bù sǐ yuē wèi rén hū zǐ yuē huán gōng jiǔ hé zhū hóu bù yǐ bīng jū guǎn zhòng zhī lì yě rú qí rén rú qí rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu said, \"The Duke Hwan caused his brother Chiu to be killed, when Shao Hu died with his master, but Kwan Chung did not die. May not I say that he was wanting in virtue?\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-018","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，管仲非仁者与。桓公杀公子纠，不能死，又相之。子曰，管仲相桓公，霸诸侯，一匡天下，民到于今受其赐，微管仲，吾其被发左衽矣。岂若匹夫匹妇之为谅也，自经于沟渎，而莫之知也。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē guǎn zhòng fēi rén zhě yú huán gōng shā gōng zǐ jiū bù néng sǐ yòu xiàng zhī zǐ yuē guǎn zhòng xiàng huán gōng bà zhū hóu yì kuāng tiān xià mín dào yú jīn shòu qí cì wēi guǎn zhòng wú qí pī fà zuǒ rèn yǐ qǐ ruò pǐ fū pǐ fù zhī wéi liàng yě zì jīng yú gōu dú ér mò zhī zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung said, \"Kwan Chung, I apprehend, was wanting in virtue. When the Duke Hwan caused his brother Chiu to be killed, Kwan Chung was not able to die with him. Moreover, he became prime minister to Hwan.\" 2. The Master said, \"Kwan Chung acted as prime minister to the Duke Hwan, made him leader of all the princes, and united and rectified the whole kingdom. Down to the present day, the people enjoy the gifts which he conferred. But for Kwan Chung, we should now be wearing our hair unbound, and the lappets of our coats buttoning on the left side. 3. \"Will you require from him the small fidelity of common","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-019","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"公叔文子之臣，大夫僎，与文子同升诸公。子闻之曰，可以为文矣。","pinyin":"gōng shū wén zǐ zhī chén dà fū zhuàn yǔ wén zǐ tóng shēng zhū gōng zǐ wén zhī yuē kě yǐ wéi wén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The great officer, Hsien, who had been family-minister to Kung-shu Wan, ascended to the prince's court in company with Wan. 2. The Master, having heard of it, said, \"He deserved to be considered wan (the accomplished).\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-020","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子言卫灵公之无道也。康子曰，夫如是，奚而不丧。孔子曰，仲叔圉治宾客，祝𬶍治宗庙，王孙贾治军旅。夫如是，奚其丧。","pinyin":"zǐ yán wèi líng gōng zhī wú dào yě kāng zǐ yuē fú rú shì xī ér bú sàng kǒng zǐ yuē zhòng shū yǔ zhì bīn kè zhù tuó zhì zōng miào wáng sūn jiǎ zhì jūn lǚ fú rú shì xī qí sàng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master was speaking about the unprincipled course of the duke Ling of Wei, when Ch'i K'ang said, \"Since he is of such a character, how is it he does not lose his State?\" 2. Confucius said, \"The Chung-shu Yu has the superintendence of his guests and of strangers; the litanist, T'o, has the management","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-021","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，其言之不怍，则为之也难。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qí yán zhī bú zuò zé wéi zhī yě nán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who speaks without modesty will find it difficult to make his words good.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-022","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"陈成子弑简公。孔子沐浴而朝，告于哀公曰，陈恒弑其君，请讨之。公曰，告夫三子。孔子曰，以吾从大夫之后，不敢不告也。君曰，告夫三子者。之三子告，不可。孔子曰，以吾从大夫之后，不敢不告也。","pinyin":"chén chéng zǐ shì jiǎn gōng kǒng zǐ mù yù ér cháo gào yú āi gōng yuē chén héng shì qí jūn qǐng tǎo zhī gōng yuē gào fú sān zǐ kǒng zǐ yuē yǐ wú cóng dà fū zhī hòu bù gǎn bú gào yě jūn yuē gào fú sān zǐ zhě zhī sān zǐ gào bù kě kǒng zǐ yuē yǐ wú cóng dà fū zhī hòu bù gǎn bú gào yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Chan Ch'ang murdered the Duke Chien of Ch'i. 2. Confucius bathed, went to court, and informed the duke Ai, saying, \"Chan Hang has slain his sovereign. I beg that you will undertake to punish him.\" 3. The duke said, \"Inform the chiefs of the three families of it.\" 4. Confucius retired, and said, \"Following in the rear of the great officers, I did not dare not to represent such a matter, and my prince says, 'Inform the chiefs of the three families of it.'\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-023","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子路问事君。子曰，勿欺也，而犯之。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn shì jūn zǐ yuē wù qī yě ér fàn zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu asked how a ruler should be served. The Master said, \"Do not impose on him, and, moreover, withstand him to his face.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-024","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子上达，小人下达。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ shàng dá xiǎo rén xià dá","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The progress of the superior man is upwards; the progress of the mean man is downwards.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-025","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，古之学者为己，今之学者为人。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǔ zhī xué zhě wèi jǐ jīn zhī xué zhě wèi rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In ancient times, men learned with a view to their own improvement. Now-a-days, men learn with a view to the approbation of others.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-026","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"蘧伯玉使人于孔子。孔子与之坐，而问焉，曰，夫子何为。","pinyin":"qú bó yù shǐ rén yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ yǔ zhī zuò ér wèn yān yuē fū zǐ hé wéi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Chu Po-yu sent a messenger with friendly inquiries to Confucius. 2. Confucius sat with him, and questioned him. \"What,\" said he, \"is your master engaged in?\" The messenger replied, \"My master is","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-027","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不在其位，不谋其政。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú zài qí wèi bù móu qí zhèng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who is not in any particular office, has nothing to do with plans for the administration of its duties.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-028","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，君子思不出其位。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē jūn zǐ sī bù chū qí wèi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"The superior man, in his thoughts, does not go out of his place.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-029","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":29,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子耻其言而过其行。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ chǐ qí yán ér guò qí xíng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is modest in his speech, but exceeds in his actions.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-030","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":30,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子道者三，我无能焉，仁者不忧，知者不惑，勇者不惧。子贡曰，夫子自道也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ dào zhě sān wǒ wú néng yān rén zhě bù yōu zhì zhě bú huò yǒng zhě bú jù zǐ gòng yuē fū zǐ zì dào yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"The way of the superior man is threefold, but I am not equal to it. Virtuous, he is free from anxieties; wise, he is free from perplexities; bold, he is free from fear. 2. Tsze-kung said, \"Master, that is what you yourself say.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-031","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":31,"classicalChinese":"子贡方人，子曰，赐也贤乎哉，夫我则不暇。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng fāng rén zǐ yuē cì yě xián hū zāi fú wǒ zé bù xiá","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung was in the habit of comparing men together. The Master said, \"Tsze must have reached a high pitch of excellence! Now, I have not leisure for this.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-032","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":32,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不患人之不己知，患其不能也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú huàn rén zhī bù jǐ zhī huàn qí bù néng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I will not be concerned at men's not knowing me; I will be concerned at my own want of ability.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-033","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":33,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不逆诈，不亿不信，抑亦先觉者，是贤乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bú nì zhà bú yì bú xìn yì yì xiān jué zhě shì xián hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who does not anticipate attempts to deceive him, nor think beforehand of his not being believed, and yet apprehends these things readily (when they occur);—is he not a man of superior worth?\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-034","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":34,"classicalChinese":"微生亩谓孔子曰，丘何为是栖栖者与，无乃为佞乎。孔子曰，非敢为佞也，疾固也。","pinyin":"wēi shēng mǔ wèi kǒng zǐ yuē mǒu hé wéi shì xī xī zhě yú wú nǎi wéi nìng hū kǒng zǐ yuē fēi gǎn wéi nìng yě jí gù yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Wei-shang Mau said to Confucius, \"Ch'iu, how is it that you keep roosting about? Is it not that you are an insinuating talker?\" 2. Confucius said, \"I do not dare to play the part of such a talker, but I hate obstinacy.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-035","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":35,"classicalChinese":"子曰，骥，不称其力，称其德也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jì bù chēng qí lì chēng qí dé yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A horse is called a ch'i, not because of its strength, but because of its other good qualities.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-036","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":36,"classicalChinese":"或曰，以德报怨，何如。子曰，何以报德。以直报怨，以德报德。","pinyin":"huò yuē yǐ dé bào yuàn hé rú zǐ yuē hé yǐ bào dé yǐ zhí bào yuàn yǐ dé bào dé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Some one said, \"What do you say concerning the principle that injury should be recompensed with kindness?\" 2. The Master said, \"With what then will you recompense kindness? 3. \"Recompense injury with justice, and recompense kindness with kindness.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-037","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":37,"classicalChinese":"子曰，莫我知也夫。子贡曰，何为其莫知子也。子曰，不怨天，不尤人，下学而上达，知我者，其天乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mò wǒ zhī yě fú zǐ gòng yuē hé wéi qí mò zhī zǐ yě zǐ yuē bú yuàn tiān bù yóu rén xià xué ér shàng dá zhī wǒ zhě qí tiān hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Alas! there is no one that knows me.\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"What do you mean by thus saying—that no one knows you?\" The Master replied, \"I do not murmur against","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-038","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":38,"classicalChinese":"公伯寮愬子路于季孙，子服景伯以吿，曰，夫子固有惑志于公伯寮，吾力犹能肆诸市朝。子曰，道之将行也与，命也，道之将废也与，命也，公伯寮其如命何。","pinyin":"gōng bó liáo sù zǐ lù yú jì sūn zǐ fú jǐng bó yǐ gào yuē fū zǐ gù yǒu huò zhì yú gōng bó liáo wú lì yóu néng sì zhū shì cháo zǐ yuē dào zhī jiāng xíng yě yú mìng yě dào zhī jiāng fèi yě yú mìng yě gōng bó liáo qí rú mìng hé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Kung-po Liao, having slandered Tsze-lu to Chi-sun, Tsze-fu Ching-po informed Confucius of it, saying, \"Our master is certainly being led astray by the Kung-po Liao, but I have still power enough left to cut Liao off, and expose his corpse in the market and in the court.\" 2. The Master said, \"If my principles are to advance, it is so ordered. If they are to fall to the ground, it is so ordered. What can the Kung-po Liao do where such ordering is concerned?\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-039","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":39,"classicalChinese":"子曰，贤者辟世。其次辟地。其次辟色。其次辟言。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xián zhě bì shì qí cì bì dì qí cì bì sè qí cì bì yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Some men of worth retire from the world. 2. Some retire from particular states. 3. Some retire because of disrespectful looks. 4. Some retire because of contradictory language.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-040","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":40,"classicalChinese":"子曰，作者七人矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zuò zhě qī rén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Those who have done this are seven men.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-041","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":41,"classicalChinese":"子路宿于石门，晨门曰，奚自。子路曰，自孔氏。曰，是知其不可而为之者与。","pinyin":"zǐ lù sù yú shí mén chén mén yuē xī zì zǐ lù yuē zì kǒng shì yuē shì zhī qí bù kě ér wéi zhī zhě yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu happening to pass the night in Shih-man, the gatekeeper said to him, \"Whom do you come from?\" Tsze-lu said, \"From Mr. K'ung.\" \"It is he,—is it not?\"—said the other, \"who knows the impracticable nature of the times and yet will be doing in them.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-042","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":42,"classicalChinese":"子击磬于卫。有荷蒉者而过孔氏之门者，曰，有心哉。击磬乎。既而曰，鄙哉，硁硁乎。莫己知也，斯已而已矣。深则厉，浅则揭。子曰，果哉。末之难矣。","pinyin":"zǐ jí qìng yú wèi yǒu hè kuì zhě ér guò kǒng shì zhī mén zhě yuē yǒu xīn zāi jí qìng hū jì ér yuē bǐ zāi kēng kēng hū mò jǐ zhī yě sī jǐ ér yǐ yǐ shēn zé lì qiǎn zé qì zǐ yuē guǒ zāi mò zhī nán yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master was playing, one day, on a musical stone in Wei, when a man, carrying a straw basket, passed the door","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-043","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":43,"classicalChinese":"子张曰，书云，高宗谅阴三年不言，何谓也。子曰，何必高宗，古之人皆然，君薨，百官总己，以听于冢宰，三年。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng yuē shū yún gāo zōng liáng ān sān nián bù yán hé wèi yě zǐ yuē hé bì gāo zōng gǔ zhī rén jiē rán jūn hōng bǎi guān zǒng jǐ yǐ tìng yú zhǒng zǎi sān nián","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang said, \"What is meant when the Shu says that Kao-tsung, while observing the usual imperial mourning, was for three years without speaking?\" 2. The Master said, \"Why must Kao-tsung be referred to as an example of this? The ancients all did so. When the sovereign died, the officers all attended to their several duties, taking instructions from the prime minister for three years.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-044","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":44,"classicalChinese":"子曰，上好礼，则民易使也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shàng hào lǐ zé mín yì shǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When rulers love to observe the rules of propriety, the people respond readily to the calls on them for service.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-045","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":45,"classicalChinese":"子路问君子，子曰，修己以敬。曰，如斯而已乎。曰，修己以安人，曰，如斯而已乎。曰，修己以安百姓。修己以安百姓，尧舜其犹病诸。","pinyin":"zǐ lù wèn jūn zǐ zǐ yuē xiū jǐ yǐ jìng yuē rú sī ér yǐ hū yuē xiū jǐ yǐ ān rén yuē rú sī ér yǐ hū yuē xiū jǐ yǐ ān bǎi xìng xiū jǐ yǐ ān bǎi xìng yáo shùn qí yóu bìng zhū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu asked what constituted the superior man. The Master said, \"The cultivation of himself in reverential carefulness.\" \"And is this all?\" said Tsze-lu. \"He cultivates himself so as to give rest to others,\" was the reply. \"And is this all?\" again asked Tsze-lu. The Master said, \"He cultivates himself so as to give rest to all the people. He cultivates himself so as to give rest to all the people:—even Yao and Shun were still solicitous about this.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-046","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":46,"classicalChinese":"原壤夷俟。子曰，幼而不孙弟，长而无述焉，老而不死，是为贼。以杖叩其胫。","pinyin":"yuán rǎng yí sì zǐ yuē yòu ér bú xùn tì zhǎng ér wú shù yān lǎo ér bù sǐ shì wéi zéi yǐ zhàng kòu qí jìng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Yuan Zang was squatting on his heels, and","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"xian-wen-047","bookSlug":"xian-wen","bookNumber":14,"sentenceNumber":47,"classicalChinese":"阙党童子将命，或问之曰，益者与。子曰，吾见其居于位也，见其与先生并行也，非求益者也，欲速成者也。","pinyin":"quē dǎng tóng zǐ jiāng mìng huò wèn zhī yuē yì zhě yú zǐ yuē wú jiàn qí jū yú wèi yě jiàn qí yǔ xiān shēng bìng xíng yě fēi qiú yì zhě yě yù sù chéng zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. A youth of the village of Ch'ueh was employed by Confucius to carry the messages between him and his visitors. Some one asked about him, saying, \"I suppose he has made great progress.\" 2. The Master said, \"I observe that he is fond of occupying the seat of a full-grown man; I observe that he walks shoulder to shoulder with his elders. He is not one who is seeking to make progress in learning. He wishes quickly to become a man.\"","themes":["analects","xian-wen"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"wei-ling-gong","number":15,"zhTitle":"卫灵公","enTitle":"Wei Ling Kung","chapterCount":41,"sentences":[{"id":"wei-ling-gong-001","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"衞灵公问陈于孔子。孔子对曰，俎豆之事，则尝闻之矣，军旅之事，未之学也。明日遂行。在陈绝粮，从者病，莫能兴。子路愠见曰，君子亦有穷乎。子曰，君子固穷，小人穷斯滥矣。","pinyin":"wèi líng gōng wèn zhèn yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ duì yuē zǔ dòu zhī shì zé cháng wén zhī yǐ jūn lǚ zhī shì wèi zhī xué yě míng rì suì xíng zài chén jué liáng zòng zhě bìng mò néng xīng zǐ lù yùn xiàn yuē jūn zǐ yì yǒu qióng hū zǐ yuē jūn zǐ gù qióng xiǎo rén qióng sī làn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Duke Ling of Wei asked Confucius about tactics. Confucius replied, \"I have heard all about sacrificial vessels, but I have not learned military matters.\" On this, he took his departure the next day. 2. When he was in Chan, their provisions were exhausted, and his followers became so ill that they were unable to rise. 3. Tsze-lu, with evident dissatisfaction, said, \"Has the superior man likewise to endure in this way?\" The Master said, \"The superior man may indeed have to endure want, but the mean man, when he is in want, gives way to unbridled license.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-002","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，赐也，女以予为多学而识之者与。对曰，然，非与。曰，非也，予一以贯之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē cì yě rǔ yǐ yú wéi duō xué ér zhì zhī zhě yú duì yuē rán fēi yú yuē fēi yě yú yī yǐ guàn zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Ts'ze, you think, I suppose, that I am one who learns many things and keeps them in memory?\" 2. Tsze-kung replied, \"Yes,—but perhaps it is not so?\" 3. \"No,\" was the answer; \"I seek a unity all-pervading.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-003","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，由，知德者鲜矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yóu zhī dé zhě xiǎn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Yu, those who know virtue are few.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-004","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子曰，无为而治者，其舜也与，夫何为哉，恭己正南面而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú wéi ér zhì zhě qí shùn yě yú fú hé wéi zāi gōng jǐ zhèng nán miàn ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"May not Shun be instanced as having governed efficiently without exertion? What did he do? He did nothing but gravely and reverently occupy his royal seat.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-005","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子张问行。子曰，言忠信，行笃敬，虽蛮貊之邦行矣。言不忠信，行不笃敬，虽州里行乎哉。立，则见其参于前也，在舆，则见其倚于衡也。夫然后行。子张书诸绅。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn xíng zǐ yuē yán zhōng xìn xíng dǔ jìng suī mán mò zhī bāng xíng yǐ yán bù zhōng xìn xíng bù dǔ jìng suī zhōu lǐ xíng hū zāi lì zé jiàn qí shēn yú qián yě zài yú zé jiàn qí yǐ yú héng yě fú rán hòu xíng zǐ zhāng shū zhū shēn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang asked how a man should conduct himself, so as to be everywhere appreciated. 2. The Master said, \"Let his words be sincere and truthful, and his actions honourable and careful;—such conduct may be practised among the rude tribes of the South or the North. If his words be","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-006","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子曰，直哉史鱼，邦有道如矢，邦无道如矢。君子哉，蘧伯玉，邦有道，则仕，邦无道，则可卷而怀之。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhí zāi shǐ yú bāng yǒu dào rú shǐ bāng wú dào rú shǐ jūn zǐ zāi qú bó yù bāng yǒu dào zé shì bāng wú dào zé kě juǎn ér huái zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Truly straightforward was the historiographer Yu. When good government prevailed in his State, he was like an arrow. When bad government prevailed, he was like an arrow. 2. A superior man indeed is Chu Po-yu! When good government prevails in his state, he is to be found in office. When bad government prevails, he can roll his principles up, and keep them in his breast.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-007","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子曰，可与言，而不与之言，失人，不可与言，而与之言，失言，知者不失人，亦不失言。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē kě yǔ yán ér bù yǔ zhī yán shī rén bù kě yǔ yán ér yǔ zhī yán shī yán zhì zhě bù shī rén yì bù shī yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When a man may be spoken with, not to speak to him is to err in reference to the man. When a man may not be spoken with, to speak to him is to err in reference to our words. The wise err neither in regard to their man nor to their words.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-008","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，志士，仁人，无求生以害仁，有杀身以成仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì shì rén rén wú qiú shēng yǐ hài rén yǒu shā shēn yǐ chéng rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The determined scholar and the man of virtue will not seek to live at the expense of injuring their virtue. They will even sacrifice their lives to preserve their virtue complete.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-009","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子贡问为仁。子曰，工欲善其事，必先利其器，居是邦也，事其大夫之贤者，友其士之仁者。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn wéi rén zǐ yuē gōng yù shàn qí shì bì xiān lì qí qì jū shì bāng yě shì qí dà fū zhī xián zhě yǒu qí shì zhī rén zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked about the practice of virtue. The Master said, \"The mechanic, who wishes to do his work well, must first sharpen his tools. When you are living in any state, take service with the most worthy among its great officers, and make friends of the most virtuous among its scholars.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-010","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"颜渊问为邦。子曰，行夏之时，乘殷之辂，服周之冕，乐则韶舞。放郑声，远佞人。郑声淫，佞人殆。","pinyin":"yán yuān wèn wéi bāng zǐ yuē xíng xià zhī shí chéng yīn zhī lù fú zhōu zhī miǎn yuè zé sháo wǔ fàng zhèng shēng yuǎn nìng rén zhèng shēng yín nìng rén dài","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yen Yuan asked how the government of a country should be administered. 2. The Master said, \"Follow the seasons of Hsia.","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-011","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人无远虑，必有近忧。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén wú yuǎn lǜ bì yǒu jìn yōu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"If a man take no thought about what is distant, he will find sorrow near at hand.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-012","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，已矣乎，吾未见好德如好色者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǐ yǐ hū wú wèi jiàn hào dé rú hào sè zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"It is all over! I have not seen one who loves virtue as he loves beauty.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-013","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，臧文仲，其窃位者与。知柳下惠之贤，而不与立也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zāng wén zhòng qí qiè wèi zhě yú zhī liǔ xià huì zhī xián ér bù yǔ lì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Was not Tsang Wan like one who had stolen his situation? He knew the virtue and the talents","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-014","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，躬自厚，而薄责于人，则远怨矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gōng zì hòu ér bó zé yú rén zé yuǎn yuàn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who requires much from himself and little from others, will keep himself from being the object of resentment.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-015","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不曰如之何，如之何者，吾末如之何也已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bù yuē rú zhī hé rú zhī hé zhě wú mò rú zhī hé yě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When a man is not in the habit of saying—'What shall I think of this? What shall I think of this?' I can indeed do nothing with him!\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-016","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，群居终日，言不及义，好行小慧，难矣哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qún jū zhōng rì yán bù jí yì hào xíng xiǎo huì nán yǐ zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When a number of people are together, for a whole day, without their conversation turning on righteousness, and when they are fond of carrying out the suggestions of a small shrewdness;—theirs is indeed a hard case.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-017","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子义以为质，礼以行之，孙以出之，信以成之。君子哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yì yǐ wéi zhì lǐ yǐ xíng zhī xùn yǐ chū zhī xìn yǐ chéng zhī jūn zǐ zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man in everything considers righteousness to be essential. He performs it according to the rules of propriety. He brings it forth in humility. He completes it with sincerity. This is indeed a superior man.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-018","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子病无能焉，不病人之不己知也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bìng wú néng yān bú bìng rén zhī bù jǐ zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is distressed by his want of ability. He is not distressed by men's not knowing him.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-019","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子疾没世，而名不称焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ jí mò shì ér míng bù chēng yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man dislikes the thought of his name not being mentioned after his death.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-020","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子求诸己，小人求诸人。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ qiú zhū jǐ xiǎo rén qiú zhū rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"What the superior man seeks, is in himself. What the mean man seeks, is in others.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-021","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子矜而不争，群而不党。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ jīn ér bù zhēng qún ér bù dǎng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is dignified, but does not wrangle. He is sociable, but not a partizan.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-022","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子不以言举人，不以人废言。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bù yǐ yán jǔ rén bù yǐ rén fèi yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man does not promote a man simply on account of his words, nor does he put aside good words because of the man.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-023","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子贡问曰，有一言，而可以终身行之者乎。子曰，其恕乎，己所不欲，勿施于人。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng wèn yuē yǒu yì yán ér kě yǐ zhōng shēn xíng zhī zhě hū zǐ yuē qí shù hū jǐ suǒ bú yù wù shī yú rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung asked, saying, \"Is there one word which may serve as a rule of practice for all one's life?\" The Master said, \"Is not reciprocity such a word? What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-024","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾之于人也谁毁，谁誉，如有所誉者，其有所试矣。斯民也，三代之所以直道而行也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú zhī yú rén yě shuí huǐ shuí yù rú yǒu suǒ yù zhě qí yǒu suǒ shì yǐ sī mín yě sān dài zhī suǒ yǐ zhí dào ér xíng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"In my dealings with men, whose evil do I blame, whose goodness do I praise, beyond what is proper? If I do sometimes exceed in praise, there must be ground for it in my examination of the individual. 2. \"This people supplied the ground why the three dynasties pursued the path of straightforwardness.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-025","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾犹及史之阙文也，有马者，借人乘之，今亡矣夫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú yóu jí shǐ zhī quē wén yě yǒu mǎ zhě jiè rén chéng zhī jīn wú yǐ fú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Even in my early days, a historiographer would leave a blank in his text, and he who had a horse would lend him to another to ride. Now, alas! there are no such things.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-026","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，巧言乱德，小不忍，则乱大谋。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qiǎo yán luàn dé xiǎo bù rěn zé luàn dà móu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Specious words confound virtue. Want of forbearance in small matters confounds great plans.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-027","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":27,"classicalChinese":"子曰，众恶之，必察焉，众好之，必察焉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhòng wù zhī bì chá yān zhòng hào zhī bì chá yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When the multitude hate a man, it is necessary to examine into the case. When the multitude like a man, it is necessary to examine into the case.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-028","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":28,"classicalChinese":"子曰，人能弘道，非道弘人。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē rén néng hóng dào fēi dào hóng rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A man can enlarge the principles which he follows; those principles do not enlarge the man.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-029","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":29,"classicalChinese":"子曰，过而不改，是谓过矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē guò ér bù gǎi shì wèi guò yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"To have faults and not to reform them,—this, indeed, should be pronounced having faults.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-030","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":30,"classicalChinese":"子曰，吾尝终日不食，终夜不寝，以思，无益，不如学也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wú cháng zhōng rì bù shí zhōng yè bù qǐn yǐ sī wú yì bù rú xué yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I have been the whole day","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-031","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":31,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子谋道不谋食，耕也，馁在其中矣，学也，禄在其中矣，君子忧道，不忧贫。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ móu dào bù móu shí gēng yě něi zài qí zhōng yǐ xué yě lù zài qí zhōng yǐ jūn zǐ yōu dào bù yōu pín","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The object of the superior man is truth. Food is not his object. There is plowing;—even in that there is sometimes want. So with learning;—emolument may be found in it. The superior man is anxious lest he should not get truth; he is not anxious lest poverty should come upon him.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-032","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":32,"classicalChinese":"子曰，知及之，仁不能守之，虽得之，必失之。知及之，仁能守之，不庄以莅之，则民不敬。知及之，仁能守之，庄以莅之，动之不以礼，未善也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē zhì jí zhī rén bù néng shǒu zhī suī dé zhī bì shī zhī zhì jí zhī rén néng shǒu zhī bùz huān gyǐ lì zhī zé mín bú jìng zhì jí zhī rén néng shǒu zhī zhuān gyǐ lì zhī dòng zhī bù yǐ lǐ wèi shàn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"When a man's knowledge is sufficient to attain, and his virtue is not sufficient to enable him to hold, whatever he may have gained, he will lose again. 2. \"When his knowledge is sufficient to attain, and he has virtue enough to hold fast, if he cannot govern with dignity, the people will not respect him. 3. \"When his knowledge is sufficient to attain, and he has virtue enough to hold fast; when he governs also with dignity, yet if he try to move the people contrary to the rules of propriety:—full excellence is not reached.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-033","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":33,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子不可小知，而可大受也，小人不可大受，而可小知也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ bù kě xiǎo zhī ér kě dà shòu yě xiǎo rén bù kě dà shòu ér kě xiǎo zhī yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man cannot be known in little matters; but he may be intrusted with great concerns. The small man may not be intrusted with great concerns, but he may be known in little matters.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-034","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":34,"classicalChinese":"子曰，民之于仁也，甚于水火，水火吾见蹈而死者矣，未见蹈仁而死者也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē mín zhī yú rén yě shèn yú shuǐ huǒ shuǐ huǒ wú jiàn dǎo ér sǐ zhě yǐ wèi jiàn dǎo rén ér sǐ zhě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Virtue is more to man than either water or fire. I have seen men die from treading on water and fire, but I have never seen a man die from treading the course of virtue.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-035","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":35,"classicalChinese":"子曰，当仁，不让于师。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dāng rén bú ràng yú shī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Let every man consider virtue as what devolves on himself. He may not yield the performance of it even to his teacher.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-036","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":36,"classicalChinese":"子曰，君子贞，而不谅。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē jūn zǐ zhēn ér bú liàng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"The superior man is correctly firm, and not firm merely.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-037","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":37,"classicalChinese":"子曰，事君敬其事，而后其食。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē shì jūn jìng qí shì ér hòu qí shí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"A minister, in serving his prince, reverently discharges his duties, and makes his emolument a secondary consideration.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-038","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":38,"classicalChinese":"子曰，有教，无类。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yǒu jiào wú lèi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In teaching there should be no distinction of classes.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-039","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":39,"classicalChinese":"子曰，道不同，不相为谋。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dào bù tóng bù xiāng wéi móu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Those whose courses are different cannot lay plans for one another.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-040","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":40,"classicalChinese":"子曰，辞，达而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē cí dá ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"In language it is simply required that it convey the meaning.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-ling-gong-041","bookSlug":"wei-ling-gong","bookNumber":15,"sentenceNumber":41,"classicalChinese":"师冕见，及阶，子曰，阶也。及席，子曰，席也。","pinyin":"shī miǎn xiàn jí jiē zǐ yuē jiē yě jí xí zǐ yuē xí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Music-master, Mien, having called upon him, when they came to the steps, the Master said, \"Here are the steps.\" When they came to the mat for the guest to sit upon, he said, \"Here is the mat.\" When all were seated, the Master informed him, saying, \"So and so is here; so and so is here.\" 2. The Music-master, Mien, having gone out, Tsze-chang asked, saying. \"Is it the rule to tell those things to the Music-master?\" 3. The Master said, \"Yes. This is certainly the rule for those who lead the blind.\"","themes":["analects","wei-ling-gong"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"ji-shi","number":16,"zhTitle":"季氏","enTitle":"Ke She","chapterCount":14,"sentences":[{"id":"ji-shi-001","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"季氏将伐颛臾。冉有季路见于孔子曰，季氏将有事于颛臾。","pinyin":"jì shì jiāng fá zhuān yú rǎn yǒu jì lù xiàn yú kǒng zǐ yuē jì shì jiāng yǒu shì yú zhuān yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The head of the Chi family was going to attack Chwan-yu. 2. Zan Yu and Chi-lu had an interview with Confucius, and said, \"Our chief, Chi, is going to commence operations against Chwan-yu.\" 3. Confucius said, \"Ch'iu, is it not you who are in fault here? 4. \"Now, in regard to Chwan-yu, long ago, a former king appointed its ruler to preside over the sacrifices to the eastern Mang; moreover, it is in the midst of the territory of our State; and its ruler is a minister in direct connexion with the sovereign:—What has your chief to do with attacking it?\" 5. Zan Yu said, \"Our master wishes the thing; neither of us two ministers wishes it.\" 6. Confucius said, \"Ch'iu, there are the words of Chau Zan,—'When he can put forth his ability, he takes his place in the ranks of office; when he finds himself unable to do so, he retires from it. How can he be used as a guide to a blind man, who does not support him when tottering, nor raise him up when fallen?' 7. \"And further, you speak wrongly. When a tiger or rhinoceros escapes from his cage; when a tortoise or piece of jade is injured in its repository:—whose is the fault?\" 8. Zan Yu said, \"But at present, Chwan-yu is strong and near to Pi; if our chief do not now take it, it will hereafter be a sorrow to his descendants.\" 9. Confucius said. \"Ch'iu, the superior man hates that declining to say—'I want such and such a thing,' and framing explanations for the conduct. 10. \"I have heard that rulers of States and chiefs of families are not troubled lest their people should be few, but are troubled lest they should not keep their several places; that they are not troubled with fears of poverty, but are troubled with fears of a want of contented repose among the people in their several places. For when the people keep their several places, there will be no poverty; when harmony prevails, there will be no scarcity of people; and when there is such a contented repose, there will be no rebellious upsettings. 11. \"So it is.—Therefore, if remoter people are not submissive, all the influences of civil culture and virtue are to be cultivated to attract them to be so; and when they have been so attracted, they must be made contented and tranquil. 12. \"Now, here are you, Yu and Ch'iu, assisting your chief. Remoter people are not submissive, and, with your help, he cannot attract them to him. In his own territory there are divisions and downfalls, leavings and separations, and, with your help, he cannot preserve it. 13. \"And yet he is planning these hostile movements within the State.—I am afraid that the sorrow of the Chi-sun family will not be on account of Chwan-yu, but will be found within the screen of their own court.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-002","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，天下有道，则礼乐征伐，自天子出，天下无道，则礼乐征伐，自诸侯出，自诸侯出，盖十世希不失矣，自大夫出，五世希不失矣，陪臣执国命，三世希不失矣。天下有道，则政不在大夫。天下有道，则庶人不议。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē tiān xià yǒu dào zé lǐ yuè zhēng fá zì tiān zǐ chū tiān xià wú dào zé lǐ yuè zhēng fá zì zhū hóu chū zì zhū hóu chū gài shí shì xī bù shī yǐ zì dà fū chū wǔ shì xī bù shī yǐ péi chén zhí guó mìng sān shì xī bù shī yǐ tiān xià yǒu dào zé zhèng bú zài dà fū tiān xià yǒu dào zé shù rén bú yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Confucius said, \"When good government prevails in the empire, ceremonies, music, and punitive military expeditions proceed from the son of Heaven. When bad government prevails in the empire, ceremonies, music, and punitive military expeditions proceed from the princes. When these things proceed from the princes, as a rule, the cases will be few in which they do not lose their power in ten generations. When they proceed from the Great officers of the princes, as a rule, the cases will be few in which they do not lose their power in five generations. When the subsidiary ministers of the great officers hold in their grasp the orders of the state, as a rule, the cases will be few in which they do not lose their power in three generations. 2. \"When right principles prevail in the kingdom, government will not be in the hands of the Great officers. 3. \"When right principles prevail in the kingdom, there will be no discussions among the common people.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-003","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，禄之去公室，五世矣，政逮于大夫，四世矣，故夫三桓之子孙微矣。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē lù zhī qù gōng shì wǔ shì yǐ zhèng dài yú dà fū sì shì yǐ gù fú sān huán zhī zǐ sūn wēi yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"The revenue of the state has left the ducal House now for five generations. The government has been in the hands of the Great officers for four generations. On this account, the descendants of the three Hwan are much reduced.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-004","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，益者三友，损者三友，友直，友谅，友多闻，益矣，友便辟，友善柔，友便佞，损矣。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē yì zhě sān yǒu sǔn zhě sān yǒu yǒu zhí yǒu liàng yǒu duō wén yì yǐ yǒu pián pì yǒu shàn róu yǒu pián nìng sǔn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"There are three friendships which are advantageous, and three which are injurious. Friendship with the upright; friendship with the sincere; and friendship with the man of much observation:—these are advantageous. Friendship with the man of specious airs; friendship with the insinuatingly soft; and friendship with the glib-tongued:—these are injurious.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-005","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，益者三乐，损者三乐，乐节礼乐，乐道人之善，乐多贤友，益矣，乐骄乐，乐佚游，乐宴乐，损矣。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē yì zhě sān lè sǔn zhě sān lè lè jié lǐ yuè lè dào rén zhī shàn lè duō xián yǒu yì yǐ lè jiāo lè lè yì yóu lè yàn lè sǔn yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"There are three things men find enjoyment in which are advantageous, and three things they find enjoyment in which are injurious. To find enjoyment in the discriminating study of ceremonies and music; to find enjoyment in","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-006","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，侍于君子有三愆，言未及之而言，谓之躁，言及之而不言，谓之隐，未见颜色而言，谓之瞽。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē shì yú jūn zǐ yǒu sān qiān yán wèi jí zhī ér yán wèi zhī zào yán jí zhī ér bù yán wèi zhī yǐn wèi jiàn yán sè ér yán wèi zhī gǔ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"There are three errors to which they who stand in the presence of a man of virtue and station are liable. They may speak when it does not come to them to speak;—this is called rashness. They may not speak when it comes to them to speak;—this is called concealment. They may speak without looking at the countenance of their superior;—this is called blindness.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-007","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，君子有三戒，少之时，血气未定，戒之在色，及其壮也，血气方刚，戒之在斗，及其老也，血气既衰，戒之在得。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yǒu sān jiè shào zhī shí xuè qì wèi dìng jiè zhī zài sè jí qíz huàng yě xuè qì fāng gāng jiè zhī zài dòu jí qí lǎo yě xuè qì jì shuāi jiè zhī zài dé","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"There are three things which the superior man guards against. In youth, when the physical powers","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-008","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，君子有三畏，畏天命，畏大人，畏圣人之言。小人不知天命，而不畏也，狎大人，侮圣人之言。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yǒu sān wèi wèi tiān mìng wèi dà rén wèi shèng rén zhī yán xiǎo rén bù zhī tiān mìng ér bú wèi yě xiá dà rén wǔ shèng rén zhī yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Confucius said, \"There are three things of which the superior man stands in awe. He stands in awe of the ordinances of Heaven. He stands in awe of great men. He stands in awe of the words of sages. 2. \"The mean man does not know the ordinances of Heaven, and consequently does not stand in awe of them. He is disrespectful to great men. He makes sport of the words of sages.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-009","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，生而知之者，上也，学而知之者，次也，困而学之，又其次也。困而不学，民斯为下矣。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē shēng ér zhī zhī zhě shàng yě xué ér zhī zhī zhě cì yě kùn ér xué zhī yòu qí cì yě kùn ér bù xué mín sī wéi xià yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"Those who are born with the possession of knowledge are the highest class of men. Those who learn, and so, readily, get possession of knowledge, are the next.","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-010","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，君子有九思，视思明，听思聪，色思温，貌思恭，言思忠，事思敬，疑思问，忿思难，见得思义。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yǒu jiǔ sī shì sī míng tīng sī cōng sè sī wēn mào sī gōng yán sī zhōng shì sī jìng yí sī wèn fèn sī nàn jiàn dé sī yì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Confucius said, \"The superior man has nine things which are subjects with him of thoughtful consideration. In regard to the use of his eyes, he is anxious to see clearly. In regard to the use of his ears, he is anxious to hear distinctly. In regard to his countenance, he is anxious that it should be benign. In regard to his demeanor, he is anxious that it should be respectful. In regard to his speech, he is anxious that it should be sincere. In regard to his doing of business, he is anxious that it should be reverently careful. In regard to what he doubts about, he is anxious to question others. When he is angry, he thinks of the difficulties (his anger may involve him in). When he sees gain to be got, he thinks of righteousness.\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-011","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"孔子曰，见善如不及，见不善如探汤。吾见其人矣，吾闻其语矣。隐居以成其志，行义以达其道。吾闻其语矣，未见其人也。","pinyin":"kǒng zǐ yuē jiàn shàn rú bù jí jiàn bú shàn rú tàn tāng wú jiàn qí rén yǐ wú wén qí yǔ yǐ yǐn jū yǐ qiú qí zhì xíng yì yǐ dá qí dào wú wén qí yǔ yǐ wèi jiàn qí rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Confucius said, \"Contemplating good, and pursuing it, as if they could not reach it; contemplating evil, and shrinking from it, as they would from thrusting the hand into boiling water:—I have seen such men, as I have heard such words. 2. \"Living in retirement to study their aims, and practising","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-012","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"齐景公有马千驷，死之日，民无德而称焉，伯夷叔齐，饿于首阳之下，民到于今称之。其斯之谓与。","pinyin":"qí jǐng gōng yǒu mǎ qiān sì sǐ zhī rì mín wú dé ér chēng yān bó yí shū qí è yú shǒu yáng zhī xià mín dào yú jīn chēng zhī qí sī zhī wèi yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The duke Ching of Ch'i had a thousand teams, each of four horses, but on the day of his death, the people did not praise him for a single virtue. Po-i and Shu-ch'i died of hunger at the foot of the Shau-yang mountain, and the people, down to the present time, praise them. 2. \"Is not that saying illustrated by this?\"","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-013","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"陈亢问于伯鱼曰，子亦有异闻乎。对曰，未也，尝独立，鲤趋而过庭，曰，学诗乎。对曰，未也。不学诗，无以言。","pinyin":"chén gāng wèn yú bó yú yuē zǐ yì yǒu yì wén hū duì yuē wèi yě cháng dú lì lǐ qū ér guò tíng yuē xué shī hū duì yuē wèi yě bú xué shī wú yǐ yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Ch'an K'ang asked Po-yu, saying, \"Have you heard any lessons from your father different from what we have all heard?\" 2. Po-yu replied, \"No. He was standing alone once, when I passed below the hall with hasty steps, and said to me, 'Have you learned the Odes?' On my replying 'Not yet,' he added, 'If you do not learn the Odes, you will not be fit to converse with.' I retired and studied the Odes.","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"ji-shi-014","bookSlug":"ji-shi","bookNumber":16,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"邦君之妻，君称之曰夫人，夫人自称曰小童，邦人称之曰君夫人，称诸异邦曰寡小君，异邦人称之，亦曰君夫人。","pinyin":"bāng jūn zhī qī jūn chēng zhī yuē fū rén fū rén zì chēng yuē xiǎo tóng bāng rén chēng zhī yuē jūn fū rén chēng zhū yì bāng yuē guǎ xiǎo jūn yì bāng rén chēng zhī yì yuē jūn fū rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The wife of the prince of a state is called by him fu zan . She calls herself hsiao t'ung . The people of the State call her chun fu zan , and, to the people of other States, they call her k'wa hsiao chun . The people of other states also call her chun fu zan .","themes":["analects","ji-shi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"yang-huo","number":17,"zhTitle":"阳货","enTitle":"Yang Ho","chapterCount":26,"sentences":[{"id":"yang-huo-001","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"阳货欲见孔子，孔子不见，归孔子豚。孔子时其亡也，而往拜之，遇诸涂。谓孔子曰，来，予与尔言。曰，怀其宝而迷其邦，可谓仁乎。曰，不可。好从事而亟失时，可谓知乎。曰，不可。日月逝矣，岁不我与。孔子曰，诺，吾将仕矣。","pinyin":"yáng huò yù jiàn kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ bú jiàn kuì kǒng zǐ tún kǒng zǐ shí qí wú yě ér wǎng bài zhī yù zhū tú wèi kǒng zǐ yuē lái yú yǔ ěr yán yuē huái qí bǎo ér mí qí bāng kě wèi rén hū yuē bù kě hào cóng shì ér qì shī shí kě wèi zhì hū yuē bù kě rì yuè shì yǐ suì bù wǒ yǔ kǒng zǐ yuē nuò wú jiāng shì yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yang Ho wished to see Confucius, but Confucius would not go to see him. On this, he sent a present of a pig to Confucius, who, having chosen a time when Ho was not at home, went to pay his respects for the gift. He met him, however, on the way. 2. Ho said to Confucius, \"Come, let me speak with you.\" He then asked, \"Can he be called benevolent who keeps his jewel in his","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-002","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子曰，性相近也，习相远也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xìng xiāng jìn yě xí xiāng yuǎn yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"By nature, men are nearly alike; by practice, they get to be wide apart.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-003","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，唯上知与下愚不移。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wéi shàng zhì yǔ xià yú bù yí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"There are only the wise of the highest class, and the stupid of the lowest class, who cannot be changed.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-004","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子之武城，闻弦歌之声。夫子莞尔而笑曰，割鸡焉用牛刀。子游对曰，昔者偃也，闻诸夫子曰，君子学道则爱人，小人学道则易使也。子曰，二三子，偃之言是也，前言戏之耳。","pinyin":"zǐ zhī wǔ chéng wén xián gē zhī shēng fū zǐ wǎn ěr ér xiào yuē gē jī yān yòng niú dāo zǐ yóu duì yuē xī zhě yǎn yě wén zhū fū zǐ yuē jūn zǐ xué dào zé ài rén xiǎo rén xué dào zé yì shǐ yě zǐ yuē èr sān zǐ yǎn zhī yán shì yě qián yán xì zhī ěr","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master, having come to Wu-ch'ang, heard there the sound of stringed instruments and singing. 2. Well pleased and smiling, he said, \"Why use an ox knife to kill a fowl?\" 3. Tsze-yu replied, \"Formerly, Master, I heard you say,—'When the man of high station is well instructed, he loves men; when the man of low station is well instructed, he is easily ruled.'\" 4. The Master said, \"My disciples, Yen's words are right. What I said was only in sport.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-005","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"公山弗扰以费畔，召，子欲往。子路不说，曰，末之也已，何必公山氏之之也。子曰，夫召我者，而岂徒哉。如有用我者，吾其为东周乎。","pinyin":"gōng shān fú rǎo yǐ bì pàn zhào zǐ yù wǎng zǐ lù bú yuè yuē mò zhī yě yǐ hé bì gōng shān shì zhī zhī yě zǐ yuē fú zhào wǒ zhě ér qǐ tú zāi rú yǒu yòng wǒ zhě wú qí wèi dōng zhōu hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Kung-shan Fu-zao, when he was holding Pi, and in an attitude of rebellion, invited the Master to visit him, who was rather inclined to go. 2. Tsze-lu was displeased, and said, \"Indeed, you cannot go! Why must you think of going to see Kung-shan?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-006","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子张问仁于孔子，孔子曰，能行五者于天下为仁矣。请问之，曰，恭，宽，信，敏，惠，恭，则不侮，宽，则得众，信，则人任焉，敏，则有功，惠，则足以使人。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn rén yú kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ yuē néng xíng wǔ zhě yú tiān xià wéi rén yǐ qǐng wèn zhī yuē gōng kuān xìn mǐn huì gōng zé bù wǔ kuān zé dé zhòng xìn zé rén rèn yān mǐn zé yǒu gōng huì zé zú yǐ shǐ rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang asked Confucius about perfect virtue. Confucius said, \"To be able to practise five things everywhere under heaven constitutes perfect virtue.\" He begged to ask what they were, and was told, \"Gravity, generosity of soul, sincerity, earnestness, and kindness. If you are grave, you will not be treated with disrespect. If you are generous, you will win all. If you are sincere, people will repose trust in you. If you are earnest, you will accomplish much. If you are kind, this will enable you to employ the services of others.","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-007","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"佛肸召。子欲往。子路曰，昔者由也，闻诸夫子曰，亲于其身，为不善者，君子不入也，佛肸以中牟畔，子之往也，如之何。子曰，然，有是言也，不曰坚乎，磨而不磷，不曰白乎，涅而不缁。吾岂匏瓜也哉，焉能系而不食。","pinyin":"bì xī zhào zǐ yù wǎng zǐ lù yuē xī zhě yóu yě wén zhū fū zǐ yuē qīn yú qí shēn wéi bú shàn zhě jūn zǐ bú rù yě bì xī yǐ zhōng móu pàn zǐ zhī wǎng yě rú zhī hé zǐ yuē rán yǒu shì yán yě bù yuē jiān hū mó ér bú lìn bù yuē bái hū niè ér bù zī wú qǐ páo guā yě zāi yān néng xì ér bù shí","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Pi Hsi inviting him to visit him, the Master was inclined to go. 2. Tsze-lu said, \"Master, formerly I have heard you say, 'When a man in his own person is guilty of doing evil, a superior man will not associate with him.' Pi Hsi is in rebellion, holding possession of Chung-mau; if you go to him, what shall be said?\" 3. The Master said, \"Yes, I did use these words. But is it not said, that, if a thing be really hard, it may be ground without being made thin? Is it not said, that, if a thing be really white, it may be steeped in a dark fluid without being made black? 4. \"Am I a bitter gourd! How can I be hung up out of the way of being eaten?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-008","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子曰，由也，女闻六言六蔽矣乎。对曰，未也。居，吾语女。好仁不好学，其蔽也愚，好智不好学，其蔽也荡，好信不好学，其蔽也贼，好直不好学，其蔽也绞，好勇不好学，其蔽也乱，好刚不好学，其蔽也狂。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yóu yě rǔ wén liù yán liù bì yǐ hū duì yuē wèi yě jū wú yù rǔ hào rén bú hào xué qí bì yě yú hào zhì bú hào xué qí bì yě dàng hào xìn bú hào xué qí bì yě zéi hào zhí bú hào xué qí bì yě jiǎo hào yǒng bú hào xué qí bì yě luàn hào gāng bú hào xué qí bì yě kuáng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Yu, have you heard the six words to which are attached six becloudings?\" Yu replied, \"I have not.\" 2. \"Sit down, and I will tell them to you. 3. \"There is the love of being benevolent without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to a foolish simplicity. There is the love of knowing without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to dissipation of mind. There is the love of being sincere without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to an injurious disregard of consequences. There is the love of straightforwardness without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to rudeness. There is the love of boldness without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to insubordination. There is the love of firmness without the love of learning;—the beclouding here leads to extravagant conduct.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-009","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子曰，小子，何莫学夫诗。诗可以兴。可以观。可以群。可以怨。迩之事父，远之事君。多识于鸟兽草木之名。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xiǎo zǐ hé mò xué fú shī shī kě yǐ xīng kě yǐ guān kě yǐ qún kě yǐ yuàn ěr zhī shì fù yuǎn zhī shì jūn duō zhì yú niǎo shòu cǎo mù zhī míng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"My children, why do you not study the Book of Poetry? 2. \"The Odes serve to stimulate the mind. 3. \"They may be used for purposes of self-contemplation. 4. \"They teach the art of sociability. 5. \"They show how to regulate feelings of resentment. 6. \"From them you learn the more immediate duty of serving one's father, and the remoter one of serving one's prince. 7. \"From them we become largely acquainted with the names of birds, beasts, and plants.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-010","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子谓伯鱼曰，女为周南召南矣乎，人而不为周南召南，其犹正墙面而立也与。","pinyin":"zǐ wèi bó yú yuē rǔ wéi zhōu nán shào nán yǐ hū rén ér bù wéi zhōu nán shào nán qí yóu zhèng qiáng miàn ér lì yě yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said to Po-yu, \"Do you give yourself to the Chau-nan and the Shao-nan. The man who has not studied the Chau-nan and the Shao-nan, is like one who stands with his face right against a wall. Is he not so?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-011","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子曰，礼云礼云，玉帛云乎哉，乐云乐云，钟鼓云乎哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē lǐ yún lǐ yún yù bó yún hū zāi yuè yún yuè yún zhōng gǔ yún hū zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"'It is according to the rules of propriety,' they say.—'It is according to the rules of propriety,' they say. Are gems and silk all that is meant by propriety? 'It is music,' they say.—'It is music,' they say. Are bells and drums all that is meant by music?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-012","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子曰，色厉而内荏，譬诸小人，其犹穿窬之盗也与。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē sè lì ér nèi rěn pì zhū xiǎo rén qí yóu chuān yú zhī dào yě yú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"He who puts on an appearance of stern firmness, while inwardly he is weak, is like one of the small, mean people;—yea, is he not like the thief who breaks through, or climbs over, a wall?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-013","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子曰，乡原，德之贼也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē xiāng yuàn dé zhī zéi yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Your good, careful people of the villages are the thieves of virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-014","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子曰，道听而涂说，德之弃也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē dào tīng ér tú shuō dé zhī qì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"To tell, as we go along, what we have heard on the way, is to cast away our virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-015","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子曰，鄙夫，可与事君也与哉。其未得之也，患得之，既得之，患失之。苟患失之，无所不至矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bǐ fū kě yǔ shì jūn yě yú zāi qí wèi dé zhī yě huàn dé zhī jì dé zhī huàn shī zhī gǒu huàn shī zhī wú suǒ bú zhì yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"There are those mean creatures! How impossible it is along with them to serve one's prince! 2. \"While they have not got their aims, their anxiety is how to get them. When they have got them, their anxiety is lest they should lose them. 3. \"When they are anxious lest such things should be lost, there is nothing to which they will not proceed.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-016","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"子曰，古者，民有三疾，今也或是之亡也。古之狂也肆，今之狂也荡，古之矜也廉，今之矜也忿戾，古之愚也直，今之愚也诈而已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē gǔ zhě mín yǒu sān jí jīn yě huò shì zhī wú yě gǔ zhī kuáng yě sì jīn zhī kuáng yě dàng gǔ zhī jīn yě lián jīn zhī jīn yě fèn lì gǔ zhī yú yě zhí jīn zhī yú yě zhà ér yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Anciently, men had three failings, which now perhaps are not to be found. 2. \"The high-mindedness of antiquity showed itself in a disregard of small things; the high-mindedness of the present day shows itself in wild license. The stern dignity of antiquity showed itself in grave reserve; the stern dignity of the present day shows itself in quarrelsome perverseness. The stupidity of antiquity showed itself in straightforwardness; the stupidity of the present day shows itself in sheer deceit.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-017","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"子曰，巧言令色鲜矣仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē qiǎo yán lìng sè xiǎn yǐ rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Fine words and an insinuating appearance are seldom associated with virtue.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-018","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"子曰，恶紫之夺朱也，恶郑声之乱雅乐也，恶利口之覆邦家者。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wù zǐ zhī duó zhū yě wù zhèng shēng zhī luàn yǎ yuè yě wù lì kǒu zhī fù bāng jiā zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"I hate the manner in which purple takes away the luster of vermilion. I hate the way in which the songs of Chang confound the music of the Ya. I hate those who with their sharp mouths overthrow kingdoms and families.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-019","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"子曰，予欲无言。子贡曰，子如不言，则小子何述焉。子曰，天何言哉，四时行焉，百物生焉，天何言哉。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē yú yù wú yán zǐ gòng yuē zǐ rú bù yán zé xiǎo zǐ hé shù yān zǐ yuē tiān hé yán zāi sì shí xíng yān bǎi wù shēng yān tiān hé yán zāi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"I would prefer not speaking.\" 2. Tsze-kung said, \"If you, Master, do not speak, what shall we, your disciples, have to record?\" 3. The Master said, \"Does Heaven speak? The four seasons pursue their courses, and all things are continually being produced, but does Heaven say anything?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-020","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"孺悲欲见孔子，孔子辞以疾，将命者出户，取瑟而歌，使之闻之。","pinyin":"rú bēi yù jiàn kǒng zǐ kǒng zǐ cí yǐ jí jiāng mìng zhě chū hù qǔ sè ér gē shǐ zhī wén zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Zu Pei wished to see Confucius, but Confucius declined, on the ground of being sick, to see him. When the bearer of this message went out at the door, (the Master) took his lute and sang to it, in order that Pei might hear him.","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-021","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"宰我问，三年之丧，期已久矣。君子三年不为礼，礼必坏，三年不为乐，乐必崩。旧谷既没，新谷既升，钻燧改火，期可已矣。子曰，食夫稻，衣夫锦，于女安乎。曰，安。女安，则为之。夫君子之居丧，食旨不甘，闻乐不乐，居处不安，故不为也。今女安，则为之。宰我出。子曰，予之不仁也。子生三年，然后免于父母之怀。夫三年之丧，天下之通丧也。予也，有三年之爱于其父母乎。","pinyin":"zǎi wǒ wèn sān nián zhī sāng qī yǐ jiǔ yǐ jūn zǐ sān nián bù wéi lǐ lǐ bì huài sān nián bù wéi yuè yuè bì bēng jiù gǔ jì mò xīn gǔ jì shēng zuān suì gái huǒ jī kě yǐ yǐ zǐ yuē shí fú dào yì fú jǐn yú rǔ ān hū yuē ān rǔ ān zé wéi zhī fú jūn zǐ zhī jū sāng shí zhǐ bù gān wén yuè bú lè jū chǔ bù ān gù bù wéi yě jīn rǔ ān zé wéi zhī zǎi wǒ chū zǐ yuē yú zhī bù rén yě zǐ shēng sān nián rán hòu miǎn yú fù mǔ zhī huái fú sān nián zhī sāng tiān xià zhī tōng sāng yě yú yě yǒu sān nián zhī ài yú qí fù mǔ hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsai Wo asked about the three years\" mourning for parents, saying that one year was long enough. 2. \"If the superior man,\" said he, \"abstains for three years from the observances of propriety, those observances will be quite lost. If for three years he abstains from music, music will be ruined. 3. \"Within a year the old grain is exhausted, and the new grain has sprung up, and, in procuring fire by friction, we go through all the changes of wood for that purpose. After a complete year, the mourning may stop.\" 4. The Master said, \"If you were, after a year, to eat good rice, and wear embroidered clothes, would you feel at ease?\" \"I should,\" replied Wo. 5. The Master said, \"If you can feel at ease, do it. But a superior man, during the whole period of mourning, does not enjoy pleasant food which he may eat, nor derive pleasure from music which he may hear. He also does not feel at ease, if he is comfortably lodged. Therefore he does not do what you propose. But now you feel at ease and may do it.\" 6. Tsai Wo then went out, and the Master said, \"This shows Yu's want of virtue. It is not till a child is three years old that it is allowed to leave the arms of its parents. And the three years\" mourning is universally observed throughout the empire. Did Yu enjoy the three years\" love of his parents?\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-022","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"子曰，饱食终日，无所用心，难矣哉，不有博弈者乎，为之犹贤乎已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bǎo shí zhōng rì wú suǒ yòng xīn nán yǐ zāi bù yǒu bó yì zhě hū wéi zhī yóu xián hū yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Hard is it to deal with him, who will stuff himself with food the whole day, without applying his mind to anything good! Are there not gamesters and chess players? To be one of these would still be better than doing nothing at all.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-023","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"子路曰，君子尙勇乎。子曰，君子义以为上，君子有勇而无义，为乱，小人有勇而无义，为盗。","pinyin":"zǐ lù yuē jūn zǐ shàng yǒng hū zǐ yuē jūn zǐ yì yǐ wéi shàng jūn zǐ yǒu yǒng ér wú yì wéi luàn xiǎo rén yǒu yǒng ér wú yì wéi dào","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-lu said, \"Does the superior man esteem valour?\" The Master said, \"The superior man holds righteousness to be of highest importance. A man in a superior situation, having valour without righteousness, will be guilty of insubordination; one of the lower people having valour without righteousness, will commit robbery.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-024","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，君子亦有恶乎。子曰，有恶，恶称人之恶者，恶居下流而讪上者，恶勇而无礼者，恶果敢而窒者。曰，赐也亦有恶乎。恶徼以为知者，恶不孙以为勇者，恶讦以为直者。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē jūn zǐ yì yǒu wù hū zǐ yuē yǒu wù wù chēng rén zhī è zhě wù jū xià liú ér shàn shàng zhě wù yǒng ér wú lǐ zhě wù guǒ gǎn ér zhì zhě yuē cì yě yì yǒu wù hū wù jiǎo yǐ wéi zhì zhě wù bú xùn yǐ wéi yǒng zhě wù jié yǐ wéi zhí zhě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-kung said, \"Has the superior man his hatreds also?\" The Master said, \"He has his hatreds. He hates those who proclaim the evil of others. He hates the man who,","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-025","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"子曰，唯女子与小人，为难养也，近之则不孙，远之则怨。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē wéi nǘ zǐ yǔ xiǎo rén wéi nán yǎng yě jìn zhī zé bú xùn yuǎn zhī zé yuàn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"Of all people, girls and servants are the most difficult to behave to. If you are familiar with them, they lose their humility. If you maintain a reserve towards them, they are discontented.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yang-huo-026","bookSlug":"yang-huo","bookNumber":17,"sentenceNumber":26,"classicalChinese":"子曰，年四十而见恶焉，其终也已。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē nián sì shí ér jiàn wù yān qí zhōng yě yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The Master said, \"When a man at forty is the object of dislike, he will always continue what he is.\"","themes":["analects","yang-huo"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"wei-zi","number":18,"zhTitle":"微子","enTitle":"Wei Tsze","chapterCount":11,"sentences":[{"id":"wei-zi-001","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"微子去之，箕子为之奴，比干谏而死。孔子曰，殷有三仁焉。","pinyin":"wēi zǐ qù zhī jī zǐ wéi zhī nú bǐ gān jiàn ér sǐ kǒng zǐ yuē yīn yǒu sān rén yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Viscount of Wei withdrew from the court. The Viscount of Chi became a slave to Chau. Pi-kan remonstrated with him and died. 2. Confucius said, \"The Yin dynasty possessed these three men of virtue.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-002","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"柳下惠为士师，三黜。人曰，子未可以去乎。曰，直道而事人，焉往而不三黜。枉道而事人，何必去父母之邦。","pinyin":"liǔ xià huì wéi shì shī sān chù rén yuē zǐ wèi kě yǐ qù hū yuē zhí dào ér shì rén yān wǎng ér bù sān chù wǎng dào ér shì rén hé bì qù fù mǔ zhī bāng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Hui of Liu-hsia being chief criminal judge, was thrice dismissed from his office. Some one said to him, \"Is it not yet time for you, sir, to leave this?\" He replied, \"Serving men in an upright way, where shall I go to, and not experience such a thrice-repeated","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-003","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"齐景公待孔子，曰，若季氏，则吾不能，以季孟之闲待之。曰，吾老矣，不能用也。孔子行。","pinyin":"qí jǐng gōng dài kǒng zǐ yuē ruò jì shì zé wú bù néng yǐ jì mèng zhī jiān dài zhī yuē wú lǎo yǐ bù néng yòng yě kǒng zǐ xíng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The duke Ching of Ch'i, with reference to the manner in which he should treat Confucius, said, \"I cannot treat him as I would the chief of the Chi family. I will treat him in a manner between that accorded to the chief of the Chi, and that given to the chief of the Mang family.\" He also said, \"I am old; I cannot use his doctrines.\" Confucius took his departure.","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-004","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"齐人归女乐。季桓子受之，三日不朝，孔子行。","pinyin":"qí rén kuì nǚ yuè jì huán zǐ shòu zhī sān rì bù cháo kǒng zǐ xíng","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The people of Ch'i sent to Lu a present of female musicians, which Chi Hwan received, and for three days no court was held. Confucius took his departure.","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-005","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"楚狂接舆，歌而过孔子，曰，凤兮。凤兮。何德之衰。往者不可谏，来者犹可追。已而。已而。今之从政者殆而。孔子下，欲与之言。趋而辟之，不得与之言。","pinyin":"chǔ kuáng jiē yú gē ér guò kǒng zǐ yuē fèng xī fèng xī hé dé zhī shuāi wǎng zhě bù kě jiàn lái zhě yóu kě zhuī yǐ ér yǐ ér jīn zhī cóng zhèng zhě dài ér kǒng zǐ xià yù yǔ zhī yán qū ér bì zhī bù dé yǔ zhī yán","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The madman of Ch'u, Chieh-yu, passed by Confucius, singing and saying, \" o fang ! o fang ! How is your","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-006","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"长沮，桀溺耦而耕。孔子过之，使子路问津焉。长沮曰，夫执舆者为谁。子路曰，为孔丘。曰，是鲁孔丘与。曰，是也。曰，是知津矣。问于桀溺，桀溺曰，子为谁。曰，为仲由。曰，是鲁孔丘之徒与。对曰，然。曰，滔滔者，天下皆是也，而谁以易之。且而与其从辟人之士也，岂若从辟世之士哉。耰而不辍。子路行以告，夫子怃然曰，鸟兽不可与同群。吾非斯人之徒与而谁与。天下有道，丘不与易也。","pinyin":"cháng jū jié nì ǒu ér gēng kǒng zǐ guò zhī shǐ zǐ lù wèn jīn yān cháng jū yuē fú zhí yú zhě wéi shuí zǐ lù yuē wéi kǒng mǒu yuē shì lǔ kǒng mǒu yú yuē shì yě yuē shì zhī jīn yǐ wèn yú jié nì jié nì yuē zǐ wéi shuí yuē wéi zhòng yóu yuē shì lǔ kǒng mǒu zhī tú yú duì yuē rán yuē tāo tāo zhě tiān xià jiē shì yě ér shuí yǐ yì zhī qiě ěr yǔ qí cóng bì rén zhī shì yě qǐ ruò cóng bì shì zhī shì zāi yōu ér bú chuò zǐ lù xíng yǐ gào fū zǐ wǔ rán yuē niǎo shòu bù kě yǔ tóng qún wú fēi sī rén zhī tú yǔ ér shuí yǔ tiān xià yǒu dào mǒu bù yǔ yì yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Ch'ang-tsu and Chieh-ni were at work in the field together, when Confucius passed by them, and sent Tsze-lu to inquire for the ford. 2. Ch'ang-tsu said, \"Who is he that holds the reins in the carriage there?\" Tsze-lu told him, \"It is K'ung Ch'iu.\" \"Is it not K'ung Ch'iu of Lu?\" asked he. \"Yes,\" was the reply, to which the other rejoined, \"He knows the ford.\" 3. Tsze-lu then inquired of Chieh-ni, who said to him, \"Who are you, sir?\" He answered, \"I am Chung Yu.\" \"Are you not the disciple of K'ung Ch'iu of Lu?\" asked the other. \"I am,\" replied he, and then Chieh-ni said to him, \"Disorder, like a swelling flood, spreads over the whole empire, and who is he that will change its state for you? Than follow one who merely withdraws from this one and that one, had you not better follow those who have withdrawn from the world altogether?\" With this he fell to covering up the seed, and proceeded with his work, without stopping. 4. Tsze-lu went and reported their remarks, when the Master observed with a sigh, \"It is impossible to associate with birds and beasts, as if they were the same with us. If I associate not with these people,—with mankind,—with whom shall I associate? If right principles prevailed through the empire, there would be no use for me to change its state.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-007","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子路从而后，遇丈人，以杖荷蓧。子路问曰，子见夫子乎。丈人曰，四体不勤，五谷不分，孰为夫子。植其杖而芸。子路拱而立。止子路宿，杀鸡为黍而食之，见其二子焉。明日，子路行以告。子曰，隐者也。使子路反见之。至则行矣。子路曰，不仕无义。长幼之节，不可废也，君臣之义，如之何其废之。欲洁其身，而乱大伦。君子之仕也，行其义也。道之不行，已知之矣。","pinyin":"zǐ lù zòng ér hòu yù zhàng rén yǐ zhàng hè diào zǐ lù wèn yuē zǐ jiàn fū zǐ hū zhàng rén yuē sì tǐ bù qín wǔ gǔ bù fēn shú wéi fū zǐ zhì qí zhàng ér yún zǐ lù gǒng ér lì zhǐ zǐ lù sù shā jī wéi shǔ ér sì zhī xiàn qí èr zǐ yān míng rì zǐ lù xíng yǐ gào zǐ yuē yǐn zhě yě shǐ zǐ lù fǎn jiàn zhī zhì zé xíng yǐ zǐ lù yuē bú shì wú yì zhǎng yòu zhī jié bù kě fèi yě jūn chén zhī yì rú zhī hé qí fèi zhī yù jié qí shēn ér luàn dà lún jūn zǐ zhī shì yě xíng qí yì yě dào zhī bù xíng yǐ zhī zhī yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-lu, following the Master, happened to fall behind, when he met an old man, carrying across his shoulder on a staff a basket for weeds. Tsze-lu said to him, \"Have you seen my master, sir!\" The old man replied, \"Your four limbs are unaccustomed to toil; you cannot distinguish the five kinds of grain:—who is your master?\" With this, he planted his staff in the ground, and proceeded to weed. 2. Tsze-lu joined his hands across his breast, and stood before him. 3. The old man kept Tsze-lu to pass the night in his house, killed a fowl, prepared millet, and feasted him. He also introduced to him his two sons. 4. Next day, Tsze-lu went on his way, and reported his adventure. The Master said, \"He is a recluse,\" and sent Tsze-lu back to see him again, but when he got to the place, the old man was gone. 5. Tsze-lu then said to the family, \"Not to take office is not","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-008","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"逸民，伯夷，叔齐，虞仲，夷逸，朱张，柳下惠，少连。子曰，不降其志，不辱其身，伯夷叔齐与。谓柳下惠，少连，降志辱身矣。言中伦，行中虑，其斯而已矣。谓虞仲，夷逸，隐居放言，身中清，废中权。我则异于是，无可无不可。","pinyin":"yì mín bó yí shū qí yú zhòng yí yì zhū zhāng liǔ xià huì shào lián zǐ yuē bú jiàng qí zhì bù rǔ qí shēn bó yí shū qí yú wèi liǔ xià huì shào lián jiàng zhì rǔ shēn yǐ yán zhòng lún xíng zhòng lǜ qí sī ér yǐ yǐ wèi yú zhòng yí yì yǐn jū fàng yán shēn zhòng qīng fèi zhòng quán wǒ zé yì yú shì wú kě wú bù kě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The men who have retired to privacy from the world have been Po-i, Shu-ch'i, Yu-chung, I-yi, Chu-chang, Hui of Liu-hsia, and Shao-lien. 2. The Master said, \"Refusing to surrender their wills, or to submit to any taint in their persons;—such, I think, were Po-i and Shu-ch'i. 3. \"It may be said of Hui of Liu-hsia, and of Shao-lien, that they surrendered their wills, and submitted to taint in their persons,","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-009","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"大师挚适齐，亚饭干适楚，三饭缭适蔡，四饭缺适秦。鼓方叔入于河，播鼗武入于汉，少师阳，击磬襄入于海。","pinyin":"tài shī zhì shì qí yà fàn gān shì chǔ sān fàn liáo shì cài sì fàn quē shì qín gǔ fāng shū rù yú hé bǒ táo wǔ rù yú hàn shào shī yáng jī qìng xiāng rù yú hǎi","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The grand music master, Chih, went to Ch'i. 2. Kan, the master of the band at the second meal, went to Ch'u. Liao, the band master at the third meal, went to Ts'ai. Chueh, the band master at the fourth meal, went to Ch'in. 3. Fang-shu, the drum master, withdrew to the north of the river.","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-010","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"周公谓鲁公曰，君子不施其亲，不使大臣怨乎不以，故旧无大故，则不弃也，无求备于一人。","pinyin":"zhōu gōng wèi lǔ gōng yuē jūn zǐ bù shǐ qí qīn bù shǐ dà chén yuàn hū bù yǐ gù jiù wú dà gù zé bú qì yě wú qiú bèi yú yì rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The duke of Chau addressed his son, the duke of Lu, saying, \"The virtuous prince does not neglect his relations. He does not cause the great ministers to repine at his not employing them. Without some great cause, he does not dismiss from their offices the members of old families. He does not seek in one man talents for every employment.\"","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"wei-zi-011","bookSlug":"wei-zi","bookNumber":18,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"周有八士，伯达，伯适，仲突，仲忽，叔夜，叔夏，季随，季䯄。","pinyin":"zhōu yǒu bā shì bó dá bó kuò zhòng tū zhòng hū shū yè shū xià jì suí jì guā","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"To Chau belonged the eight officers, Po-ta, Po-kwo, Chung-tu, Chung-hwu, Shu-ya, Shu-hsia, Chi-sui, and Chi-kwa.","themes":["analects","wei-zi"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"zi-zhang","number":19,"zhTitle":"子张","enTitle":"Tsze-chang","chapterCount":25,"sentences":[{"id":"zi-zhang-001","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"子张曰，士，见危致命，见得思义，祭思敬，丧思哀，其可已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng yuē shì jiàn wēi zhì mìng jiàn dé sī yì jì sī jìng sāng sī āi qí kě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang said, \"The scholar, trained for public duty, seeing threatening danger, is prepared to sacrifice his life. When the opportunity of gain is presented to him, he thinks of righteousness. In sacrificing, his thoughts are reverential. In mourning, his thoughts are about the grief which he should feel. Such a man commands our approbation indeed.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-002","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子张曰，执德不弘，信道不笃，焉能为有，焉能为亡。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng yuē zhí dé bù hóng xìn dào bù dǔ yān néng wéi yǒu yān néng wéi wú","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-chang said, \"When a man holds fast to virtue, but without seeking to enlarge it, and believes right principles, but without firm sincerity, what account can be made of his existence or non-existence?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-003","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子夏之门人问交于子张。子张曰，子夏云何。对曰，子夏曰，可者与之，其不可者拒之。子张曰，异乎吾所闻，君子尊贤而容众，嘉善而矜不能，我之大贤与，于人何所不容，我之不贤与，人将拒我，如之何其拒人也。","pinyin":"zǐ xià zhī mén rén wèn jiāo yú zǐ zhāng zǐ zhāng yuē zǐ xià yún hé duì yuē zǐ xià yuē kě zhě yǔ zhī qí bù kě zhě jù zhī zǐ zhāng yuē yì hū wú suǒ wén jūn zǐ zūn xián ér róng zhòng jiā shàn ér jīn bù néng wǒ zhī dà xián yú yú rén hé suǒ bù róng wǒ zhī bù xián yú rén jiāng jù wǒ rú zhī hé qí jù rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The disciples of Tsze-hsia asked Tsze-chang about the principles that should characterize mutual intercourse. Tsze-chang asked, \"What does Tsze-hsia say on the subject?\" They replied, \"Tsze-hsia says:—'Associate with those who can advantage you. Put away from you those who cannot do so.'\" Tsze-chang observed, \"This is different from what I have learned. The superior man honours the talented and virtuous, and bears with all. He praises the good, and pities the incompetent. Am I possessed of great talents and virtue?—who is there among men whom I will not bear with? Am I devoid of talents and virtue?—men will put me away from them. What have we to do with the putting away of others?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-004","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":4,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，虽小道，必有可观者焉，致远恐泥，是以君子不为也。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē suī xiǎo dào bì yǒu kě guān zhě yān zhì yuǎn kǒng nì shì yǐ jūn zǐ bù wéi yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"Even in inferior studies and employments there is something worth being looked at; but if it be","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-005","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":5,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，日知其所亡，月无忘其所能，可谓好学也已矣。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē rì zhī qí suǒ wú yuè wú wàng qí suǒ néng kě wèi hào xué yě yǐ yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"He, who from day to day recognises what he has not yet, and from month to month does not forget what he has attained to, may be said indeed to love to learn.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-006","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":6,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，博学而笃志，切问而近思，仁在其中矣。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē bó xué ér dǔ zhì qiè wèn ér jìn sī rén zài qí zhōng yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"There are learning extensively, and having a firm and sincere aim; inquiring with earnestness, and reflecting with self-application:—virtue is in such a course.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-007","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":7,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，百工居肆，以成其事，君子学以致其道。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē bǎi gōng jū sì yǐ chéng qí shì jūn zǐ xué yǐ zhì qí dào","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"Mechanics have their shops to dwell in, in order to accomplish their works. The superior man learns, in order to reach to the utmost of his principles.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-008","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":8,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，小人之过也，必文。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē xiǎo rén zhī guò yě bì wèn","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"The mean man is sure to gloss his faults.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-009","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":9,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，君子有三变，望之俨然，即之也温，听其言也厉。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē jūn zǐ yǒu sān biàn wàng zhī yǎn rán jí zhī yě wēn tīng qí yán yě lì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"The superior man undergoes three changes. Looked at from a distance, he appears stern; when approached, he is mild; when he is heard to speak, his language is firm and decided.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-010","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":10,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，君子信而后劳其民，未信，则以为厉己也，信而后谏，未信，则以为谤己也。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē jūn zǐ xìn ér hòu láo qí mín wèi xìn zé yǐ wéi lì jǐ yě xìn ér hòu jiàn wèi xìn zé yǐ wéi bàng jǐ yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"The superior man, having obtained their confidence, may then impose labours on his people. If he have not gained their confidence, they will think that he is oppressing them. Having obtained the confidence of his prince, one may then remonstrate with him. If he have not gained his confidence, the prince will think that he is vilifying him.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-011","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":11,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，大德不逾闲，小德出入可也。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē dà dé bù yú xián xiǎo dé chū rù kě yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"When a person does not transgress the boundary line in the great virtues, he may pass and repass it in the small virtues.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-012","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":12,"classicalChinese":"子游曰，子夏之门人小子，当洒扫，应对，进退，则可矣，抑末也，本之则无，如之何。子夏闻之曰，噫，言游过矣，君子之道，孰先传焉，孰后倦焉，譬诸草木，区以别矣，君子之道，焉可诬也，有始有卒者，其惟圣人乎。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu yuē zǐ xià zhī mén rén xiǎo zǐ dāng sǎ sǎo yìng duì jìn tuì zé kě yǐ yì mò yě běn zhī zé wú rú zhī hé zǐ xià wén zhī yuē yī yán yóu guò yǐ jūn zǐ zhī dào shú xiān chuán yān shú hòu juàn yān pì zhū cǎo mù qū yǐ bié yǐ jūn zǐ zhī dào yān kě wú yě yǒu shǐ yǒu zú zhě qí wéi shèng rén hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-yu said, \"The disciples and followers of Tsze-hsia, in sprinkling and sweeping the ground, in answering and replying, in advancing and receding, are sufficiently accomplished. But these are only the branches of learning, and they are left ignorant of what is essential.—How can they be acknowledged as sufficiently taught?\" 2. Tsze-hsia heard of the remark and said, \"Alas! Yen Yu is wrong. According to the way of the superior man in teaching, what departments are there which he considers of prime importance, and delivers? what are there which he considers of secondary importance, and allows himself to be idle about? But as in the case of plants, which are assorted according to their classes, so he deals with his disciples. How can the way of a superior man be such as to make fools of any of them? Is it not the sage alone, who can unite in one the beginning and the consummation of learning?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-013","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":13,"classicalChinese":"子夏曰，仕而优则学，学而优则仕。","pinyin":"zǐ xià yuē shì ér yōu zé xué xué ér yōu zé shì","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"The officer, having discharged all his duties, should devote his leisure to learning. The student, having completed his learning, should apply himself to be an officer.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-014","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":14,"classicalChinese":"子游曰，丧致乎哀而止。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu yuē sāng zhì hū āi ér zhǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"Mourning, having been carried to the utmost degree of grief, should stop with that.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-015","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":15,"classicalChinese":"子游曰，吾友张也，为难能也，然而未仁。","pinyin":"zǐ yóu yuē wú yǒu zhāng yě wéi nán néng yě rán ér wèi rén","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-hsia said, \"My friend Chang can do things which are hard to be done, but yet he is not perfectly virtuous.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-016","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":16,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，堂堂乎张也，难与并为仁矣。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē táng táng hū zhāng yě nán yǔ bìng wéi rén yǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"How imposing is the manner of Chang! It is difficult along with him to practise virtue.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-017","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":17,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，吾闻诸夫子，人未有自致者也，必也亲丧乎。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē wú wén zhū fū zǐ rén wèi yǒu zì zhì zhě yě bì yě qīn sāng hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"I heard this from our Master:—'Men may not have shown what is in them to the full extent, and yet they will be found to do so, on occasion of mourning for their parents.'\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-018","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":18,"classicalChinese":"曾子曰，吾闻诸夫子，孟庄子之孝也，其他可能也，其不改父之臣，与父之政，是难能也。","pinyin":"zēng zǐ yuē wú wén zhū fū zǐ mèng zhuāng zǐ zhī xiào yě qí tā kě néng yě qí bù gǎi fù zhī chén yǔ fù zhī zhèng shì nán néng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The philosopher Tsang said, \"I have heard this from our Master:—'The filial piety of Mang Chwang, in other matters, was what other men are competent to, but, as seen in his not changing the ministers of his father, nor his father's mode of government, it is difficult to be attained to.'\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-019","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":19,"classicalChinese":"孟氏使阳肤为士师，问于曾子。曾子曰，上失其道，民散，久矣，如得其情，则哀矜而勿喜。","pinyin":"mèng shì shǐ yáng fū wéi shì shī wèn yú zēng zǐ zēng zǐ yuē shàng shī qí dào mín sàn jiǔ yǐ rú dé qí qíng zé āi jīn ér wù xǐ","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"The chief of the Mang family having appointed Yang Fu to be chief criminal judge, the latter consulted the philosopher Tsang. Tsang said, \"The rulers have failed in their duties, and the people consequently have been disorganised, for a long time. When you have found out the truth of any accusation, be grieved for and pity them, and do not feel joy at your own ability.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-020","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":20,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，纣之不善，不如是之甚也。是以君子恶居下流，天下之恶皆归焉。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē zhòu zhī bú shàn bù rú shì zhī shèn yě shì yǐ jūn zǐ wù jū xià liú tiān xià zhī è jiē guī yān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung said, \"Chau's wickedness was not so great as that name implies. Therefore, the superior man hates to dwell","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-021","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":21,"classicalChinese":"子贡曰，君子之过也，如日月之食焉，过也，人皆见之，更也，人皆仰之。","pinyin":"zǐ gòng yuē jūn zǐ zhī guò yě rú rì yuè zhī shí yān guò yě rén jiē jiàn zhī gēng yě rén jiē yǎng zhī","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Tsze-kung said, \"The faults of the superior man are like the eclipses of the sun and moon. He has his faults, and all men see them; he changes again, and all men look up to him.\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-022","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":22,"classicalChinese":"卫公孙朝问于子贡曰，仲尼焉学。子贡曰，文，武之道，未坠于地，在人。贤者识其大者，不贤者识其小者，莫不有文，武之道焉。夫子焉不学。而亦何常师之有。","pinyin":"wèi gōng sūn cháo wèn yú zǐ gòng yuē zhòng ní yān xué zǐ gòng yuē wén wǔ zhī dào wèi zhuì yú dì zài rén xián zhě zhì qí dà zhě bù xián zhě zhì qí xiǎo zhě mò bù yǒu wén wǔ zhī dào yān fū zǐ yān bù xué ér yì hé cháng shī zhī yǒu","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Kung-sun Ch'ao of Wei asked Tsze-kung, saying, \"From whom did Chung-ni get his learning?\" 2. Tsze-kung replied, \"The doctrines of Wan and Wu have not yet fallen to the ground. They are to be found among men. Men of talents and virtue remember the greater principles of them, and others, not possessing such talents and virtue, remember the smaller. Thus, all possess the doctrines of Wan and Wu. Where could our Master go that he should not have an opportunity of learning them? And yet what necessity was there for his having a regular master?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-023","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":23,"classicalChinese":"叔孙武叔语大夫于朝曰，子贡贤于仲尼。子服景伯以告子贡。子贡曰，譬之宫墙，赐之墙也，及肩，窥见室家之好。夫子之墙，数仞，不得其门而入，不见宗庙之美，百官之富。得其门者或寡矣，夫子之云，不亦宜乎。","pinyin":"shū sūn wǔ shū yù dà fū yú cháo yuē zǐ gòng xián yú zhòng ní zǐ fú jǐng bó yǐ gào zǐ gòng zǐ gòng yuē pì zhī gōng qiáng cì zhī qiáng yě jí jiān kuī jiàn shì jiā zhī hǎo fū zǐ zhī qiáng shù rèn bù dé qí mén ér rù bú jiàn zōng miào zhī měi bǎi guān zhī fù dé qí mén zhě huò guǎ yǐ fū zǐ zhī yún bú yì yí hū","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Shu-sun Wu-shu observed to the great officers in the court, saying, \"Tsze-kung is superior to Chung-ni.\" 2. Tsze-fu Ching-po reported the observation to Tsze-kung, who said, \"Let me use the comparison of a house and its encompassing wall. My wall only reaches to the shoulders. One may peep over it, and see whatever is valuable in the apartments. 3. \"The wall of my Master is several fathoms high. If one do not find the door and enter by it, he cannot see the ancestral temple with its beauties, nor all the officers in their rich array. 4. \"But I may assume that they are few who find the door. Was not the observation of the chief only what might have been expected?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-024","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":24,"classicalChinese":"叔孙武叔毁仲尼。子贡曰，无以为也，仲尼不可毁也，他人之贤者，丘陵也，犹可逾也，仲尼，日月也，无得而逾焉，人虽欲自绝，其何伤于日月乎，多见其不知量也。","pinyin":"shū sūn wǔ shū huǐ zhòng ní zǐ gòng yuē wú yǐ wéi yě zhòng ní bù kě huǐ yě tā rén zhī xián zhě qiū líng yě yóu kě yú yě zhòng ní rì yuè yě wú dé ér yú yān rén suī yù zì jué qí hé shāng yú rì yuè hū duō jiàn qí bù zhī liàng yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"Shu-sun Wu-shu having spoken revilingly of Chung-ni, Tsze-kung said, \"It is of no use doing so. Chung-ni cannot be reviled. The talents and virtue of other men are hillocks and mounds which may be stepped over. Chung-ni is the sun or moon, which it is not possible to step over. Although a man may wish to cut himself off from the sage, what harm can he do to the sun or moon? He only shows that he does not know his own capacity.","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"zi-zhang-025","bookSlug":"zi-zhang","bookNumber":19,"sentenceNumber":25,"classicalChinese":"陈子禽谓子贡曰，子为恭也，仲尼岂贤于子乎。子贡曰，君子一言以为知，一言以为不知，言不可不慎也。夫子之不可及也，犹天之不可阶而升也。夫子之得邦家者，所谓立之斯立，道之斯行，绥之斯来，动之斯和。其生也荣，其死也哀。如之何其可及也。","pinyin":"chén zǐ qín wèi zǐ gòng yuē zǐ wéi gōng yě zhòng ní qǐ xián yú zǐ hū zǐ gòng yuē jūn zǐ yì yán yǐ wéi zhì yì yán yǐ wéi bú zhì yán bù kě bú shèn yě fū zǐ zhī bù kě jí yě yóu tiān zhī bù kě jiē ér shēng yě fū zǐ zhī dé bāng jiā zhě suǒ wèi lì zhī sī lì dǎo zhī sī xíng suī zhī sī lái dòng zhī sī hé qí shēng yě róng qí sǐ yě āi rú zhī hé qí kě jí yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Ch'an Tsze-ch'in, addressing Tsze-kung, said, \"You are too modest. How can Chung-ni be said to be superior to you?\" 2. Tsze-kung said to him, \"For one word a man is often deemed to be wise, and for one word he is often deemed to be foolish. We ought to be careful indeed in what we say. 3. \"Our Master cannot be attained to, just in the same way as the heavens cannot be gone up to by the steps of a stair. 4. \"Were our Master in the position of the ruler of a State or the chief of a Family, we should find verified the description which has been given of a sage's rule:—he would plant the people, and forthwith they would be established; he would lead them on, and forthwith they would follow him; he would make them happy, and forthwith multitudes would resort to his dominions; he would stimulate them, and forthwith they would be harmonious. While he lived, he would be glorious. When he died, he would be bitterly lamented. How is it possible for him to be attained to?\"","themes":["analects","zi-zhang"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]},{"slug":"yao-yue","number":20,"zhTitle":"尧曰","enTitle":"Yao Yueh","chapterCount":3,"sentences":[{"id":"yao-yue-001","bookSlug":"yao-yue","bookNumber":20,"sentenceNumber":1,"classicalChinese":"尧曰，咨。尔舜。天之历数在尔躬，允执其中。四海困穷，天禄永终。舜亦以命禹。曰，予小子履，敢用玄牡，敢昭告于皇皇后帝，有罪不敢赦。帝臣不蔽，简在帝心。朕躬有罪，无以万方，万方有罪，罪在朕躬。周有大赉，善人是富。虽有周亲，不如仁人，百姓有过，在予一人。谨权量，审法度，修废官，四方之政行焉。兴灭国，继绝世，举逸民，天下之民归心焉。所重，民，食，丧，祭。宽则得众，信则民任焉，敏则有功，公则说。","pinyin":"yáo yuē zī ěr shùn tiān zhī lì shù zài ěr gōng yǔn zhí qí zhōng sì hǎi kùn qióng tiān lù yǒng zhōng shùn yì yǐ mìng yǔ yuē yú xiǎo zǐ lǚ gǎn yòng xuán mǔ gǎn zhāo gào yú huáng huáng hòu dì yǒu zuì bù gǎn shè dì chén bú bì jiǎn zài dì xīn zhèn gōng yǒu zuì wú yǐ wàn fāng wàn fāng yǒu zuì zuì zài zhèn gōng zhōu yǒu dà lài shàn rén shì fù suī yǒu zhōu qīn bù rú rén rén bǎi xìng yǒu guò zài yú yì rén jǐn quán liàng shěn fǎ dù xiū fèi guān sì fāng zhī zhèng xíng yān xīng miè guó jì jué shì jǔ yì mín tiān xià zhī mín guī xīn yān suǒ zhòng mín shí sāng jì kuān zé dé zhòng xìn zé mín rèn yān mǐn zé yǒu gōng gōng zé yuè","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Yao said, \"Oh! you, Shun, the Heaven-determined order of succession now rests in your person. Sincerely hold fast the due Mean. If there shall be distress and want within the four seas, the Heavenly revenue will come to a perpetual end.\" 2. Shun also used the same language in giving charge to Yu. 3. T'ang said, \"I the child Li, presume to use a dark-coloured victim, and presume to announce to Thee, O most great and sovereign God, that the sinner I dare not pardon, and thy ministers, O God, I do not keep in obscurity. The examination of them is by thy mind, O God. If, in my person, I commit offences, they are not to be attributed to you, the people of the myriad regions. If you in the myriad regions commit offences, these offences must rest on my person.\" 4. Chau conferred great gifts, and the good were enriched. 5. \"Although he has his near relatives, they are not equal to my virtuous men. The people are throwing blame upon me, the One man.\" 6. He carefully attended to the weights and measures, examined the body of the laws, restored the discarded officers, and the good government of the kingdom took its course. 7. He revived States that had been extinguished, restored families whose line of succession had been broken, and called to office those who had retired into obscurity, so that throughout the kingdom the hearts of the people turned towards him. 8. What he attached chief importance to, were the food of the people, the duties of mourning, and sacrifices. 9. By his generosity, he won all. By his sincerity, he made the people repose trust in him. By his earnest activity, his achievements were great. By his justice, all were delighted.","themes":["analects","yao-yue"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yao-yue-002","bookSlug":"yao-yue","bookNumber":20,"sentenceNumber":2,"classicalChinese":"子张问于孔子曰，何如斯可以从政矣。子曰，尊五美，屏四恶，斯可以从政矣。子张曰，何谓五美。子曰，君子惠而不费，劳而不怨，欲而不贪，泰而不骄，威而不猛。子张曰，何谓惠而不费。子曰，因民之所利而利之，斯不亦惠而不费乎。择可劳而劳之，又谁怨。欲仁而得仁，又焉贪。君子无众寡，无小大，无敢慢，斯不亦泰而不骄乎。君子正其衣冠，尊其瞻视，俨然人望而畏之，斯不亦威而不猛乎。子张曰，何谓四恶。子曰，不教而杀谓之虐，不戒视成谓之暴，慢令致期谓之贼，犹之与人也，出纳之吝，谓之贪。","pinyin":"zǐ zhāng wèn yú kǒng zǐ yuē hé rú sī kě yǐ cóng zhèng yǐ zǐ yuē zūn wǔ měi bǐng sì è sī kě yǐ cóng zhèng yǐ zǐ zhāng yuē hé wèi wǔ měi zǐ yuē jūn zǐ huì ér bú fèi láo ér bú yuàn yù ér bù tān tài ér bù jiāo wēi ér bù měng zǐ zhāng yuē hé wèi huì ér bú fèi zǐ yuē yīn mín zhī suǒ lì ér lì zhī sī bú yì huì ér bú fèi hū zé kě láo ér láo zhī yòu shuí yuàn yù rén ér dé rén yòu yān tān jūn zǐ wú zhòng guǎ wú xiǎo dà wú gǎn màn sī bú yì tài ér bù jiāo hū jūn zǐ zhèng qí yī guān zūn qí zhān shì yǎn rán rén wàng ér wèi zhī sī bú yì wēi ér bù měng hū zǐ zhāng yuē hé wèi sì è zǐ yuē bú jiào ér shā wèi zhī nüè bú jiè shì chéng wèi zhī bào màn lìng zhì qī wèi zhī zéi yóu zhī yǔ rén yě chū nà zhī lìn wèi zhī tān","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. Tsze-chang asked Confucius, saying, \"In what way should a person in authority act in order that he may conduct government properly?\" The Master replied, \"Let him honour the five excellent, and banish away the four bad, things;—then may he conduct government properly.\" Tsze-chang said, \"What are meant by the five excellent things?\" The Master said, \"When the person in authority is beneficent without great expenditure; when he lays tasks on the people without their repining; when he pursues what he desires without being covetous; when he maintains a dignified ease without being proud; when he is majestic without being fierce.\" 2. Tsze-chang said, \"What is meant by being beneficent without great expenditure?\" The Master replied, \"When the person in authority makes more beneficial to the people the things from which they naturally derive benefit;—is not this being beneficent without great expenditure? When he chooses the labours which are proper, and makes them labour on them, who will repine? When his desires are set on benevolent government, and he secures it, who will accuse him of covetousness? Whether he has to do with many people or few, or with things great or small, he does not dare to indicate any disrespect;—is not this to maintain a dignified ease without any pride? He adjusts his clothes and cap, and throws a dignity into his looks, so that, thus dignified, he is looked at with awe;—is not this to be majestic without being fierce?\" 3. Tsze-chang then asked, \"What are meant by the four bad things?\" The Master said, \"To put the people to death without having instructed them;—this is called cruelty. To require from them, suddenly, the full tale of work, without having given them warning;—this is called oppression. To issue orders as if without urgency, at first, and, when the time comes, to insist on them with severity;—this is called injury. And, generally, in the giving pay","themes":["analects","yao-yue"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]},{"id":"yao-yue-003","bookSlug":"yao-yue","bookNumber":20,"sentenceNumber":3,"classicalChinese":"子曰，不知命，无以为君子也。不知礼，无以立也。不知言，无以知人也。","pinyin":"zǐ yuē bù zhī mìng wú yǐ wéi jūn zǐ yě bù zhī lǐ wú yǐ lì yě bù zhī yán wú yǐ zhī rén yě","modernChinese":"白话导读待审校。当前版本先保证《论语》简体原文与 James Legge 英译逐章完整对应。","english":"1. The Master said, \"Without recognising the ordinances of Heaven, it is impossible to be a superior man. 2. \"Without an acquaintance with the rules of Propriety, it is impossible for the character to be established. 3. \"Without knowing the force of words, it is impossible to know men.\"","themes":["analects","yao-yue"],"notes":["底本：Wikisource 整理的 James Legge《The Chinese Classics》公版中英文本（1893–1895）。","简体化：原文由繁体转为简体（OpenCC），与公版英译逐句对应。"]}]}]}
